// Add new story objects to this array. Keep each retelling in your own wording.
window.TALEATLAS_STORIES = [
{id:'lion-mouse',title:'The Lion and the Mouse',region:'Ancient Greece',country:'Greece',theme:'Kindness',readingTime:'3 min',summary:'A tiny mouse discovers that even the smallest friend can make a mighty difference.',moral:'Kindness is never wasted.',leftPage:'A lion was sleeping beneath a shady tree when a little mouse hurried across his paw. The lion woke with a roar and caught the frightened creature. “Please let me go,” squeaked the mouse. “One day, I may help you.” The lion laughed at the thought of such a tiny animal helping him, but he opened his paw and let the mouse run free.',rightPage:'Some days later, hunters caught the lion in a strong net. He tugged and growled, but the ropes held tight. Hearing his cries, the mouse hurried over. With sharp little teeth, it gnawed through one rope, then another, until the lion could step out. The lion thanked his small friend and understood that strength comes in many forms.'},
{id:'monkey-crocodile',title:'The Monkey and the Crocodile',region:'South Asia',country:'India',theme:'Wisdom',readingTime:'4 min',summary:'A quick-thinking monkey finds a way out of a dangerous river crossing.',moral:'A calm mind can find a way through trouble.',leftPage:'Beside a wide river stood a fruit tree full of sweet, red fruit. A monkey lived among its branches and often tossed fruit to a crocodile resting below. Before long, the two became friends. One day, the crocodile invited the monkey to visit his home across the water. The monkey climbed onto his back, and they set off.',rightPage:'Halfway across, the crocodile admitted that he had been sent to bring the monkey to his family for a cruel purpose. The monkey felt afraid, but kept his voice calm. “I left my heart in the tree,” he said. “Take me back so I can fetch it.” The crocodile turned around. As soon as they reached the bank, the monkey sprang safely into the branches. He told the crocodile that a friend should never be tricked.'},
{id:'momotaro',title:'Momotaro, the Peach Boy',region:'East Asia',country:'Japan',theme:'Courage',readingTime:'4 min',summary:'A child found inside a giant peach grows up to help a village in need.',moral:'Courage grows stronger when friends work together.',leftPage:'Long ago in Japan, an elderly couple lived beside a river. One day, the woman found an enormous peach floating downstream. When she and her husband opened it, a healthy baby boy appeared inside. They named him Momotaro, which means Peach Boy, and raised him with great love. As he grew, he became brave, thoughtful, and strong.',rightPage:'When Momotaro heard that ogres were troubling the nearby islands, he decided to help. His parents packed him millet dumplings for the journey. Along the road, a dog, a monkey, and a pheasant joined him after he shared his food. Together they crossed the sea, faced the ogres, and asked them to stop frightening people. The companions returned home with the stolen treasures and were welcomed as heroes.'},
{id:'anansi-wisdom',title:'Anansi and the Pot of Wisdom',region:'Africa',country:'Ghana and Akan traditions',theme:'Wisdom',readingTime:'4 min',summary:'Anansi tries to keep all wisdom for himself and learns why it should be shared.',moral:'Wisdom grows when people share it.',leftPage:'Anansi the spider once gathered all the world’s wisdom into a pot. He decided to keep it for himself, certain that nobody else deserved to know so much. He planned to hide the pot high in a tree, where no one could reach it. He tied it in front of himself and began to climb, but the pot kept bumping against the trunk.',rightPage:'Anansi’s young son watched and suggested tying the pot on his back instead. The simple advice worked at once. Anansi stopped climbing and stared at the pot. If his own child had thought of something he had missed, then wisdom could not belong to him alone. In frustration, he dropped the pot, and it broke. In the story, wisdom spread through the world, where everyone could learn and contribute.'},
{id:'tortoise-hare',title:'The Tortoise and the Hare',region:'Ancient Greece',country:'Greece',theme:'Perseverance',readingTime:'3 min',summary:'A speedy hare learns not to underestimate a steady tortoise.',moral:'Steady effort can beat careless confidence.',leftPage:'A hare loved to boast about how fast he could run. One day, he teased a tortoise for moving so slowly. The tortoise calmly challenged him to a race. The other animals gathered to watch, and the race began. The hare dashed far ahead and looked back. The tortoise was still moving, one careful step after another.',rightPage:'Certain he would win, the hare decided to rest beneath a tree and soon fell asleep. The tortoise did not stop. He kept going along the path, past the tree, and toward the finish line. When the hare finally woke, he raced as fast as he could, but the tortoise had already arrived. The animals cheered for the tortoise, whose steady effort had carried him to the end.'},
{id:'stone-soup',title:'Stone Soup',region:'Europe',country:'European oral traditions',theme:'Community',readingTime:'4 min',summary:'Travellers help a cautious village discover the joy of sharing.',moral:'Small contributions can make something wonderful together.',leftPage:'Three hungry travellers arrived in a village, but the villagers were worried about their own supplies and offered no food. The travellers filled a pot with water, placed a clean stone inside, and set it over a fire. They announced that they were making stone soup. Curious villagers came to watch. “It would taste even better with a carrot,” one traveller said.',rightPage:'A villager brought a carrot. Another added potatoes, and someone else brought onions and herbs. Soon the pot smelled delicious. Everyone contributed a little, and the travellers shared the soup with the whole village. The people discovered that they had enough when they worked together. Different versions of this traditional tale are told in many places, but its warm message remains familiar.'},
{id:'bremen-musicians',title:'The Bremen Town Musicians',region:'Europe',country:'Germany',theme:'Friendship',readingTime:'4 min',summary:'Four animals who feel unwanted discover that they are stronger together.',moral:'Friends can help one another find a new beginning.',leftPage:'An old donkey set out for the town of Bremen, hoping to become a musician. On the road, he met a tired dog, a cat, and a rooster. Each had left home because someone thought they were no longer useful. The donkey invited them to join him. They travelled together, sharing the road and encouraging one another.',rightPage:'At night, they saw a house where robbers were feasting. The four friends climbed on one another and made the loudest, strangest music they could. Startled by the noise and shadows, the robbers fled into the forest. The animals settled into the cosy house, found food and shelter, and stayed together. Their journey had led them somewhere better than they had imagined.'},
{id:'boy-wolf',title:'The Boy Who Cried Wolf',region:'Ancient Greece',country:'Greece',theme:'Honesty',readingTime:'3 min',summary:'A shepherd boy learns that trust is hard to rebuild once it is lost.',moral:'People need to be able to trust your words.',leftPage:'A young shepherd watched a flock of sheep on a hillside. One quiet afternoon, he grew bored and shouted, “Wolf! A wolf is chasing the sheep!” The villagers hurried up the hill to help, only to discover that the boy had played a trick. He laughed at their worried faces. A few days later, he played the same trick again, and the villagers came running once more.',rightPage:'Then a wolf really did appear. The frightened boy called for help with all his strength. The villagers heard him, but remembered the earlier tricks and thought he was joking again. The boy managed to get away and learned a serious lesson: honesty matters even when telling the truth feels less exciting. The story reminds us that trust is built by being truthful again and again.'},
{id:'porridge-pot',title:'The Magic Porridge Pot',region:'Europe',country:'Germany; a version collected by the Brothers Grimm',theme:'Carefulness',readingTime:'3 min',summary:'A magical pot makes porridge, but someone must remember how to stop it.',moral:'A gift is best used with care.',leftPage:'A kind girl met an old woman in the forest and helped her. As a reward, the woman gave her a little pot and taught her the words that would make it cook sweet porridge. She also taught the girl the words to make it stop. At home, the girl and her mother were delighted. Whenever they were hungry, the pot made a warm meal.',rightPage:'One day, the girl was away and her mother tried to use the pot. She remembered how to start it, but forgot how to stop it. The porridge filled the pot, then the kitchen, then the road outside. When the girl returned, she said the stopping words, and at last the pot became still. The village had to eat its way through the porridge before everyone could pass along the road.'},
{id:'fisherman-wife',title:'The Fisherman and His Wife',region:'Europe',country:'Germany; a version collected by the Brothers Grimm',theme:'Contentment',readingTime:'4 min',summary:'A series of wishes shows how endless wanting can spoil simple happiness.',moral:'Gratitude helps us recognise what is already enough.',leftPage:'A fisherman caught a strange fish that spoke and begged to be released. The fisherman kindly returned it to the sea without asking for a reward. When he told his wife, she wished for a better home. The fisherman returned to the shore, called to the fish, and the wish came true. But soon his wife wanted more: a grander house, then a palace.',rightPage:'Each time, the fisherman felt uneasy but went back to the sea. Each wish was granted, yet his wife remained dissatisfied and asked for still more. At last, she wished to rule over everything. The fisherman called the fish one final time, and the wish was not granted. When he returned home, they were back in their old, simple hut. The tale invites readers to think about gratitude, fairness, and knowing when enough is enough.'},
{id:'clever-rabbit',title:'The Clever Rabbit and the Lion',region:'South Asia',country:'India; Panchatantra tradition',theme:'Problem-solving',readingTime:'3 min',summary:'A small rabbit protects the forest animals with a clever plan.',moral:'Thought and courage can be more useful than size.',leftPage:'A proud lion frightened the animals of the forest, who worried that he would hunt them all. The animals agreed that one would visit him each day, hoping to prevent a greater disaster. One day, it was the turn of a small rabbit. The rabbit travelled slowly and arrived late, saying that another lion had tried to stop him on the way.',rightPage:'The angry lion demanded to see this rival. The rabbit led him to a deep well and pointed down. The lion saw his own reflection in the water and heard his roar echo back. Believing another lion was inside, he leapt toward it and fell into the well. The animals were safe again. In this traditional story, the rabbit’s careful thinking succeeds where strength alone would not.'},
{id:'grateful-crane',title:'The Grateful Crane',region:'East Asia',country:'Japan',theme:'Gratitude',readingTime:'4 min',summary:'A kind act leads to a mysterious gift, and a promise must be respected.',moral:'Gratitude and respect matter more than curiosity.',leftPage:'On a snowy day, a poor man freed a crane caught in a trap. Later, a young visitor arrived at his home and asked to stay. The man welcomed the guest. The visitor began weaving beautiful cloth, asking only that nobody watch while the work was done. The cloth was so lovely that the man could sell it and live more comfortably.',rightPage:'Curiosity eventually overcame the man’s promise, and he looked inside the weaving room. There he discovered the visitor was the crane he had rescued, using its own feathers to make the cloth. The crane was saddened that the promise had been broken and had to leave. Versions of this Japanese tale vary, but many centre on kindness, gratitude, and respecting another person’s boundaries.'},
{id:"the-tortoise-with-a-pretty-daughter",title:"The Tortoise with a Pretty Daughter",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Adet’s joyful song brings the prince’s attention, but an unfair royal rule threatens to keep her from the celebration. Her kindness helps the king see that everyone belongs.",moral:"A celebration is brighter when everyone is welcome.",leftPage:"In a Southern Nigerian town, Tortoise loved his daughter Adet, whose bright smile made neighbors happy. The king had made a foolish rule: no girl said to be prettier than the prince’s palace portraits could join the spring celebration. When the prince heard Adet sing while helping carry baskets to the market, he invited her to the palace feast. Tortoise worried the king would turn them away.",rightPage:"At the feast, the king saw Adet waiting outside and asked what was wrong. She gently asked why a celebration should leave anyone out, then offered to teach the palace children her song. As they sang together, the king realized his rule was unfair and canceled it. Everyone joined the dance, and Tortoise went home proud: Adet had won hearts through her kindness."},
{id:"the-kings-magic-drum",title:"The King's Magic Drum",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Sharing",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A proud Tortoise borrows a king’s food-making drum, then learns that keeping a promise matters more than showing off.",moral:"A promise kept—and a meal shared—can make everyone feel welcome.",leftPage:"In Calabar, King Efriam’s drum could summon a splendid meal whenever people gathered. He lent it to Tortoise after a palm nut from Tortoise’s tree rolled to the king’s little daughter, and Tortoise asked for the drum instead of other gifts. The king explained its rule: never step over a stick or fallen branch while carrying it. Tortoise proudly invited everyone to supper.",rightPage:"As Tortoise carried the drum to impress his guests, he missed a branch on the path and stepped over it. The dishes turned sour, and the feast was spoiled. Tortoise admitted his mistake; the king kindly helped everyone share the simple food they had brought. Tortoise returned the drum and promised to watch his steps. At the next feast, he helped welcome every guest."},
{id:"ituen-and-the-kings-wife",title:"Ituen and the King’s Wife",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Sharing",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A resourceful young man helps a queen find a fair way to share market food, and his family’s honest explanation inspires a kinder custom.",moral:"Honest words and thoughtful sharing can turn worry into a better way for everyone.",leftPage:"In Calabar, young Ituen had little food, so he gathered leftover grain after market each day. One morning, Queen Attem’s helper noticed his bright smile and strong hands. The queen needed someone to help plan a fair way to share unsold food, and invited him to the palace. Ituen worried the king might misunderstand, but his mother said, “Go with honesty, and tell us what happens.”",rightPage:"At the palace, Ituen showed the queen how hungry families could receive food before scraps were swept away. The king arrived and wondered why a visitor had come secretly at night. Ituen explained everything, and his mother confirmed the truth. The king thanked them and opened a sharing table in the market; leftovers were set aside for dogs and birds too. From then on, no one had to search for a meal."},
{id:"of-the-pretty-stranger-who-killed-the-king",title:"The Pretty Stranger Who Killed the King",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Conflict",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A witch from Itu disguises herself as a beautiful stranger, kills King Mbotu, and helps Itu take Old Town by surprise.",moral:"War brings loss and grief to whole communities.",leftPage:"Mbotu, king of Old Town, had won many wars, taken prisoners, and made many enemies. The people of Itu wanted to defeat him but could not beat his army in battle, so they asked a witch for help. The witch changed herself into a beautiful young woman and hid a knife before traveling to Old Town. A celebration was underway, and everyone admired her. King Mbotu invited her to the palace and said he would marry her that day. She prepared his meal with medicine that made him sleepy. While he slept, she killed King Mbotu and carried proof of his death back to Itu.",rightPage:"The king of Itu gathered soldiers and secretly traveled by canoe through the creeks to Old Town. The people there discovered Mbotu’s death the next morning and began to mourn. While they were grieving, Itu’s army attacked Old Town by surprise. Without their leader or time to prepare, the people of Old Town were defeated; many were killed or taken prisoner. The people of Itu celebrated, while Old Town was left in grief."},
{id:"the-disobedient-daughter-who-married-a-skull",title:"Afiong and the Stranger with a Borrowed Face",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Listening",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Afiong follows a charming stranger to a faraway land, where a kind elder helps her return home and reconsider her choice.",moral:"Pause, listen to people who care for you, and take time to know someone before trusting them.",leftPage:"In Cobham Town, Afiong loved hearing that she was the loveliest girl around. Her parents asked her to consider kind young people they knew, but she only wanted to meet someone handsome and exciting. One day, a charming stranger appeared at the market. Afiong invited him home, and soon set out with him for his faraway country, despite her parents’ worried advice.",rightPage:"Across the border, the stranger’s borrowed good looks disappeared: he was a skull. Afiong felt afraid, but his gentle mother welcomed her. Afiong helped the old woman with daily chores, and the mother thanked her by sending her home on a soft breeze. Her parents rejoiced. Afiong listened to their counsel, made peace, and chose a kind companion from her own town."},
{id:"the-king-who-married-the-cocks-daughter",title:"The King Who Married the Cock’s Daughter",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"When jealous guests trick the Cock’s daughter at a feast, the king learns to listen, apologize, and welcome her back as she is.",moral:"Be fair to others, and never judge someone for being different.",leftPage:"In Calabar, King Effiom married Adia, the Cock’s daughter, and admired her cheerful ways. Adia loved bright kernels of corn; her father had warned the king that she might peck them up. Some palace ladies, jealous of the king’s attention, scattered corn at a feast. Adia happily pecked a few kernels, and the guests began to giggle. Hurt and embarrassed, the king sent her home.",rightPage:"That evening, a kind courtier explained that jealous guests had planned the trick. The king saw he had judged Adia unfairly, and went to her father’s cottage to apologize. He invited Adia back, promising no more tricks or tests. Adia agreed, but asked that corn be shared with birds and people alike. From then on, the palace set out a bowl of grain in the courtyard. At palace feasts, no one was laughed at for being different."},
{id:"concerning-the-woman-the-ape-and-the-child",title:"Concerning the Woman, the Ape, and the Child",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Trust",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"When a worried rumor makes a father fear his child’s ape friend, he takes time to see what is really happening. The family and neighbors find a kind, safe way to care for both child and ape.",moral:"A careful question can turn a fearful guess into understanding.",leftPage:"Nkoyo often brought her baby to the yam farm, where a gentle ape visited from the forest. He sat nearby, made funny faces, and fetched the baby’s bright wooden rattle when it rolled away. One afternoon, a passing hunter saw them together and hurried to tell the child’s father that the ape was carrying the baby off.",rightPage:"Okun felt worried, but Nkoyo asked him to come and look before deciding. They found the ape sitting beside the baby beneath a shady tree, returning the rattle whenever it slipped away. Okun thanked the ape with a basket of fruit, and the neighbors helped make a little fenced play space near the farm, where the family could visit their new friend safely."},
{id:"the-fish-and-the-leopards-wife",title:"The Fish and the Leopard’s Wife",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Honesty and friendship",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A fish breaks a promise while visiting his friends, then admits his mistake and makes things right.",moral:"Honesty helps repair trust, and being a good friend means respecting others’ homes.",leftPage:"Long ago in Calabar, Fish could walk on land and often visited his friend Leopard and Leopard’s wife. One day, while Leopard gathered firewood, Mrs. Leopard prepared a little plate of fruit for supper. Fish had promised not to touch it, but the sweet smell tempted him. He ate every piece, then felt ashamed when she found the empty plate.",rightPage:"When Leopard returned, Mrs. Leopard told him what happened, and Fish admitted the truth instead of making excuses. King Eyo listened and said that friendship needs honesty, and that a guest must respect another home. Fish apologized and helped gather fresh fruit. Since Fish loved cool water, he chose to live in the river, visiting the leopard family only when invited."},
{id:"why-the-worms-live-underneath-the-ground",title:"Why the Worms Live Underneath the Ground",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Staying Safe",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"When boastful driver ants challenge the worms, the little creatures discover that the safest place for them is beneath the ground.",moral:"Knowing when to step away can be a wise kind of strength.",leftPage:"Long ago, King Eyo invited the creatures to a grand feast. Afterward, the head driver ant stood up and bragged that no one could match his people’s strength. He laughed at the worms for being small and wiggly. The worms felt hurt, so the king proposed a contest to settle the argument, and everyone gathered to watch.",rightPage:"When the ants marched toward the meeting place in a great dark ribbon, the worms saw how powerful a crowd they made. Rather than risk a frightening clash, the worms slipped safely beneath the soft earth. King Eyo praised their clever choice and asked the ants to stop boasting. Ever since, worms have made their home underground, coming up after rain and ducking below when danger draws near."},
{id:"why-the-sun-and-the-moon-live-in-the-sky",title:"Why the Sun and the Moon Live in the Sky",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Friendship",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"The Sun and Moon welcome their friend Water, whose many companions fill their home until they find a new place in the sky.",moral:"Good friends make room for one another—and adapt when plans change.",leftPage:"Long ago, the Sun and Water were friends, though Water had never visited the Sun’s home. When the Sun asked why, Water explained that his many companions—fish, frogs, and flowing streams—would need a very wide place to gather. The Sun promised to build a spacious courtyard. With Moon’s help, he prepared it and invited Water to visit the next day.",rightPage:"Water arrived with his lively crowd, and soon the courtyard brimmed. The Sun checked that everyone was comfortable, but the guests kept arriving; the friendly hosts climbed onto the roof to stay dry. As the water rose higher, Sun and Moon floated safely upward into the open sky. There they found a bright home above the clouds, while Water and his creatures filled the rivers and sea below."},
{id:"the-story-of-the-lightning-and-the-thunder",title:"The Story of the Lightning and the Thunder",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Listening",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A quick-tempered ram and his mother are asked to leave town, and the king finds them a home in the sky.",moral:"A pause to listen can help keep others safe.",leftPage:"Long ago, Thunder, a mother sheep, and Lightning, her restless ram, lived near a busy town. Whenever Lightning grew cross, he flashed across roofs and trees, startling everyone. His mother called him to pause, but he rushed on. At last, the neighbors asked their king for help, worried that a careless flash might reach their homes or gardens.",rightPage:"The king first sent them far from town, but Lightning’s quick flashes still made people uneasy. So the king gave them a home high in the sky, above houses and fields. There, Thunder could call after her son whenever he darted ahead. Lightning learned to keep his bright flashes among the clouds, and people below saw the light, then heard Thunder’s rumbling reminder to be careful. From then on, they watched storms from safe shelter."},
{id:"why-dead-people-are-buried",title:"The Message Dog Forgot",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Listening",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A forgotten message and a sheep’s mixed-up words explain how people came to bury their loved ones.",moral:"Listen carefully, and take care when passing a message along.",leftPage:"In the first days, people and animals lived near the Creator, who hoped that those who died would soon rise again. He asked Dog, his swift messenger, to tell everyone: “Lay your loved one in the family yard, cover the place with cool ashes, and wait one day.” Dog hurried off, but a tempting bone made him stop—and the important words slipped from his mind.",rightPage:"When Dog did not return, Creator sent Sheep with the same message. Sheep nibbled grass and remembered only the final instruction: “Place the departed gently beneath the earth.” The people trusted Sheep’s words and began burying their loved ones. When Dog returned with the first message, they had already chosen their custom. Creator saw their kindness, and Dog practiced remembering each message before setting out again."},
{id:"of-the-fat-woman-who-melted-away",title:"The Woman Made of Palm Oil",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A woman who melts in the sun is pressured to work outdoors, but her sister and household find a caring, fair solution.",moral:"Listen to one another and share work fairly, with care for each person’s needs.",leftPage:"In a Southern Nigerian village, a woman made of palm oil was known as the Fat Woman. She stayed in the shade because sunshine could make her melt. At her new home, another wife grew cross at carrying all the firewood and water. When she urged the Fat Woman into the sunny field, her little sister warned her to stay beneath a tree—but wanting peace, the Fat Woman stepped into the sun.",rightPage:"She became a small golden puddle beneath a leaf, and her sister carefully gathered it into a cool, covered pot. When the husband returned, he listened to both women and helped them agree: chores would be shared, and the Fat Woman would work only in shade. After three months, the sister opened the pot; the Fat Woman stood smiling, whole again. The household welcomed her and began its fairer routine together."},
{id:"the-king-and-the-ju-ju-tree",title:"The King and the Ju Ju Tree",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Respect for nature",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"King Udo learns that working with the Ju Ju tree protects both the spring and the people who depend on it.",moral:"When we respect nature and listen before acting, we can find a solution that helps everyone.",leftPage:"When King Udo returned to the spring behind his house, he found a great Ju Ju tree spreading across the path. “Clear it away,” he ordered, but every cut closed again. The king tried once himself, and a tiny twig brushed his eye, leaving it sore. His daughter suggested they ask the tree why it guarded the spring.",rightPage:"A gentle healer rinsed the king’s eye, then helped his daughter listen beneath the leaves. The tree’s roots, it seemed, were holding the spring’s banks together. The king apologized and asked the villagers to make a narrow path without harming the roots. The tree lifted one branch, revealing clear water. From then on, the king and his people cared for the spring together."},
{id:"of-the-pretty-girl-and-the-seven-jealous-women",title:"Akim and the River Guardian",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Akim’s jealous companions leave her without the customary gift for the river guardian, but a little bird tells the truth and helps her family bring her home. Their town turns jealousy into a chance to make things right.",moral:"Jealousy can hurt others; honesty and kindness help mend what has gone wrong.",leftPage:"Akim, a kind young woman from Ibibio, was invited by seven companions to a lively town gathering. Though they envied her, she went with them, hoping to enjoy the music. At the river, the others offered gifts to its guardian, as was the custom, but they had not shared any with Akim. The guardian gently carried her beneath the water to a quiet, safe place.",rightPage:"A little bird had seen everything, and told Akim’s worried parents the truth. They brought traditional gifts to the river, and the guardian returned Akim safely to shore. At a gathering, the seven companions admitted what they had done. The town asked them to apologize and help Akim prepare the next celebration. They worked together, and soon the music and friendship returned."},
{id:"the-lucky-fisherman",title:"The Lucky Fisherman",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Perseverance",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A patient fisherman studies the river, finds a better place for his traps, and shares what he learns with his neighbors.",moral:"Careful patience and sharing can help a whole community thrive.",leftPage:"Long ago, beside a river in Southern Nigeria, Akon Obo had little to spare. He wove fish baskets from bamboo-palm strips and baited them with palm nuts, but his first catches were small. Rather than give up, he watched the water each morning. One day he spotted tiny ripples beneath a shaded bend and guessed the fish were gathering in a deeper pool.",rightPage:"Akon carefully set his baskets near the bend. Soon they held enough fish for his family, with more to sell at the market. When neighbors asked how he had found such a good spot, he showed them how to watch the river and weave sturdy traps. Before long, many homes had food to share. They called Akon the Lucky Fisherman, and he smiled: a little patience had brought luck to everyone."},
{id:"the-orphan-boy-and-the-magic-stone",title:"The Orphan Boy and the Magic Stone",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Generosity",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"After losing his father, Ayong finds a magic stone that can grant wishes. When it is time to return it, he chooses to share what he has gained and help his home flourish.",moral:"A gift becomes more meaningful when we use it to help others.",leftPage:"Ayong Kita was still young when his father died, and trouble at home sent him into the green forest alone. Hungry and worried, he remembered a dream in which his father had shown him where treasure lay. An old woman kindly guided him away from unsafe water, then gave him a shining stone and told him how its wishes could help him find a safe home.",rightPage:"Ayong found the treasure and used the stone to build a welcoming home, where neighbors could gather and share meals. Years later, the old woman reminded him that the stone belonged in the stream where she had found it. Ayong returned it with thanks, then used his own good fortune to help his home village thrive. His neighbors trusted him because he cared for them."},
{id:"concerning-the-fate-of-essido-and-his-evil-companions",title:"Eyo and Essido’s New Beginning",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Honesty",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Essido squanders his inheritance and listens to companions who urge him to betray his generous brother. A last moment of honesty opens a better path for them both.",moral:"Good friends help us choose what is right; honesty can turn us toward a fresh start.",leftPage:"In a riverside town lived two brothers, Eyo and Essido. When their father died, kind Eyo shared his inheritance with Essido. But Essido spent his share on grand parties, while Eyo worked steadily trading yams and fish. Soon Essido had nothing left. His companions, who liked the feasts, urged him to borrow more and run away with them to another town.",rightPage:"There, the companions soon spent the borrowed money too. They suggested a wicked plan: Essido should pretend to mend his ways, then secretly harm Eyo to take his wealth. Remembering his brother’s kindness, Essido hurried home and confessed the plan before anyone was hurt. Eyo forgave him but asked him to work honestly. Essido agreed, and the brothers traded together, sharing fair profits and welcoming kinder friends."},
{id:"the-story-of-the-drummer-and-the-alligators",title:"The Story of the Drummer and the Alligators",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Courage and kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A devoted mother seeks help when her gifted drummer son is taken by the alligators, and a town meeting brings the truth to light.",moral:"Courage and care can help bring the truth to light.",leftPage:"Long ago beside Calabar River lived Affiong and her son Edet, who could make a drum sing like rain on leaves. When jealous youths lured him toward the water, alligators carried him to their hidden hall. Affiong did not give up. She asked the clever river guide Ininen for help, and together they watched the tide and planned a quiet rescue.",rightPage:"At low tide, Ininen guided Edet home; shaken and silent, the boy slowly found his voice again with his mother’s care. They kept a clue from the hidden hall and brought it to a town meeting. When the youths recognized it, they admitted their wrong and promised to stop frightening people. The chiefs set them to helping mend canoes, and Edet played his drum at a joyful riverside dance."},
{id:"the-election-of-the-king-bird",title:"The Election of the King Bird",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910); community varies",theme:"Leadership",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"When the birds cannot agree on a leader, the calm fishing eagle helps them notice one another’s strengths and is chosen as king bird.",moral:"A good leader listens calmly and helps everyone’s gifts shine.",leftPage:"Long ago, King Essiya needed a leader for every kind of bird, but the birds all claimed the honor. Hawks bragged about speed, tall waders about their long legs, and tiny forest birds could hardly be heard above the commotion. Essiya invited them to gather by the river and asked them to show how they could help keep the bird world in harmony. Then everyone began talking at once.",rightPage:"While the others swooped and squabbled, Ituen, the black-and-white fishing eagle, stayed high in a tree, watching patiently. When the king asked him to speak, he spread his wings and called for quiet, then pointed out how each bird had a useful gift: speed, careful steps, keen eyes, or a gentle song. The birds listened and settled down. Essiya named Ituen king bird, to lead by courage and calm sense."},
{id:"the-wolf-and-the-lamb",title:"The Wolf and the Lamb",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Fairness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A wolf invents unfair complaints against a lamb by a stream, but the lamb’s call brings help and safety.",moral:"A calm, truthful answer matters, and unfairness should not go unchallenged.",leftPage:"On a sunny hillside in Ancient Greece, a young lamb paused beside a clear stream for a drink. A wolf appeared on the opposite bank and complained, “You are muddling my water!” The lamb gently replied, “The stream flows from you toward me, so my sip cannot cloud the water above you.” The wolf frowned, searching for another reason to scold.",rightPage:"“You once spoke unkindly of me,” he growled. “I was only born this spring,” said the lamb. The wolf had no fair answer, but the lamb called loudly for the shepherd. Hearing the call, the shepherd came over and led the little lamb safely home. The wolf slipped away, while the stream went on sparkling for everyone."},
{id:"the-ass-and-the-grasshopper",title:"The Ass and the Grasshopper",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Good Judgment",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"An ass tries to sing like the grasshoppers by living on dew, but dies of hunger.",moral:"What nourishes one creature may not nourish another.",leftPage:"An ass heard grasshoppers chirping and was enchanted by their song. He asked what food gave them such lovely voices. “We live on dew,” they replied. The ass decided he would eat nothing but dew too, hoping to learn to sing like them.",rightPage:"The ass continued eating only dew and had no other food. Before long, he grew weak and died of hunger. The grasshoppers kept chirping in the grass. Their diet suited them, but it could not keep an ass healthy."},
{id:"the-wolf-and-the-crane",title:"The Wolf and the Crane",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Caution",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A crane removes a bone from a wolf’s throat, but he refuses to pay her promised fee.",moral:"When you help someone dangerous, do not expect a reward; be glad to escape safely.",leftPage:"A wolf had a bone stuck in his throat. He hired a crane to reach in with her long beak and pull it out, promising to pay her well. The crane carefully did the job and drew her head back safely. Then she asked for the payment the wolf had promised.",rightPage:"Wolf refused to pay the promised fee. He told Crane she should be glad she escaped his jaws unharmed. The crane flew away safely, but received no reward. She had trusted a dangerous wolf and was fortunate to get out of danger."},
{id:"the-father-and-his-sons",title:"The Father and His Sons",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Working Together",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A father helps his quarrelsome sons discover that they are stronger when they work together.",moral:"When we cooperate, we can do more than we can alone.",leftPage:"In a sunny village in Greece, a father’s three sons argued over every small chore. One morning, he asked them to bring a handful of slender sticks from the garden. Each son insisted his way of gathering them was best, and soon the sticks lay scattered while the brothers huffed at one another. Their father smiled and invited them to try a little test.",rightPage:"He tied the sticks into a neat bundle and let each son try to bend it; none could. Then he untied it and handed them one stick at a time, which each snapped easily. The brothers understood: alone, each could do only a little, but together they were sturdy. They gathered the sticks side by side and planned their chores as a team."},
{id:"the-bat-and-the-weasels",title:"The Bat and the Weasels",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Cleverness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A quick-thinking bat finds a peaceful way past two weasels who each mistake him for someone they dislike.",moral:"When trouble changes, pause and use what makes you unique.",leftPage:"At twilight, Pip the bat fluttered down to rest beside an olive tree. A weasel spotted him and blocked the path. “I never let a bird pass,” she declared. Pip kept his voice calm. “I’m not a bird,” he said. “Look at my soft fur and tiny paws—I’m a bat, more like a little mouse.” The weasel blinked, then let him go.",rightPage:"Pip hurried along, but another weasel stopped him near the garden wall. “Mice must turn back!” she warned. Pip thought quickly. “You’re right that I have paws,” he said, “but see these wide wings? I’m a bat, and the evening sky is my home.” The weasel stepped aside. Pip flew safely home, glad he had stayed calm and noticed what each moment called for."},
{id:"the-cock-and-the-jewel",title:"The Cock and the Jewel",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"What We Need",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A hungry rooster finds a shining jewel, but chooses useful grain to share with his hens.",moral:"Something beautiful is not always what we need most.",leftPage:"At sunrise, a rooster named Kiko scratched the courtyard earth, hoping to find breakfast for himself and his hens. Suddenly something bright winked beneath a leaf. He uncovered a jewel, smooth and sparkling like a drop of sunlight. “What a treasure!” he clucked, then wondered whether it might be something his hungry flock could eat.",rightPage:"He carried the jewel to the hens, who admired its colors but could not peck it into breakfast. Nearby lay one small barley grain he had missed. The rooster chose the grain and shared it with his friends. As they ate, he set the jewel safely beside the path, where its owner might find it. It shone beautifully, but a meal was what they needed most."},
{id:"the-swallow-and-the-crow",title:"The Swallow and the Crow",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Being prepared",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A swallow and a crow compare their feathers, then discover that bright colors and practical warmth each have their place.",moral:"Looks may catch the eye, but steady, useful qualities matter when the weather changes.",leftPage:"One bright spring morning, a Swallow swooped over a garden, showing off her smooth, shining feathers. “Just look at my lovely colors!” she chirped. A Crow nearby admired them, but soon the two birds began comparing their plumage. The Swallow thought beauty was the finest thing a bird could have; the Crow quietly wondered what those bright feathers would do when chilly days arrived.",rightPage:"A cool wind soon swept through the garden. The Swallow tucked herself into a snug, sunny nook, while the Crow’s thick, dark feathers kept her cozy on a branch. “Your feathers are splendid for spring,” said the Crow, “and mine are useful in the cold.” The Swallow smiled. They agreed that each bird had what she needed, and stopped arguing about whose feathers were best."},
{id:"the-dog-and-the-shadow",title:"The Dog and the Shadow",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Being Content",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A dog mistakes his reflection for another dog with a larger snack and learns to value what he already has.",moral:"Be grateful for what you have instead of reaching for an illusion.",leftPage:"On a bright morning, a small dog named Niko trotted home across a stone bridge with a piece of bread he had been given. In the clear stream below, another dog seemed to carry an even bigger piece. Niko paused, puzzled: the stranger’s bread looked like a feast, and he wondered whether he could somehow trade for it.",rightPage:"Niko leaned closer and opened his mouth to call. His own bread slipped gently into the shallow water and floated to the bank, while the other dog vanished with the ripples: it had only been his reflection. Niko retrieved his bread, brushed off a few drops, and continued home, glad to enjoy what he already had instead of chasing an illusion."},
{id:"hercules-and-the-wagoner",title:"Hercules and the Wagoner",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Helping Yourself",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"When a wagon gets stuck in a muddy rut, its driver learns that asking for help must go hand in hand with doing his own best.",moral:"Ask for help when you need it, and remember to do your part, too.",leftPage:"One bright morning, a wagoner was taking a load of apples along a country lane. After a night of rain, one wheel sank into a muddy rut, and the wagon would not budge. The wagoner tugged at the reins, then sat beside the road and called to Hercules, the strong hero, asking him to come and move the wagon.",rightPage:"A calm voice answered, “I can guide you, but you must begin. Place your shoulder by the wheel, ask the oxen to pull, and push with all your strength.” The wagoner stood up and tried. The wheel rolled free, and the apples continued safely down the lane. Smiling, he thanked Hercules and remembered to take the first helpful step himself."},
{id:"the-mole-and-his-mother",title:"The Mole and His Mother",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Knowing Your Strengths",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A young mole boasts that he can see, but a gentle test helps him recognize both his limits and his other ways of learning.",moral:"It is wiser to be honest about what we can do and curious about what we can learn.",leftPage:"Under a warm Greek hillside, a young mole told his mother, “I can see just as well as anyone!” Mother did not argue. She set a tiny pinch of fragrant frankincense on a smooth stone and asked, “What have I placed here?” The mole leaned close, felt the stone, and guessed, “A little pebble.” He waited proudly for her answer.",rightPage:"“It is frankincense,” said Mother kindly. “Your eyes cannot see it, but your nose can help you discover it.” She let him sniff the sweet, woody scent, then invited him to find a nearby herb by smell. This time he named it correctly. The young mole smiled: he could not see, but he had other ways to learn—and no need to pretend."},
{id:"the-fawn-and-his-mother",title:"The Fawn and His Mother",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Courage",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A curious fawn asks why his swift, strong mother still feels afraid of barking dogs. Together, they find a calm way to face her worry and choose a safe path.",moral:"Being brave does not mean never feeling afraid; it means finding a wise way forward, one small step at a time.",leftPage:"One bright morning, a young fawn watched his mother leap easily over a fallen branch. “You are swift and strong,” he said. “Why do you hurry away when dogs bark in the distance?” Just then, a sharp woof echoed from beyond the meadow. Mother’s ears lifted, and she began to tremble. The fawn saw that her fear was real, even though she was powerful.",rightPage:"“I cannot always stop myself from feeling afraid,” Mother admitted. The fawn stood beside her, and they took slow breaths beneath a broad oak while the distant dog’s bark faded. When the path grew quiet, Mother thanked him for staying near. They walked home together, keeping safely to the meadow trail; she still felt a flutter, but now knew she could pause and choose what to do."},
{id:"the-ass-the-fox-and-the-lion",title:"The Ass, the Fox, and the Lion",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Trust and honesty",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"An Ass spots a Fox’s secret plan and uses a loud bray to escape danger, learning to choose honest companions.",moral:"Choose friends who keep their promises, and think carefully when something feels wrong.",leftPage:"On a warm morning in ancient Greece, an Ass and a Fox agreed to travel together and look out for each other. Soon they met a Lion resting beside the path. The Fox quietly offered to lead the Ass into a hollow, hoping to win the Lion’s favor. But the Ass heard the whisper and noticed the Fox’s eyes darting toward him.",rightPage:"“Friends don’t make secret plans that put one another in danger,” said the Ass. He stamped and gave a loud bray that echoed through the olive trees. The Lion, startled by the sudden noise, stepped back, and the Ass trotted safely toward a nearby farm. The Fox was left alone with the Lion and hurried away, wishing he had chosen honesty. From then on, the Ass picked companions who kept their promises."},
{id:"the-flies-and-the-honey-pot",title:"The Flies and the Honey-Pot",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Carefulness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A group of flies find a spilled honey jar and learn that a little patience can keep a sweet treat from becoming a sticky problem.",moral:"Enjoy good things thoughtfully; a little care can help everyone stay safe.",leftPage:"One warm morning, a honey jar slipped from a shelf and tipped onto a broad plate in the kitchen. A dozen curious flies soon discovered the golden puddle. They swooped down for a taste, laughing at their lucky find. But when they tried to lift off again, their feet clung to the sticky honey, and their wings could not flutter freely.",rightPage:"A little fly named Pip called, “Let’s stop tugging and ask for help!” Their buzzing reached Lina, the kind kitchen helper. She gently placed a clean leaf beside the plate, then used a spoon to loosen each fly and set it on the leaf. After resting in the sunshine, they flew away—this time taking turns at a safe drop of honey on a flower."},
{id:"the-farmer-and-the-snake",title:"The Farmer and the Snake",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Kindness and Caution",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"On a frosty morning, a farmer helps a cold snake warm up, then safely guides it back to the wild.",moral:"Be kind, but remember to treat wild creatures with care and keep a safe distance.",leftPage:"One frosty morning, a farmer found a snake curled beneath a hedge, too cold to move. He felt sorry for it and carried it toward his cottage. Then he paused: a wild snake might be frightened when it woke. Instead of bringing it indoors, he placed it gently in a sunny patch beside a warm stone and watched from a few steps away.",rightPage:"As the sun warmed the stone, the snake lifted its head and flicked its tongue. Startled by the farmer nearby, it gave a warning hiss. The farmer stepped back and used a long branch to clear a path toward the meadow. The snake glided into the grass unharmed, and the farmer went home glad he had helped without forgetting to be careful."},
{id:"the-man-and-the-lion",title:"The Man and the Lion",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Fairness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A traveler and a lion debate who is stronger, then discover that a statue may tell only its maker’s side of the story.",moral:"Before deciding what is true, listen to more than one side.",leftPage:"One bright morning, a traveler and a lion walked along a forest path, each sure that his kind was strongest. The traveler pointed to his broad shoulders; the lion showed his powerful paws. Their friendly boasting grew louder, but neither could prove his claim. At a clearing, they found a stone statue showing a man standing proudly above a lion.",rightPage:"The traveler smiled. “There—you see, a man has overcome a lion!” The lion studied the carving and answered, “A person made this statue. If lions carved stone, perhaps it would show things another way.” The traveler paused, then laughed at himself. They continued down the path, agreeing that one picture cannot tell the whole story—and that every side deserves a voice."},
{id:"the-mountain-in-labor",title:"The Mountain in Labor",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Surprises",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A rumbling mountain draws a worried crowd, but its surprising visitor is very small.",moral:"A great deal of noise may lead to a small surprise; wait and see before you worry.",leftPage:"One morning in ancient Greece, a tall mountain began to rumble. Pebbles hopped, and a soft puff of dust drifted from its side. Shepherds paused, birds fluttered into the sky, and soon people hurried over from nearby villages. “Something enormous must be happening,” they whispered, gathering at a safe distance to see what the mountain would do.",rightPage:"They waited and waited. At last, a tiny mouse poked its nose from a little hole, whiskers twitching, then scurried across the grass. The mountain grew quiet. The villagers smiled at their worried guesses and let the mouse go on its way. What sounded like a grand event had ended with one small visitor—and no disaster at all."},
{id:"the-bear-and-the-fox",title:"The Bear and the Fox",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A boastful bear says he is considerate of people when they are far away. A fox reminds him that true kindness matters when others are right there, too.",moral:"Kindness counts most when we show it to those around us.",leftPage:"One bright morning in an olive grove, Bear spotted Fox and began praising his own good manners. “I’m so considerate of people,” he declared, “that I never bother them when they are tucked away in their homes.” Fox listened, while a family of villagers came along the path carrying baskets of olives and bread.",rightPage:"“That is thoughtful,” Fox replied, “but it would be kinder still to be gentle when people are right here beside you.” Just then, Bear noticed he was standing across the narrow path. He stepped aside, held a low branch so the family could pass, and greeted them with a friendly smile. Fox nodded: Bear’s care was finally in the right place."},
{id:"the-tortoise-and-the-eagle",title:"The Tortoise and the Eagle",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A tortoise begs an eagle to carry her into the sky, then falls to her death when he lets her go.",moral:"Not every wish is safe to pursue; think carefully about what you can do safely.",leftPage:"A tortoise watched sea birds flying and complained that she could never fly. An eagle asked what reward she would offer for a ride. The tortoise promised him all the riches of the Red Sea. The eagle agreed, lifted her high in his talons, and carried her toward the clouds.",rightPage:"Then the eagle let her go. The tortoise fell from the sky onto a rocky mountain and died. In her final moment, she understood that she had asked for something she could not safely do. A tortoise can travel steadily on the ground, but cannot fly among the clouds."},
{id:"the-fox-and-the-goat",title:"The Fox and the Goat",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Think Before You Leap",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A thirsty goat follows a fox into a well, then must find a way back out after the fox leaves without keeping his promise.",moral:"Pause to think before you leap, and remember that trust is built by keeping promises.",leftPage:"One hot afternoon, Fox slipped into an old stone well while chasing a fallen fig. The sides were too smooth for him to climb. Soon Goat came by, thirsty from the dusty path. “Is the water cool?” she asked. “Delightful,” said Fox, hiding his worry. Goat leaned over, lost her footing, and splashed gently into the water beside him.",rightPage:"Fox proposed that Goat brace herself against the wall so he could climb onto her back and reach the rim. He scrambled out, then hurried away without helping. Goat called loudly, and a shepherd nearby lowered a rope and bucket. With his help she climbed to safety. Afterward, she always looked carefully before stepping near a well—and Fox’s promises no longer fooled her."},
{id:"the-raven-and-the-swan",title:"The Raven and the Swan",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Aesopic tradition, Townsend collection (1867); authorship uncertain",theme:"Self-Acceptance",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A raven tries to copy a swan’s pale feathers, then discovers that his own gifts are worth keeping.",moral:"You do not need to become someone else to be wonderful; care for the gifts that are already yours.",leftPage:"One bright morning, a raven watched a swan glide across a lake, her white feathers shining in the sun. “Perhaps the water makes her so bright,” he wondered. Hoping to look just like her, he left the familiar fields where he found plenty to eat and settled beside the lake, dipping and rinsing his dark wings again and again.",rightPage:"At last, the raven looked at his reflection and saw that his feathers were still as dark as ever. But he also noticed his own strong wings and clever eyes. A nearby swan showed him where berries grew along the bank, and soon he was enjoying a fine supper. The raven flew home content, glad to be himself and to know a new place, too."},
{id:"my-lord-bag-of-rice",title:"My Lord Bag of Rice",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Ozaki, Japanese Fairy Tales (1908); adapted retelling",theme:"Courage and kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A fearless traveler helps a dragon king protect his home, and receives a humble gift that proves generous and magical.",moral:"A brave heart and a helpful spirit can bring unexpected blessings.",leftPage:"Long ago in Japan, a traveler named Hidesato reached a bridge where a great dragon lay across the way. Though startled, he stepped carefully past without harming it. The dragon became a courteous man, the ruler of a lake palace, and asked Hidesato for help: a many-legged creature was troubling his family, and he needed a brave friend.",rightPage:"Hidesato went with the lake ruler and waited patiently through the night. When the troublesome creature appeared, he used his bow to drive it away, and the palace grew peaceful again. Grateful, the ruler gave him a bell, a fine cloth, and a small bag of rice that never emptied. Hidesato shared the rice with neighbors, who joyfully called him Lord Bag of Rice."},
{id:"the-tongue-cut-sparrow",title:"The Tongue-Cut Sparrow",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Ozaki, Japanese Fairy Tales (1908); adapted retelling",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A kind woodcutter searches for the sparrow he loves and discovers that a small mistake can be met with patience, forgiveness, and friendship.",moral:"Kindness and forgiveness can help friends find their way back to one another.",leftPage:"Long ago in Japan, a woodcutter shared his home and supper with a little sparrow. One day, the bird mistook a bowl of rice paste for food and ate it. The woodcutter’s wife, upset about her laundry, shooed the sparrow outdoors before it could explain. When the woodcutter came home and found the bird gone, he set off through the hills to look for his friend.",rightPage:"At the edge of a bamboo grove, the sparrow greeted him with a cheerful chirp and led him to a cozy home among the stalks. Her family welcomed the woodcutter with warm tea and a lively dance. He invited the sparrow to visit again, then told his wife how kindly they had treated him. She felt sorry, and when the sparrow returned, they shared supper together."},
{id:"the-story-of-urashima-taro-the-fisher-lad",title:"The Story of Urashima Taro, the Fisher Lad",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Ozaki, Japanese Fairy Tales (1908); adapted retelling",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A kind fisher helps a sea turtle and visits a shining palace beneath the waves. When he returns to a changed village, a new friend helps him find a place for his memories and stories.",moral:"A kind deed can lead to unexpected friendships; sharing what we learn can help us feel at home.",leftPage:"One bright morning, Urashima Taro saw children crowding around a small sea turtle. He gently asked them to let it go, and they agreed when he offered his lunch fish in exchange. Taro carried the turtle to the shore and watched it paddle away. The next day, the turtle returned, bowed its head, and invited him to visit its home beneath the waves.",rightPage:"Taro rode safely on the turtle’s broad shell to a shining palace, where friendly sea folk welcomed him with music and pearly fruits. Though he enjoyed their company, he soon missed his village. The turtle carried him home with a keepsake box. Many years had passed, and the houses had changed, but a child from Taro’s family welcomed him and listened to his stories. Together they made a new home by the sea."},
{id:"the-farmer-and-the-badger",title:"The Farmer and the Badger",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Ozaki, Japanese Fairy Tales (1908); adapted retelling",theme:"Honesty",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A farmer discovers a badger nibbling his crops and learns why the visitor has been sneaking into the field. A truthful conversation leads them to a fair, friendly solution.",moral:"Honesty makes it easier to solve a problem together.",leftPage:"One autumn morning, a farmer found muddy pawprints winding through his vegetable rows. Several turnips had been nibbled, and he worried there would not be enough for winter. That evening he waited beside the garden. A small badger crept out, holding a turnip, then froze when the farmer called gently, “Why are you taking my food?”",rightPage:"The badger lowered his head and admitted his family was hungry after their hillside berries ran dry. The farmer listened, then showed him a patch of fallen vegetables he could share, asking that the growing rows be left alone. The badger agreed and helped gather weeds around the patch. By winter, both neighbors had enough, and neither needed to sneak or worry."},
{id:"the-adventures-of-kintaro-the-golden-boy",title:"The Adventures of Kintaro, the Golden Boy",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Ozaki, Japanese Fairy Tales (1908); adapted retelling",theme:"Friendship",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"In a mountain clearing, strong Kintaro invites his animal friends to a playful wrestling match. When one round turns unfair, he helps them make things right and enjoy the game together.",moral:"A good friend makes sure everyone gets a fair turn.",leftPage:"In Japan’s Ashigara Mountains, young Kintaro was famous for his strength and his friendship with the forest animals. One bright morning, he and his companions—the bear, deer, monkey, and hare—found a sunny clearing. “Let’s hold a friendly wrestling match,” Kintaro said. The deer offered to judge, while the bear shaped a soft ring in the grass.",rightPage:"The monkey and hare began their round, but soon the monkey tugged the hare’s long ear to gain an advantage. Kintaro gently called, “That isn’t fair play.” The monkey let go and apologized. They tried again, this time keeping their paws to themselves, and laughed as they tumbled softly onto the grass. Kintaro shared rice cakes with everyone, winners and friends alike."},
{id:"the-story-of-princess-hase",title:"The Story of Princess Hase",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Ozaki, Japanese Fairy Tales (1908); adapted retelling",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A gifted young princess is sent away by a jealous stepmother, but a loyal helper keeps her safe until her father finds her.",moral:"Kindness and loyalty can guide us through difficult times.",leftPage:"Long ago in Nara, Princess Hase was born after her parents prayed at Hase Temple. She grew into a thoughtful girl who loved poems and helped others. One day, her verse helped quiet a rushing river and comfort the emperor. Yet the praise made her stepmother jealous, and she sent Hase to a mountain cottage. The family’s faithful helper, Katoda, chose to stay and keep the girl safe.",rightPage:"When Hase’s father returned, he could not find his daughter and searched the hills. At last, he heard a clear voice reciting a prayer from a little cottage. There was Hase, safe with Katoda. She told her father what had happened, and he brought her home, grateful to the loyal helper. Hase’s kindness and poems were cherished, and she lived happily with her family."},
{id:"the-story-of-the-man-who-did-not-wish-to-die",title:"Sentaro and the Island Where Time Stood Still",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Ozaki, Japanese Fairy Tales (1908); adapted retelling",theme:"Contentment",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Sentaro dreams of a life without end and learns to value the ordinary days he already has.",moral:"A long life is not the same as a happy one; make today worthwhile.",leftPage:"Sentaro had a comfortable home, but one day he began to worry that sickness or death might take him away. He wished he could live forever. At a shrine, he prayed to Jofuku for a way to escape life’s end, then drifted into a vivid dream. A paper crane carried him to an island where no one ever grew old or ill.",rightPage:"At first Sentaro was delighted, but after many years of the same unchanging days, he missed home and wished to return. In his dream, the crane carried him back, and Jofuku’s messenger gently reminded him that endless life would not bring happiness by itself. Sentaro awoke at the shrine, went home, and began working with care, grateful for the days he could share with others."},
{id:"the-bamboo-cutter-and-the-moon-child",title:"The Bamboo-Cutter and the Moon-Child",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Ozaki, Japanese Fairy Tales (1908); adapted retelling",theme:"Family",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A bamboo-cutter and his wife find a tiny child inside a shining stalk and raise her with loving care. When the Moon-Child’s family calls her home, she leaves with gratitude and keeps her earthly family close in memory.",moral:"Love can make a family, and the care we share stays with us even when we must part.",leftPage:"One evening, a bamboo-cutter spotted a stalk glowing softly among the green leaves. Inside sat a tiny girl, no bigger than his hand. He and his wife welcomed her home, naming her Kaguya, and loved her as their own. She grew bright and kind, filling their little house with laughter and wonder. The old couple no longer felt alone.",rightPage:"As Kaguya grew older, she told her parents she had come from the Moon. One clear night, silver-robed visitors arrived to bring her home. She hugged the bamboo-cutter and his wife, thanked them for every kindness, and promised to remember them. Then she rose gently toward the moon. From that night on, its calm glow reminded the couple that their love had traveled with her."},
{id:"the-mirror-of-matsuyama",title:"The Mirror of Matsuyama",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Ozaki, Japanese Fairy Tales (1908); adapted retelling",theme:"Family and remembrance",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A daughter cherishes the mirror her mother left behind, believing it helps her see her mother. When her father discovers her secret, he understands the love behind it.",moral:"Remembering someone with love can help their kindness live on in us.",leftPage:"In a quiet village in Matsuyama, a kind mother gave her young daughter a little polished mirror before illness carried her away. “When you miss me, look here and remember my love,” she whispered. The girl treasured it. Years later, when her father returned from a long journey, he found his daughter gazing into the mirror and grew puzzled. He gently asked what she saw there.",rightPage:"“Mother is looking back at me,” the girl said, pointing to the face in the glass. Her father leaned closer, then smiled as he recognized how much his daughter resembled the mother he missed. He understood: the mirror had become a comfort, and his daughter had kept her mother’s loving words close. He sat beside her, and together they shared their favorite memories of her mother."},
{id:"the-goblin-of-adachigahara",title:"The Goblin of Adachigahara",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Ozaki, Japanese Fairy Tales (1908); adapted retelling",theme:"Courage",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A lost pilgrim shelters at a lonely cottage, discovers its secret, and escapes the Adachigahara goblin by keeping courage until dawn.",moral:"When something feels wrong, trust your caution, keep moving toward safety, and seek help.",leftPage:"Long ago, on the wide plain of Adachigahara in Japan, a Buddhist pilgrim lost his way as evening fell. A lantern gleamed at a lonely cottage, where an old woman welcomed him beside a warm fire. Before sleeping, she warned him not to open the back room. But a strange rustle and glimmer came from behind its screen, and the curious pilgrim peeked inside.",rightPage:"Among scattered hats and walking staffs, he saw the old woman's shadow stretch into a goblin's shape. He hurried outside, whispering his prayer as the goblin called after him. The plain seemed endless, but he kept going, one steady step at a time. At sunrise, the goblin vanished with the night mist. Safe on the road, the pilgrim gave thanks and chose a brighter path."},
{id:"womens-words-part-flesh-and-blood",title:"The Farmer’s Wife and the Island of Treasure",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Wilhelm, The Chinese Fairy Book (1921); version varies",theme:"Contentment",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A patient farmer’s single millet sprout brings him an unexpected chance to prosper. When his jealous older brother follows, he learns that wanting more than he needs can lead him into trouble.",moral:"Being content with enough can be wiser than reaching for everything.",leftPage:"Two brothers shared a home, but their trust grew thin when the older brother’s wife secretly cooked most of the millet seed she was asked to lend. One uncooked grain sprouted in the younger brother’s field. He carefully tended it until it grew tall, and a great bird carried its giant ear across the sea to a bright island of treasure.",rightPage:"The younger brother took only a handful and returned safely, using it to make a good life. Jealous, his older brother followed the bird and gathered far too much. When the bird warned that sunrise was near, he finally listened and let it carry him home. He apologized to his brother, and they learned to share honestly and be thankful for enough."},
{id:"the-three-rhymsters",title:"The Three Rhymsters",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Wilhelm, The Chinese Fairy Book (1921); version varies",theme:"Quick thinking",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"At a birthday feast, a farmer is teased during a rhyme contest until his clever wife turns his odd verse into a playful scene.",moral:"A quick mind can shine in unexpected ways, so treat others with respect.",leftPage:"At a birthday feast, three sisters arrived with their husbands. The physician and the magistrate, proud of their learning, challenged the farmer husband to make a verse about four odd things: something in the sky, a tiger on the ground, scissors on the table, and a stable boy in the room. He stumbled through his words, and the others began to chuckle.",rightPage:"Then the farmer’s quick-witted wife stepped from behind a curtain. “It all makes sense as a little play,” she said: “a moonbeam lights the stage, a painted tiger puppet prowls, scissors trim its paper mask, and the stable boy comes in to drum for the maid’s dance.” The guests praised her clever turn and cheerfully drank their own penalty cups."},
{id:"the-magic-cask",title:"The Magic Cask",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Wilhelm, The Chinese Fairy Book (1921); version varies",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A magical cask brings a family riches, but a pile of caps helps a farmer see that his grandfather needs care more than work.",moral:"Kindness matters more than endless riches; share what you have and let others rest.",leftPage:"A farmer found a great clay cask while digging in his field. He carried it home, and his wife brushed it clean. To their amazement, the cask filled with brushes as quickly as she lifted them out. They sold the brushes and soon had enough to live comfortably. Then a coin slipped from the farmer’s pocket into the cask. At once, brushes vanished, and bright coins began to appear instead. Rich at last, they thought.",rightPage:"One afternoon, Grandfather’s soft red cap slipped into the cask. Out came cap after cap until the room looked like a mountain of bright hats. The farmer saw his tired grandfather laughing beneath them and felt ashamed. He helped him out, apologized, and lifted the coin from the cask. The magic stopped; together they kept enough for their needs, shared the rest, and gave Grandfather a well-earned rest."},
{id:"the-bird-with-nine-heads",title:"The Bird with Nine Heads",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Wilhelm, The Chinese Fairy Book (1921); version varies",theme:"Honesty",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A shepherd helps a princess escape a cliffside cave, and a humble token reveals who truly rescued her.",moral:"Tell the truth, and let kind deeds speak for themselves.",leftPage:"One windy afternoon, a storm swept Princess Mei to a high cliff cave, where a great bird with nine heads kept watch. A young shepherd, An, spotted her and gathered villagers to lower a basket. Mei soothed the bird’s sore wing until all nine heads dozed. Before climbing into the basket, she gave An half of her jade hairpin, promising it would show who truly helped her.",rightPage:"The villagers pulled Mei up, but their boastful leader claimed he had rescued her and left An below. A kind river dragon carried An safely down the cliff, and he reached the palace just as Mei was asked to name her rescuer. She fitted her hairpin to An’s half and told the truth. The leader apologized; Mei returned to thank the bird, and the nine heads waved farewell."},
{id:"the-great-flood",title:"The Great Flood",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Wilhelm, The Chinese Fairy Book (1921); version varies",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A kind boy and his mother shelter a traveler, prepare for a flood, and rescue animals and a stranger as the waters rise.",moral:"Kindness matters most when we offer help to everyone in need.",leftPage:"A kind boy and his mother welcomed a tired old traveler, who became like a grandmother to him. Before leaving, she warned that a great flood was coming, and showed them how to prepare a tiny wooden boat. When strange red marks appeared on the stone lions by the town gate, the boy hurried home. The boat grew large enough to carry them as rain filled the streets.",rightPage:"On the rising water, they gathered a dog, cat, mice, raven, and a drenched swarm of bees into their boat. A frightened man drifted past, and the boy insisted they save him too. When the waters fell, the man claimed their treasure, but the raven returned with a letter from the old traveler, revealing she was a guardian spirit. The judge saw the truth, and the boy, his mother, and their animal friends were free to begin again on dry ground."},
{id:"the-herd-boy-and-the-weaving-maiden",title:"The Herd Boy and the Weaving Maiden",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Wilhelm, The Chinese Fairy Book (1921); version varies",theme:"Keeping Promises",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A herdboy and a sky-weaving maiden become friends, then keep their promise to meet each year across a river of stars.",moral:"Even when life takes us in different directions, a promise and a little kindness can keep friendship bright.",leftPage:"Long ago, a kind young herdboy cared for a golden cow, while far above him a thoughtful Weaving Maiden made shining cloth for the sky. One bright evening, the cow carried him among the clouds. The Maiden greeted him warmly, and they spoke of their different worlds. Before he returned to earth, they promised to meet again and share stories beneath the stars.",rightPage:"When the sky ruler learned of their visits, he set a broad river of stars between the heavens and earth, so each could finish their important work. The herdboy and Maiden missed one another, but kept their promise. Once each year, magpies joined wings to make a bridge across the river. The friends met there, laughed together, and carried fresh stories back to their duties."},
{id:"the-lady-of-the-moon",title:"The Lady of the Moon",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Wilhelm, The Chinese Fairy Book (1921); version varies",theme:"Love",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"When Chang’e rises to the moon after tasting a gift of immortality, she finds a gentle way to stay close to the people she loves on Earth.",moral:"Even when we are far apart, love can help us find ways to care for one another.",leftPage:"Long ago, the land grew unbearably hot beneath ten bright suns. The archer Hou Yi sent nine suns safely away, leaving one to warm the fields. Grateful, the Queen Mother gave him a little herb that could grant a very long life. Hou Yi brought it home, hoping to share its wonder with his wife, Chang’e, when the time was right.",rightPage:"One day, Chang’e tasted a tiny part of the herb and began to float gently upward. She drifted all the way to the moon, where she found a quiet palace beneath a fragrant cassia tree. Though she missed Hou Yi, she looked down and saw the fields resting in cool moonlight. Each month, she sent the kindest silver glow she could, and Hou Yi knew she was thinking of him."},
{id:"the-girl-with-the-horses-head-or-the-silkworm-goddess",title:"The Girl Who Brought Silk to Her Village",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Wilhelm, The Chinese Fairy Book (1921); version varies",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A lonely girl’s playful promise leads her white horse to bring her father home. Through a gentle change of heart, she discovers a lasting way to help her family and her village with the gift of silk.",moral:"A kind promise begins with listening and caring for one another.",leftPage:"Long ago in China, a girl lived with her father and a gentle white horse. When her father traveled far away, she missed him so much that she joked to the horse, “If you bring him home, I’ll marry you!” The horse hurried off and returned with her father riding safely on his back. The girl laughed at her foolish promise, and her father thanked the clever horse.",rightPage:"That spring, the horse’s shining mane seemed to glow like moonlit thread. The girl followed its silvery strands to a mulberry grove, where tiny silkworms were making cocoons. She learned to care for them, and soon the village had beautiful silk to weave. In time, people remembered the girl as the Silkworm Goddess, a kind guardian who returned each spring to bless the silkworms and the patient hands that tended them."},
{id:"the-dragon-princess",title:"The Dragon-Princess",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Wilhelm, The Chinese Fairy Book (1921); version varies",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A fisherman discovers a dragon-castle, and the emperor learns that a respectful message and thoughtful gifts can open the way to a generous exchange with the Dragon-Princess.",moral:"A kind request and a thoughtful gift can build trust.",leftPage:"By the Sea of Dongting, a fisherman stumbled into a deep hill tunnel and followed its winding passages until he reached a bright dragon-castle. A small dragon guarded the gate, so the fisherman wisely kept his distance and found his way home. When the emperor heard his account, he asked how to approach the Dragon-Princess, keeper of the sea’s pearls, with respect.",rightPage:"An old guide advised sending a courteous letter and thoughtful gifts, not demands. Two messengers carried jade boxes, fragrant wood, and roasted swallows to the castle. The princess welcomed them, read the emperor’s request, and sent back a handful of shining pearls. A dragon carried the messengers swiftly to the riverbank, and the emperor treasured the pearls—and the lesson in kindness."},
{id:"the-frog-princess",title:"The Frog Princess",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Wilhelm, The Chinese Fairy Book (1921); version varies",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Sia marries the Frog King’s daughter, but his careless temper strains their home. When he learns to listen, apologize, and treat his wife and her frog kin with respect, they find their way back to happiness.",moral:"Respect and a sincere apology can help mend hurt feelings.",leftPage:"Long ago beside the Yangtze River, a boy named Sia learned that the Frog King’s daughter was meant to be his bride. As they grew up, Sia’s family welcomed the kind princess, whose visits brought good fortune. But Sia often teased the frogs around their home, and when his wife asked him to be gentle, he grew cross and sent her away.",rightPage:"Soon Sia saw how lonely the house felt without her. He remembered his wife’s kindness and wished he had listened. When the princess returned, he apologized and promised to treat her and the frogs with care. She forgave him, and they agreed to speak kindly when they disagreed. Their home grew peaceful again, and the Frog Princess stayed close to Sia’s family for many happy years."},
{id:"how-the-rajas-son-won-the-princess-labam",title:"How the Raja’s Son Won the Princess Labam",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892); variants vary",theme:"Kindness and gratitude",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A prince follows the clue of a talking parrot, and kindness to small and great creatures helps him reach Princess Labam’s shining kingdom.",moral:"Kindness given along the way can return as help when you need it most.",leftPage:"A young Raja’s son was told not to hunt beyond the familiar hills. But there he heard a talking parrot speak of Princess Labam, and curiosity became a brave quest. On the road, he shared food with ants and gently helped a tiger with a sore paw. Each promised to remember his kindness. Later, he cleverly settled a quarrel over four magical gifts and set off on a flying bed.",rightPage:"The flying bed carried him to Labam’s faraway country, where a kind old woman sheltered him. At night, he saw the princess shine like a lamp above the rooftops. With help from his magical gifts, he sent her lovely treasures, and she invited him to meet her openly. He told her of his journey and the friends who helped him; Labam admired his kind heart, and they chose a happy life together."},
{id:"the-lambikin",title:"The Lambikin",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892); variants vary",theme:"Quick thinking",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A little lambkin promises to share a treat with several hungry animals after visiting his granny. With her help, he finds a clever and friendly way to get home safely.",moral:"A clever idea can help solve a problem, and sharing can turn worry into friendship.",leftPage:"A little Lambikin set off along a sunny path to visit his Granny. A jackal, a vulture, and other hungry animals stopped him and said they hoped to catch him for supper. Lambikin thought quickly: “Let me visit Granny first; she’s making a lovely feast, and there may be a bite for you, too!” Curious about the feast, the animals let him pass.",rightPage:"At Granny’s cottage, Lambikin enjoyed warm porridge and sweet apples. When it was time to go, she tucked him inside a roomy wooden berry drum, which rolled gently down the path. The animals heard it humming and asked if Lambikin was inside. He popped out and invited them to share Granny’s extra treats. They all went back together, laughing, and promised to ask before visiting next time."},
{id:"punchkin",title:"Punchkin",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892); variants vary",theme:"Courage",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"When the magician Punchkin traps Princess Balna’s family, her brave son journeys to find the hidden source of his power and free them.",moral:"Courage grows stronger when we use it to help others—and kindness can turn allies into friends.",leftPage:"Long ago, clever Princess Balna lived with her family in a palace. A powerful magician named Punchkin trapped them behind enchanted doors, hoping no one could challenge him. Balna’s young son discovered the secret: Punchkin’s strength depended on a tiny green parrot hidden far away, beyond a ring of palm trees and watchful spirits. He set out to find it, though the journey seemed impossible.",rightPage:"On the way, the prince rescued two young eagles from a danger in the forest, and their grateful parents promised to help. With their help, he reached the hidden grove and gently carried the parrot away. Punchkin begged him to return it, but the prince asked him to release everyone and give up his spells. Punchkin agreed; his magic faded, the doors opened, and Balna’s family welcomed the helpful eagles home."},
{id:"the-magic-fiddle",title:"The Magic Fiddle",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892); variants vary",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Mira’s voice lives on in a magical bamboo fiddle until her family hears her story and makes things right.",moral:"Listening and sharing can help mend hurt feelings.",leftPage:"Long ago, seven brothers lived with their sister, Mira, who cooked while they worked. Their wives unfairly blamed her whenever dinner was late, and Mira felt lonely. One noon, at the village pond, the water shimmered away whenever she lowered her pot. A gentle forest spirit carried her safely into a tall bamboo grove, where she became a voice inside a hollow stalk, waiting to be heard.",rightPage:"Years later, a wandering musician shaped the bamboo into a fiddle. Its tender tune brought Mira’s brothers to tears, and a village headman’s son recognized her voice and listened. When Mira stepped from the fiddle, she told her family how lonely she had been. Her brothers apologized, and the wives promised to share the cooking. Mira returned home gladly, and the fiddle played a cheerful song for everyone."},
{id:"loving-laili",title:"Loving Laili",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892); variants vary",theme:"Love and care",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Prince Majnun finds the fairy Laili by a lotus lake, and with the help of his loyal friend Husain, begins a new life with her. When danger comes, Laili’s wisdom helps them find a safe way home.",moral:"Care means listening to one another and helping each other through trouble.",leftPage:"Prince Majnun was traveling with his loyal friend Husain when he glimpsed a beautiful fairy, Laili, reflected in a lotus lake. He searched until he found her on the far bank, and the two soon became dear to each other. Husain stayed beside Majnun through every twist of their journey, helping him reach Laili’s garden, where they promised to build a life together.",rightPage:"Later, Laili warned Majnun that a proud ruler in a neighboring garden meant trouble. This time Majnun listened. Together, he and Laili pretended to admire a maze of flowers, then slipped through its winding paths while Husain waited with their horse at the gate. They all rode safely home, where King Dantal welcomed Laili and celebrated the friends who had helped one another."},
{id:"the-soothsayers-son",title:"The Soothsayer’s Son",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892); variants vary",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Gangazara helps several creatures in need, and their friendship later saves him from an unfair accusation and leads him to unexpected happiness.",moral:"Kindness can make friends in unexpected places, and true friends remember it.",leftPage:"After his father died, Gangazara received a horoscope predicting hard times and a later happiness. On his journey he found a tiger, serpent, and rat trapped in an old well. Though warned to be careful, he helped each climb out, then rescued a goldsmith too. The goldsmith thanked him, while the three animals promised to help whenever he needed them.",rightPage:"Later, the tiger brought Gangazara a royal crown it had found. A greedy goldsmith claimed Gangazara had stolen it, and the prince sent him to prison for ten years. His animal friends made a little tunnel and helped him escape; then they showed the prince the truth. Gangazara was welcomed. At the seashore he met his brother and fainted with joy; a blessing revived him, and soon he married the princess and became king."},
{id:"harisarman",title:"Harisarman",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892); variants vary",theme:"Honesty",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A neglected man pretends to be a seer, but lucky turns of events lead him to admit the truth and earn trust.",moral:"A lucky guess may impress people, but honesty earns lasting trust.",leftPage:"Harisarman was poor and often overlooked. At a wedding feast, no one remembered to offer him food. Feeling hurt, he told his wife he would pretend to be a wise predictor. Soon, a guest’s horse wandered away. Harisarman confidently said it would be found near the southern road; the searchers found it grazing there, and everyone praised his “magic.”",rightPage:"At court, the king asked Harisarman to find stolen gold. Alone, Harisarman scolded his own tongue for pretending to know magic. Maid Jihva, who had hidden the gold, heard him and thought he knew her secret; she confessed where it was. He showed the king and admitted his lucky guess. Asked to name a frog in a jar, Harisarman murmured “Froggie,” his childhood nickname. The king praised his honesty and offered him useful work."},
{id:"the-charmed-ring",title:"The Charmed Ring",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892); variants vary",theme:"Kindness and teamwork",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A kind merchant’s son saves three animals and receives a magical ring. When it goes missing, his loyal friends work together to bring it home.",moral:"Kindness can bring unexpected friends, and teamwork helps them solve problems.",leftPage:"A young merchant’s son used his travel money to save a dog, a cat, and a little snake from danger. Though his father was disappointed, the young man cared for his new companions. The snake led him to a kind king, who gave him a charmed ring, a pot, and a spoon to thank him for saving the snake’s life.",rightPage:"The ring could call up a cozy home, while the pot and spoon made a delicious meal. One day, a curious bird carried off the shining ring, and the home vanished. The cat and dog searched together, asking their animal friends for help until they found it. Their master thanked them, spoke to the ring, and welcomed everyone back home."},
{id:"the-talkative-tortoise",title:"The Talkative Tortoise",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892); variants vary",theme:"Think before you speak",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A chatty tortoise gets a chance to visit a mountain with two duck friends, but must remember their important rule during the journey.",moral:"A thoughtful pause can help us keep a promise and stay safe.",leftPage:"In a quiet Himalayan pond lived Tavi the tortoise, who loved to fill every silence with his stories. Two wild ducks became his friends and invited him to visit their mountain home. They would carry him on a stick, they explained, but he must keep his mouth closed all the way. Tavi promised, though staying quiet felt harder than crossing the sky.",rightPage:"As they flew above fields, curious villagers called up, and Tavi felt words bubbling up: he wanted to explain that he was traveling with friends. He began to open his mouth, then remembered the promise and held tight. The ducks carried him safely to a sunny mountain meadow. There, Tavi thanked them and later told his story one turn at a time. Even the king learned to leave space for others."},
{id:"the-gold-giving-serpent",title:"The Gold-Giving Serpent",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892); variants vary",theme:"Trust",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A farmer’s family receives a daily gold coin from a serpent in return for a bowl of milk. When the farmer’s son reaches for more than the gift freely offered, the serpent withdraws. Patience and an honest apology help them begin again.",moral:"A gift is best received with gratitude, not greed.",leftPage:"Haridatta worked hard on his little farm, but the harvest was small. One hot afternoon he noticed a serpent resting beside an anthill and left it a bowl of milk as a respectful greeting. The next morning, a gold coin lay in the bowl. This happened day after day. When Haridatta had to visit the village, his son took the milk instead—and began wishing for a whole heap of coins.",rightPage:"The boy leaned toward the anthill, hoping to find the serpent’s hidden treasure, but it slipped away and the bowl stayed empty. When Haridatta returned, he explained that the coins had been a kind gift, never a promise of riches. Together they left fresh milk and a sincere apology. After a pause, the serpent returned and placed one coin in the bowl. The family thanked it, and from then on they accepted each gift patiently."},
{id:"the-farmer-and-the-money-lender",title:"The Farmer and the Money-Lender",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892); variants vary",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A farmer’s kindness earns him a wish-granting conch, which he uses to bring water and fairness to his village.",moral:"Kindness and sharing can turn even a difficult bargain toward the good of all.",leftPage:"In a sun-baked village, a farmer worked hard yet remained in debt to a money-lender. Hoping to learn how to change his luck, he set out to find Ram. He shared his last little cake with a hungry stranger beneath a tree. The stranger smiled: “I am Ram.” Touched by the farmer’s kindness, Ram gave him a conch shell that could grant a wish when blown just so.",rightPage:"Back home, the money-lender stole the shell, but could not make it work. He returned it only after demanding that every wish bring him twice as much as the farmer. When drought came, the farmer wished for water for the village. Three wells appeared—one for him and two for the lender. Instead of wishing anyone harm, the farmer asked that all three wells be shared. The lender agreed, and the village prospered together."},
{id:"raja-rasalu",title:"Raja Rasalu",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892); variants vary",theme:"Courage",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"After growing up in a hidden palace, young Rasalu rides out to meet the world. A small mishap at a village well leads him to make amends—and to find his waiting family.",moral:"Courage shines brightest when it is joined with kindness and a willingness to set things right.",leftPage:"For twelve years, Prince Rasalu grew up in a quiet palace beneath the earth, with a lively colt and a bright parrot for company. When his twelfth year arrived, he longed to see the world above. He rode out, washed at the river as his nurses had taught him, and followed a busy road toward a city, eager to discover what lay beyond his hidden home.",rightPage:"Near a village well, his colt shied at a fluttering scarf, and Rasalu’s shield tipped a row of water pots onto the soft ground. He apologized and helped the startled water-carriers gather the pieces, then sent new pots from the palace stores. Their kind words led him to the king and queen, who welcomed their brave son at last. Rasalu had found the world—and begun by helping its people."},
{id:"the-boy-who-had-a-moon-on-his-forehead-and-a-star-on-his-chin",title:"The Boy Who Had a Moon on His Forehead and a Star on His Chin",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892); variants vary",theme:"Courage",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A hidden prince with a moon mark and a star mark finds a way to show who he truly is and bring his family together again.",moral:"Your true worth shines through your actions, even when others cannot see your face or name.",leftPage:"A gardener’s daughter once wished aloud for a child with a moon on his forehead and a star on his chin. Years later, her son was born with those very marks—but jealous palace whispers kept him far from his mother. A kind old dog protected the baby, and, when the boy grew, a clever horse helped him travel safely to another kingdom, where he worked quietly as a grain seller’s helper.",rightPage:"At night, the boy sang in the palace garden, and the youngest princess recognized the gentle tune from a lullaby her mother had taught her. She invited him to a moonlit gathering, where he told his story and showed the shining marks on his face. The king sent for the boy’s mother; they embraced, and the palace welcomed them both. From then on, the boy’s song was heard at every joyful festival."},
{id:"the-demon-with-the-matted-hair",title:"The Demon with the Matted Hair",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892); variants vary",theme:"Courage",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A brave prince meets a demon who blocks a forest path. When his weapons tangle in the demon’s matted hair, the prince keeps his wits and finds a peaceful way forward.",moral:"Courage can help us stay calm, think clearly, and choose a peaceful solution.",leftPage:"Long ago, a young prince known as the Five-Weapons Prince was returning home through a deep forest. A demon with a great, tangled mass of hair stepped into the path and warned that no traveler could pass. The prince did not boast or run. He carefully tried each of his five weapons, but every one caught fast in the demon’s thick locks.",rightPage:"Now the prince stood unarmed, yet he kept his voice steady. He pointed out that the demon’s own tangled hair had trapped every weapon, and asked why they should keep struggling when they could simply speak. The demon paused, surprised by the prince’s calm. Together they cleared the path, and the demon promised to let travelers pass safely. The prince continued home, remembering that courage can make room for kindness."},
{id:"lifes-secret",title:"Life’s Secret",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Lal Behari Day, Folk-Tales of Bengal (1883)",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A prince shares the secret of the fish and necklace that protect his life, helping jealousy give way to kindness.",moral:"Honesty and kindness can turn jealousy into trust.",leftPage:"Long ago, a wandering holy visitor came to a palace where Queen Suo wished for a child. He gave her a pomegranate-flower remedy and said her future son, Dalim Kumar, would have a hidden life-secret: a great fish in the palace pond held a little box, and inside it lay a golden necklace. In time, Dalim was born and grew into a cheerful boy who loved his pigeons.",rightPage:"One afternoon, a pigeon flew into Queen Duo’s room, and she asked Dalim to explain his secret before she returned it. He told her honestly. Hearing how precious the fish and necklace were, Duo understood that jealousy had made her unkind. She gave back the bird, and together they asked the king to keep the pond peaceful and safe. From then on, both queens cheered Dalim’s games."},
{id:"phakir-chand",title:"Phakir Chand",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Lal Behari Day, Folk-Tales of Bengal (1883)",theme:"Friendship",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"When a prince is separated from his closest friend in a magical underwater palace, the minister’s son uses a clever disguise and a shining jewel to bring them together again.",moral:"A loyal friend thinks creatively and does not give up on someone they care about.",leftPage:"The king’s son and the minister’s son were inseparable friends who set out to explore the wide world. One evening, beside a quiet pond, a shining jewel revealed a path to a palace beneath the water. The king’s son ventured inside, but the path closed behind him. His friend was left on the bank, determined to find a way to bring him home.",rightPage:"In a nearby town, the minister’s son learned that a princess from the water palace was being kept in the king’s palace, and that a wanderer named Phakir Chand had a magic jewel. Disguised as Phakir, he won the jewel with a lively dance, then helped the princess slip away. Together they followed its glow beneath the pond, where the prince welcomed them with joy. The friends returned to the world above side by side."},
{id:"the-story-of-the-rakshasas",title:"The Story of the Rakshasas",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Lal Behari Day, Folk-Tales of Bengal (1883)",theme:"Honesty and kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A thoughtful Brahman and his wife meet a lonely rakshasi in a glittering, empty palace. An honest promise turns a frightening misunderstanding into a welcoming new beginning.",moral:"When we are honest about what we need, kindness can help everyone feel safe and welcome.",leftPage:"A poor Brahman set out to seek work, but followed a shining trail of coins to a quiet palace instead. A beautiful lady welcomed him as her long-lost husband and offered a grand feast. Yet the rooms were strangely empty, and no neighbors came to visit. When he asked why, the lady confessed she was a rakshasi whose nighttime roars had frightened the whole village away.",rightPage:"The Brahman’s wife soon came to find him. She saw that the rakshasi was lonely, but asked her to promise to greet people honestly and never frighten them again. The rakshasi agreed. Together they used some of the palace’s treasure to mend the village road and set out a welcoming feast. Little by little, neighbors returned, and the rakshasi gained friends while the Brahman and his wife went home in peace."},
{id:"the-story-of-swet-basanta",title:"The Story of Swet-Basanta",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Lal Behari Day, Folk-Tales of Bengal (1883)",theme:"Family and kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Separated during a hurried escape, brothers Swet and Basanta find their way back to one another with help from a royal elephant, a kind traveler, and a cheerful calf.",moral:"Kindness and loyalty can help families find their way back to each other.",leftPage:"Swet and younger brother Basanta fled their home after their stepmother grew angry when Basanta rescued his pigeon. They carried a wondrous fish said to bring gems: when its eater cried, pearls appeared; when he laughed, bright jewels fell. In a dark forest, Swet went to find a light, but a royal elephant carried him far away, and the people welcomed him as king. Basanta waited, worried.",rightPage:"Basanta’s tears became pearls, and a passing merchant offered to take him to a nearby town. A cheerful calf there knew the way to the palace and led him to Swet. At the gate Basanta laughed with relief, and bright gems appeared. Swet remembered his brother, embraced him, and sent for their family. Together they left the palace, sharing the treasure and beginning a kinder life."},
{id:"the-evil-eye-of-sani",title:"The Evil Eye of Sani",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Lal Behari Day, Folk-Tales of Bengal (1883)",theme:"Perseverance",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"When Sani’s challenge brings unexpected troubles, Sribatsa and Chintamani stay hopeful, use their gifts, and find their way home together.",moral:"Hard times are easier to face with courage, kindness, and hope.",leftPage:"Long ago, Sani, the god of difficult times, and Lakshmi, the goddess of good fortune, asked the wise Sribatsa to decide who was greater. He honored Lakshmi with the golden seat, and Sani warned that troubles would follow him for three years. Sribatsa and his wife, Chintamani, set out together, trusting Lakshmi’s promise that things would be right again.",rightPage:"A sudden wonder on their journey swept away their belongings and carried the couple apart. Sribatsa kept going, found a magical cow whose golden gifts bore his name, and used his cleverness to earn an honest post by the river. There he found Chintamani safe aboard a passing boat. Reunited, they returned home, and when Sani’s three years ended, good fortune welcomed them again."},
{id:"the-ghost-brahman",title:"The Traveler and His Ghostly Double",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Lal Behari Day, Folk-Tales of Bengal (1883)",theme:"Cleverness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A traveling Brahman returns to find a ghostly double claiming his home. When the king cannot tell them apart, a young cowherd finds a clever, peaceful way to reveal the truth.",moral:"A thoughtful idea can solve a problem that strength or authority cannot.",leftPage:"A kind but poor Brahman left his Bengali village to earn enough to support his family. One evening, a ghost who looked exactly like him appeared at his door and claimed to be the master of the house. Years later, the real Brahman returned. Neighbors and even the king could not tell the two apart, and the bewildered traveler had nowhere to turn.",rightPage:"A young cowherd who played at being king heard the Brahman’s story and offered to help. At the hearing, he set out a tiny glass bottle and invited each claimant to step inside. The ghost proudly shrank to a speck and slipped through the opening. The cowherd gently corked the bottle, and the ghost admitted the home belonged to the traveler. The family welcomed the Brahman back, while the ghost was sent peacefully on its way."},
{id:"per-gynt",title:"Per Gynt",region:"Europe",country:"Norway; The Norwegian Fairy Book (1922); tale variants",theme:"Cleverness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"On a dark mountain evening, Per Gynt finds a huge, winding troll blocking the shepherds’ hut. With patience and a lantern, he helps the muddled creature untangle itself and make room for everyone.",moral:"A calm mind and a little kindness can solve a problem better than force.",leftPage:"Long ago, Per Gynt was crossing a Norwegian mountain when dusk covered the path. He hoped to reach a shepherds’ hut before night grew colder, but a huge, winding shape blocked the doorway. “Who are you?” Per called. “I’m the Crooked One,” rumbled a voice. “I’m lost,” it added, “and I can’t tell where I end!”",rightPage:"Per lit his lantern and saw that the Crooked One had looped around the hut by mistake. “Let’s follow the path back to the open hillside,” he suggested. Slowly, the troll unwound, while Per showed him where to turn. The doorway cleared, and the grateful Crooked One helped carry firewood inside. Per and the shepherds welcomed the morning together, safe and warm."},
{id:"the-isle-of-udrost",title:"The Isle of Udröst",region:"Europe",country:"Norway; The Norwegian Fairy Book (1922); tale variants",theme:"Kindness and Gratitude",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Fisherman Isaac finds a hidden island, makes unexpected friends, and learns that a little help can bring good fortune home.",moral:"When others help you, remember to share your good fortune in return.",leftPage:"Fisherman Isaac was sailing home when fog and wind hid every familiar shore. He kept his small boat steady until, at last, it glided onto a sunny island bright with green hills. A kindly old man in a blue coat welcomed him inside. Isaac soon learned that the man’s three sons, whom he had glimpsed as cormorants at sea, were friendly after all.",rightPage:"The sons invited Isaac fishing, but his hooks brought up nothing. Their father lent him better ones, and soon Isaac had a fine catch to take home. He was given a sturdy boat and later sold fish enough to buy a schooner. Remembering the island’s welcome, Isaac always left a thoughtful gift for the people who watched over boats in winter."},
{id:"the-three-lemons",title:"The Three Lemons",region:"Europe",country:"Norway; The Norwegian Fairy Book (1922); tale variants",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A cheerful young traveler follows a magical whistle and finds three enchanted lemons. With patience and a little help, he brings three sisters safely back to their home.",moral:"A kind heart and a steady mind can turn a difficult journey into a hopeful one.",leftPage:"Mika was the youngest of three brothers, and people often overlooked him because he liked whittling by the stove. On the road to seek their fortunes, he shared his lunch with a hungry old wanderer. She thanked him and pointed out a silver whistle in the moss. When Mika played it, tiny woodland helpers appeared and showed him a tower where three sisters were enchanted inside golden lemons.",rightPage:"Mika tucked the lemons safely away, then hurried toward a stream when he heard faint calls from within. One by one, he opened them beside the water, and three sisters stepped out, smiling and grateful. He shared his warm cloak with them, and together they followed the helpers home. Their family welcomed them with music and a bright feast, while Mika discovered that kindness had brought him the finest friends of all."},
{id:"the-neighbor-underground",title:"The Neighbor Underground",region:"Europe",country:"Norway; The Norwegian Fairy Book (1922); tale variants",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A struggling farmer learns why his underground neighbor needs the cow stable moved, and their cooperation brings good fortune to both homes.",moral:"Listening to a neighbor’s needs can turn a problem into a lasting friendship.",leftPage:"Long ago in Telemark, a farmer with little luck built a cow stable on his new patch of land. One morning, a friendly stranger greeted him and pointed to a beautiful farm nearby, hidden beneath the earth. “Your stable stands over my home,” he explained. “Would you move it, so my family can eat without straw and soil falling into our supper?”",rightPage:"The farmer first worried about the cold weather and the work ahead. But after visiting the underground home and seeing the trouble for himself, he agreed to move the stable. Each night, unseen helping hands made the new building rise a little faster. From then on, the farmer’s fields and cattle prospered. In a later lean season, his neighbor secretly led the herd to safe green pasture and brought every animal home healthy in spring."},
{id:"the-troll-wedding",title:"The Troll Wedding",region:"Europe",country:"Norway; The Norwegian Fairy Book (1922); tale variants",theme:"Trust",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"At a mountain pasture, a clever dog helps a young herder see through a troll-made wedding and find her real sweetheart.",moral:"When something feels wrong, pause, trust your good sense, and seek help from someone you trust.",leftPage:"Long ago, in Norway, a young herder named Liv stayed at a mountain pasture with her loyal dog, Brisk. Her cattle were calm, and she looked forward to marrying her sweetheart. One afternoon a young man who looked just like him arrived, saying they should wed at once. Though friendly guests soon filled the hut, Liv felt puzzled: her dog knew none of them.",rightPage:"Brisk raced down the mountain and led Liv’s real sweetheart back to the hut. He called Liv by name, and the spell began to fade: the guests rolled away as balls of gray yarn, while the feast became moss and mushrooms. Liv recognized her true sweetheart, and he gently helped her home. They celebrated with family in the village, keeping the bright crown as a reminder to trust kind instincts and loyal friends."},
{id:"the-hat-of-the-huldres",title:"The Hat of the Huldres",region:"Europe",country:"Norway; The Norwegian Fairy Book (1922); tale variants",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A farmhand unknowingly wears a Huldre hat that makes him invisible, then discovers why the wedding guests could not see him.",moral:"A little patience and a friendly greeting can help everyone feel welcome.",leftPage:"On his way to a wedding feast, a farmhand spotted a curious brown strainer in the grass. He tucked it into his coat, thinking it might help at home. At the feast, everyone seemed to pass him by: no greetings, no invitation to eat. Feeling unwanted, he walked home. “I’m back,” he called, though his wife could hear him but could not see him.",rightPage:"His wife noticed the strange strainer on the bench. The moment he set it down, she saw him again—and the little Huldre hat vanished, as if it had only been lent. Now he understood why the guests had not noticed him. He hurried back, returned to the feast, and this time greeted everyone cheerfully. They welcomed him to the table, where he shared a warm meal among friends."},
{id:"the-magic-apples",title:"The Magic Apples",region:"Europe",country:"Norway; The Norwegian Fairy Book (1922); tale variants",theme:"Honesty",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A young traveler lends his enchanted gifts to a princess, then helps her when a magical mishap leaves her with silver horns.",moral:"Trust grows when we return what we borrow and make things right.",leftPage:"Long ago in Norway, a young traveler named Eirik set out to see the world. A kind old woman gave him a cap that hid him from sight, a cloth that spread a feast, and a purse that never ran empty. At a palace, the princess admired the clever gifts and asked to borrow them; Eirik trusted her, but she kept them.",rightPage:"That winter, the princess ate three strange apples from a traveling peddler, and little silver horns sprouted on her forehead. Eirik returned wearing his hiding cap and offered three antidote apples, one at a time. As each horn shrank, the princess returned his gifts and apologized. The last apple made her forehead smooth; she thanked Eirik, and they shared the feast-cloth's supper with the whole palace."},
{id:"east-of-the-sun-and-west-of-the-moon",title:"East of the Sun and West of the Moon",region:"Europe",country:"Norway; The Norwegian Fairy Book (1922); tale variants",theme:"Courage",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A brave girl searches for the prince taken by an enchantment and helps him break free.",moral:"Courage and patience can carry you through a difficult journey.",leftPage:"In a Norwegian valley, a poor family worried about winter. A white bear promised them a warm home if their youngest daughter chose to travel with him. At his shining palace, the bear became a kind prince under an enchantment. Each night he visited her, but she was asked to wait until the spell was broken. Curious, she lit a candle one evening; the light revealed him, and the spell swept him away to a castle beyond the mountains.",rightPage:"The girl asked the winds for help, and the North Wind carried her across snowy peaks to the distant castle. There, the prince was promised to someone else, and a shirt with three wax marks was set out as an impossible test. He recognized the girl and asked her to try. She washed gently, the marks disappeared, and the enchantment broke. They returned together, opening the palace as a warm welcome for weary travelers."},
{id:"kari-woodencoat",title:"Kari Woodencoat",region:"Europe",country:"Norway; The Norwegian Fairy Book (1922); tale variants",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A kind princess and a helpful blue bull journey through enchanted forests to a new home, where Kari’s identity is finally recognized.",moral:"Kindness and patience can carry us through hard times; a person’s worth is more than their appearance.",leftPage:"Kari was a kind princess, but when her father traveled away, her stepfamily sent her to tend cows and gave her little to eat. A gentle blue bull noticed her tears and shared a magic cloth that spread a warm supper whenever she needed one. Soon the jealous queen discovered their secret, and Kari and the bull set off together, seeking a safe home.",rightPage:"Through three shining forests, Kari accidentally gathered a copper leaf, a silver leaf, and a golden apple. At a faraway castle, she wore a clattering wooden coat and worked in the kitchen. On festival Sundays, a mountain friend gave her bright gowns to wear to church; the prince admired the mysterious visitor, who left behind a golden shoe. When it fit Kari, her true name was known, and she was welcomed as a princess."},
{id:"soria-moria-castle",title:"Soria-Moria Castle",region:"Europe",country:"Norway; The Norwegian Fairy Book (1922); tale variants",theme:"Courage",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Halvor finds courage to free three princesses and follows a magical path to the castle where he earns a joyful reunion.",moral:"Courage can help us do difficult things for others; perseverance can lead us home to what matters.",leftPage:"Halvor, once a boy who preferred daydreaming by the hearth to learning a trade, sailed away to see the world. A storm carried him to a distant shore, where he found a shining, empty castle and a princess who warned him of a troll. Though the troll seemed fearsome, Halvor found the courage to use a strength-giving drink and a great sword to free her. Then he set off to rescue her two sisters.",rightPage:"After freeing all three sisters, Halvor returned home, where the firelight helped his parents recognize their long-lost son. The youngest princess gave him a ring, but soon the sisters were far away again. Halvor kept searching: an old woman lent him swift boots, and the West Wind carried him toward Soria-Moria Castle. At the wedding feast, he showed the ring, and the youngest princess chose him to share her happy new life."},
{id:"connla-and-the-fairy-maiden",title:"Connla and the Fairy Maiden",region:"Europe",country:"Ireland; Jacobs, Celtic Fairy Tales (1892); shared variants",theme:"Family",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Connla meets a fairy maiden who offers him a wondrous journey. He discovers that choosing wonder need not mean forgetting the people he loves.",moral:"New adventures are wonderful, and love helps us find our way home.",leftPage:"One bright morning, Connla wandered along a green hill above the sea. A maiden in a blue cloak appeared, carrying a silver apple that never grew smaller, no matter how many bites she took. She told him of a shining island where music drifted through the trees. Connla longed to see it, but he thought of his father waiting at home.",rightPage:"The maiden held out her hand, and a little boat glided to shore. Connla sailed with her across calm, sparkling water, then saw his home far behind. He asked to return and tell his father of the island. The maiden smiled, turned the boat around, and brought him safely back. Connla shared the apple’s last sweet bite with his father, then promised to visit the island another day."},
{id:"guleesh",title:"Guleesh",region:"Europe",country:"Ireland; Jacobs, Celtic Fairy Tales (1892); shared variants",theme:"Courage",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A Mayo boy joins a fairy ride to France, then helps a worried princess find safety and her way home.",moral:"Courage can begin with noticing when someone needs help and listening to what they want.",leftPage:"In County Mayo, Guleesh wished for a life beyond his quiet home. One moonlit night, he followed merry fairy riders into a grassy hill and found himself racing across the sea. In France, the fairies planned to carry away a princess from her wedding feast. Seeing how unhappy and frightened she looked, Guleesh decided he must help her get somewhere safe.",rightPage:"When the fairies whisked the princess away, Guleesh helped her onto his horse and rode with her back to Ireland. He brought her to a kind priest, who sheltered her while Guleesh searched for a way to help. A strange healing herb restored her voice. She chose to return to her family, and Guleesh was thanked for listening and helping her find her own way."},
{id:"the-field-of-boliauns",title:"The Field of Boliauns",region:"Europe",country:"Ireland; Jacobs, Celtic Fairy Tales (1892); shared variants",theme:"Cleverness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Tom catches a leprechaun who offers to show him hidden gold. But when a clever trick makes every plant look like the marked one, Tom has to decide what matters more: a pot of coins or keeping his promise.",moral:"A little greed can muddle good judgment; honesty can bring peace of mind.",leftPage:"One bright morning, Tom Fitzpatrick spotted a tiny leprechaun beside a field of yellow boliauns. Tom gently asked where the little fellow kept his gold, and the leprechaun offered to show him. “It’s beneath that flower,” he said, pointing to one plant. Tom tied his blue ribbon around its stem, then hurried home to fetch a small spade.",rightPage:"When Tom returned, the field shimmered with blue ribbons: the leprechaun had marked every boliaun just alike! Tom laughed at the clever trick. He could spend all day digging, but he had promised to let the leprechaun go. So he untied the little fellow, who skipped away, and Tom walked home content, glad he had kept his word rather than chased uncertain treasure."},
{id:"the-horned-women",title:"The Horned Women",region:"Europe",country:"Ireland; Jacobs, Celtic Fairy Tales (1892); shared variants",theme:"Cleverness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A wool-worker welcomes a curious group of horned visitors, then uses a well’s clever advice to solve their puzzling request and send them home peacefully.",moral:"When a task seems impossible, pause, listen, and look for a clever solution.",leftPage:"One rainy evening in Ireland, Nóra was carding wool beside the hearth when a traveler with one tiny horn knocked. Nóra welcomed her in. Soon more horned visitors arrived, each carrying a spindle and asking to join the work. They spun quietly, then asked Nóra to bake a cake. “Bring water in this sieve,” they said, handing her one with wide holes.",rightPage:"At the well, Nóra sighed as every drop slipped away. A gentle voice from the water suggested she seal the holes with soft clay and moss; then she carried water home. At the well’s advice, she called, “The hill and sky shine bright!” The visitors hurried back to their mountain. Nóra closed the door, tucked the wool safely away, and shared warm cake with her family, glad she had listened and thought carefully."},
{id:"conall-yellowclaw",title:"Conall Yellowclaw",region:"Europe",country:"Ireland; Jacobs, Celtic Fairy Tales (1892); shared variants",theme:"Courage",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Conall and his three sons set out to find a king’s prized brown horse. A clever plan gets them into the stable, but honesty and compassion bring them safely home with the horse.",moral:"Courage matters, and honesty can open a kinder path than force.",leftPage:"When Conall’s sons made a serious mistake, a neighboring king gave their father a difficult choice: bring back the brown horse of Lochlann, or lose his sons. Conall gathered the three young men and crossed the sea. The horse’s king guarded the stable closely, so Conall asked the royal miller for help and devised a quiet way inside.",rightPage:"Hidden among sacks of bran, Conall and his sons reached the stable, but the lively horse made such a racket that the king discovered them. Conall admitted why they had come and asked forgiveness. Seeing a father trying to save his family, the king chose kindness: he let them take the brown horse home. The Irish king welcomed them, and the family began again in peace."},
{id:"hudden-and-dudden-and-donald-oneary",title:"Hudden and Dudden and Donald O’Neary",region:"Europe",country:"Ireland; Jacobs, Celtic Fairy Tales (1892); shared variants",theme:"Cleverness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A poor farmer turns a trick into a lesson for two greedy neighbors, who finally learn to share.",moral:"Cleverness is best when it helps people mend their ways and live more kindly.",leftPage:"Long ago in Ireland, Donald O’Neary lived beside two wealthy farmers, Hudden and Dudden, who envied his little patch of grass. His gentle cow, Daisy, grew old and died peacefully. Donald carried her hide to market. Before entering an inn, he tucked pennies into tiny slits in it, then let them tumble out as if the hide could make money. A trader paid him a purse of gold for this wondrous hide.",rightPage:"Hudden and Dudden copied Donald, but their trick failed at market, and they went home empty-handed. Angry, they planned to carry Donald to the Brown Lake in a sack. Donald persuaded them to look into its shining water: a meadow full of cattle seemed to lie below. As they leaned closer, they saw their own reflections and stopped. Donald explained the joke and shared pasture; the farmers apologized, and they became friends."},
{id:"munachar-and-manachar",title:"Munachar and Manachar",region:"Europe",country:"Ireland; Jacobs, Celtic Fairy Tales (1892); shared variants",theme:"Sharing",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"When Manachar eats all the berries, Munachar follows a chain of curious requests to gather what they need. The two friends make amends and share their next harvest.",moral:"When we share and help each other, a small problem can become a happy beginning.",leftPage:"Long ago, Munachar and Manachar went berry picking. Munachar filled his basket, while Manachar ate every berry he picked. “I need help before we have nothing left!” Munachar said, and set off to find a rod for a berry basket. But the rod asked for an axe, the axe needed a sharpening stone, and the stone needed water. The water led him to a deer, then a hound, and each helper asked for something else.",rightPage:"At the river, Munachar dipped the sieve, but water slipped through. A crow called, “Seal the holes with clay!” Munachar did, carried water to the miller, and soon the helpers passed along flour, straw, milk, and cream. He hurried back expecting a quarrel, but Manachar was waiting, sorry and ready to plant more raspberry bushes. Together they baked a cake and promised to share the next harvest."},
{id:"king-otoole-and-his-goose",title:"King O’Toole and His Goose",region:"Europe",country:"Ireland; Jacobs, Celtic Fairy Tales (1892); shared variants",theme:"Promises",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"When age leaves King O’Toole unable to hunt, a visitor helps his old goose regain her strength. The king keeps his promise and finds a new way to enjoy his land.",moral:"A promise matters, and sharing what you have can bring joy to many.",leftPage:"Long ago in Ireland, King O’Toole loved riding over the hills, but as he grew older he could no longer go hunting. His dear goose kept him company, swimming for fish and circling the lake, until she too became tired and could hardly fly. One morning the king met a kind stranger named Kavin, who offered to restore the goose’s strength in return for the land she flew over.",rightPage:"The king agreed, and Kavin gently lifted the goose and sent her gliding into the clear sky. She soared around the lake, over the green fields, and back to her friend, bright-eyed and strong again. King O’Toole gladly kept his promise, giving Kavin the land she had crossed. Kavin made it a welcoming place for travelers, while the king and goose enjoyed many happy days together."},
{id:"salmon-of-knowledge-fionn",title:"The Salmon of Knowledge",region:"Europe",country:"Ireland; Ask About Ireland, ‘The Salmon of Knowledge’",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Young Fionn helps the poet Finnegas cook a magical salmon said to hold all the world's knowledge. When Fionn accidentally burns his thumb and tastes it while cooling the burn, he becomes the one destined to receive the salmon's wisdom.",moral:"Curiosity and attentive learning can open the way to wisdom; share what you learn generously.",leftPage:"Long ago, young Fionn went to learn from Finnegas, a wise poet beside the River Boyne. Finnegas knew many things, but he was still searching for the Salmon of Knowledge, a magical fish said to hold all the world's wisdom. At last, he caught it and hurried home, asking Fionn to cook it carefully.",rightPage:"Finnegas warned him not to eat a bite, then stepped away for firewood. While turning the fish, Fionn burned his thumb and put it in his mouth to soothe it. Finnegas saw a new light in the boy's eyes: the prophecy had come true. He told Fionn to eat the fish and press that thumb to his lips again. Wisdom flooded Fionn's mind, and he grew to be a great leader."},
{id:"the-brewery-of-eggshells",title:"The Brewery of Eggshells",region:"Europe",country:"Ireland; Jacobs, Celtic Fairy Tales (1892); shared variants",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"When a worried mother hears a strange voice comment on her tiny eggshell pot, she learns how to resolve a suspected fairy mix-up gently.",moral:"Curiosity and kindness can solve worries more wisely than fear.",leftPage:"Long ago in Ireland, a farmer and his wife watched their twin babies closely. Though the babies were fed and cuddled, they seemed not to grow, and the parents worried that some fairy trick had happened. A wise neighbor told the mother to set a tiny eggshell pot by the hearth and listen carefully; sometimes, she said, an unusual visitor would reveal itself.",rightPage:"As the mother pretended to stir the little pot, one baby sat up and spoke in a voice far older than its face: “I have seen forests rise from acorns, but never a brewery made in an eggshell!” The mother understood, yet chose not to frighten anyone. She called kindly to the fair folk, and they came to exchange babies peacefully. Soon both families had their own little ones safe at home."},
{id:"how-we-got-the-name-spider-tales",title:"How We Got the Name ‘Spider Tales’",region:"Africa",country:"Ghana (Akan); Barker & Sinclair, West African Folk-Tales (1917)",theme:"Cleverness",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"Spider’s cleverness wins permission for old stories to be called Anansi tales.",moral:"Resourcefulness can help solve a difficult challenge.",leftPage:"Spider asked Nyankupon if the old stories could be named after him. Nyankupon set him a series of difficult tasks. Spider studied each one and used clever plans to complete them.",rightPage:"Impressed, Nyankupon agreed that the stories could bear Spider’s name. He explained that Anansi means Spider, and that is why the old stories are called Anansi tales."},
{id:"the-squirrel-and-the-spider",title:"The Squirrel and the Spider",region:"Africa",country:"Ghana (Akan); Barker & Sinclair, West African Folk-Tales (1917)",theme:"Honesty",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Spider’s attempt to take Squirrel’s harvest fails when Crow claims the bundles.",moral:"Taking what others have grown can leave everyone worse off.",leftPage:"Squirrel worked hard on a farm reached by climbing through the trees. Spider saw the growing harvest and hoped to take some for himself. He and his children carried bundles away, but Crow spotted them beside the road and claimed the food.",rightPage:"Spider and his children had no harvest to bring home, and Crow kept the bundles. Squirrel’s careful work had fed him, while Spider’s plan to take another animal’s food had failed."},
{id:"the-leopard-and-the-ram",title:"The Leopard and the Ram",region:"Africa",country:"Ghana (Akan); Barker & Sinclair, West African Folk-Tales (1917)",theme:"Caution",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A leopard mistakes a ram’s ordinary building work for a danger and runs away.",moral:"Look carefully before deciding that something is a threat.",leftPage:"A ram and a leopard each chose the same woodland clearing for a home. The ram began building, going about his work in the usual way. The leopard saw what was happening but misunderstood it, imagining that the ram was preparing to threaten him.",rightPage:"Frightened by his own mistaken idea, the leopard fled into the woods. The ram stayed in the clearing. The tale explains why the ram lives at home in the open while the leopard keeps to the woods."},
{id:"to-lose-an-elephant-for-the-sake-of-a-wren",title:"To Lose an Elephant for the Sake of a Wren Is a Very Foolish Thing to Do",region:"Africa",country:"Ghana (Akan); Barker & Sinclair, West African Folk-Tales (1917)",theme:"Carefulness",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"Spider wins an elephant through a dishonest trick, then loses it when he stops for a wren.",moral:"A dishonest shortcut can spoil the prize you hoped to win.",leftPage:"A king promised an elephant to anyone who could cut down a huge tree with a wooden axe. Spider secretly used a steel axe and distracted the servant who was watching. He felled the tree and won the elephant, though he had not followed the king’s rule.",rightPage:"On the way home, Spider stopped to retrieve a wren. While he was away, the elephant escaped. Spider and his family returned without the prize or dinner. His trick had won him an elephant only briefly."},
{id:"farmer-mybrow-and-the-fairies",title:"Farmer Mybrow and the Fairies",region:"Africa",country:"Ghana (Akan); Barker & Sinclair, West African Folk-Tales (1917)",theme:"Keeping Promises",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A family loses its fine crop after the farmer’s wife breaks an agreement with the fairies.",moral:"Respect agreements, especially when others have trusted you.",leftPage:"Farmer Mybrow chose a field beside the forest where the fairies lived. They offered to help his crop grow, on one condition: his wife must leave their work alone. The yams flourished, and Mybrow was pleased with the fairies’ help.",rightPage:"One day, Mybrow’s wife plucked a yam despite the agreement. When he returned, the fairies had ruined the field, and the family lost its crop. Their harvest was gone because the promise had not been kept."},
{id:"affair-of-the-hippopotamus-and-the-tortoise",title:"The Affair of the Hippopotamus and the Tortoise; or, Why the Hippopotamus Lives in the Water",region:"Africa",country:"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910)",theme:"Cleverness",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"Tortoise learns the hippopotamus’s name and makes him keep his promise to move to the water.",moral:"Careful listening can reveal what a boastful person hopes to keep secret.",leftPage:"At a feast, Hippopotamus said the guests could not eat unless someone discovered his private name. He promised that if Tortoise found it, he and his family would move to the water. Tortoise hid beside the path to the river and listened as Hippopotamus’s wife said, “Isantim,” after bumping her foot.",rightPage:"At the next feast, Tortoise spoke the name, and Hippopotamus kept his promise. He and his family moved to the water. The tale tells this as the reason hippopotamuses live there."},
{"id":"how-the-tortoise-overcame-the-elephant-and-the-hippopotamus","title":"How the Tortoise Overcame the Elephant and the Hippopotamus","region":"Africa","country":"Nigeria; Dayrell, Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria (1910)","theme":"Cleverness","readingTime":"3 min","summary":"Tortoise tricks Elephant and Hippopotamus into losing separate rope-pulling contests.","moral":"Strength and size are not the only ways to meet a challenge.","leftPage":"Elephant and Hippopotamus were good friends who often fed together. Tortoise said that although they were both big and strong, neither could pull him out of the water with a strong rope. He offered Elephant a bet, and Elephant agreed. Tortoise tied the rope to his own leg, fastened it around a rock, then hid underwater. Elephant pulled until the rope broke. Tortoise untied the rope from the rock and came ashore, showing that it was still tied to his leg. Elephant paid the promised twenty thousand rods.","rightPage":"After three months, Tortoise made the same bet with Hippopotamus. Hippopotamus proposed that he stay in the water while Tortoise remained on land; then he would pull Tortoise into the river. Tortoise agreed. He tied one end of a strong rope around Hippopotamus’s hind leg and told him to go into the water. When the hippo disappeared, Tortoise took the rope twice around a strong palm tree and hid nearby. After pulling until tired, Hippopotamus surfaced puffing water from his nostrils. Tortoise unwound the rope from the palm tree and showed him the rope still around Hippopotamus’s hind leg. Hippopotamus paid the promised rods. The elephant and the hippo agreed to take Tortoise as their friend, thinking he was very strong. The story explains that he had won through cunning, not strength. Tortoise wanted to live with both, but could not be in two places at once, so he left his son with Elephant on land and lived with Hippopotamus in the water. This explains why tortoises live on land and in water: water tortoises are much bigger because there are plenty of fish, while land tortoises are often short of food."},
{id:"the-story-of-the-fairy-bee",title:"The Story of the Fairy Bee",region:"Africa",country:"Uganda (Baganda); Baskerville, The King of the Snakes and Other Folk-Lore Stories (1922)",theme:"Friendship",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A helpful bee guides Kintu through a wizard’s tests and helps him recover his stolen cow.",moral:"A loyal friend can help you through a difficult journey.",leftPage:"Kintu became friends with a bumble-bee. Later, when his cow was stolen, he travelled to a distant country to look for it. A wizard set him difficult tests, including dealing with an enormous supper, gathering firewood from rock split by lightning, and collecting dew.",rightPage:"The bee helped Kintu through the tests and showed him how to recognize his cow among a vast herd. Kintu took the cow home. He and his small friend returned safely from the wizard’s country."},
{id:"the-story-of-the-hippos",title:"The Story of the Hippos",region:"Africa",country:"Uganda (Baganda); Baskerville, The King of the Snakes and Other Folk-Lore Stories (1922)",theme:"Helping Yourself",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"Hippos form a living bridge to help a stranded messenger cross a flooded river.",moral:"Working together can help someone reach safety.",leftPage:"A king protected the animals and sent a messenger to carry an order to Busoga. A storm washed away the canoes at the crossing, leaving the messenger stranded. An old hippo heard of the trouble and gathered the other hippos.",rightPage:"Together, the hippos formed a floating bridge, and the messenger crossed safely. The story ends by remembering how the king’s protection of hippos was repaid with their help."},
{id:"the-hare-and-the-lion-zanzibar",title:"The Hare and the Lion",region:"Africa",country:"Tanzania (Zanzibar); Bateman, Zanzibar Tales (1901)",theme:"Cleverness",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"Hare uses quick thinking to keep the honey from Lion and escapes unharmed.",moral:"A quick mind can help a smaller creature avoid danger.",leftPage:"While looking for food, Hare found bees living in a hollow calabash tree and discovered how to get honey. Lion wanted the honey too. Hare did not try to fight; instead, he used a series of quick evasions to stay ahead of Lion.",rightPage:"Lion could not catch Hare and finally admitted that Hare had beaten him. Lion went home, and Hare escaped unharmed."},
{"id":"the-lion-the-hyena-and-the-rabbit-zanzibar","title":"The Lion, the Hyena, and the Rabbit","region":"Africa","country":"Tanzania (Zanzibar); Bateman, Zanzibar Tales (1901), ch. III; adapted for young readers","theme":"Fairness","readingTime":"3 min","summary":"Caregiver note: Bateman’s original includes Rabbit and Lion eating Hyena; Rabbit later traps and eats Lion, becoming the sole owner of the farm and crops. This gentle adaptation replaces the eating threat with a lost-share consequence and changes the cave ending so Rabbit helps Lion out and the three share the harvest.","moral":"Make fair agreements and do not use cleverness to cheat your partners.","leftPage":"Lion, Hyena, and Rabbit shared a farm. On a journey to inspect their crops, they agreed that anyone who stopped alone would forfeit a share in this gentle retelling. Rabbit paused twice with riddles. Each time, all three paused together: Lion and Hyena stopped to ponder the answer, and then they continued together.","rightPage":"Later, Hyena stopped alone while Lion and Rabbit kept walking and said he was thinking of nothing. Under the gentler bargain, he would lose a share. Rabbit then showed Lion how to pass through a cave and challenged him to try. In Bateman’s original, Lion gets trapped and eaten; here Rabbit helps him out. The three return to the farm, make a kinder agreement, and share the harvest fairly."},
{id:"the-blacksmith-and-the-blackbirds",title:"The Blacksmith and the Blackbirds",region:"Africa",country:"Angola (Kimbundu); Chatelain, Folk-tales of Angola (1894)",theme:"Honesty",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A turtle-dove helps a blacksmith settle a debt the blackbirds denied.",moral:"Keep your promises and speak honestly when a debt is due.",leftPage:"A blacksmith gave his hoes to some blackbirds in exchange for beeswax. When he returned for the beeswax, the birds denied that they owed him anything. Their dispute went before the bird chief, but it did not move forward until a turtle-dove offered a plan.",rightPage:"The turtle-dove told the blacksmith to bind the birds, and when he did, they confessed and paid what they owed. The tale says the dove’s cooing is remembered as her judgment in the case."},
{"id":"man-and-turtle-angola","title":"Man and Turtle","region":"Africa","country":"Angola (Kimbundu); Adapted from Chatelain, Folk-tales of Angola (1894)","theme":"Cleverness","readingTime":"2 min","summary":"Turtle escapes from captors by turning their plan against them. Caregiver note: the captors threaten Turtle with an axe, stones, fire, and a knife before throwing him into the water.","moral":"A clever trick can turn danger into a way out.","leftPage":"People caught a turtle and discussed killing it with an axe, stones, fire, or a knife. Turtle answered each threat with a boast. At last, they decided to throw him into deep water, and Turtle pretended that this was the fate he feared most.","rightPage":"They threw Turtle into the water. He swam away, singing that the water was his home. The tale explains that turtles live in water."},
{id:"elephant-and-frog-angola",title:"Elephant and Frog",region:"Africa",country:"Angola (Kimbundu); Chatelain, Folk-tales of Angola (1894)",theme:"Cleverness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Frog boasts that Elephant is his horse, then rides on Elephant to prove the joke.",moral:"A boast can become a joke when others see what is really happening.",leftPage:"Frog told Elephant’s companions that Elephant was his horse, then denied saying it when Elephant challenged him. Frog climbed onto Elephant and asked for cords and a twig along the way, while Elephant carried him toward the gathering.",rightPage:"When they arrived, the girls greeted Elephant as Frog’s horse. Frog’s boast had followed them all the way there, and the story ends with the comic reversal."},
{id:"jackal-and-hare-angola",title:"Jackal and Hare",region:"Africa",country:"Angola (Kimbundu); Chatelain, Folk-tales of Angola (1894)",theme:"Cleverness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Hare’s hiding place frightens a boastful Jackal during their game of hide-and-seek.",moral:"Boasting can make an ordinary surprise seem frightening.",leftPage:"Jackal and Hare played hide-and-seek in the bush. Jackal hid several times, then boasted that Hare would never find him. Hare chose a hole for his hiding place and opened his eyes wide inside it.",rightPage:"Jackal saw the wide eyes, mistook them for an omen, and ran away crying. Hare laughed and reminded him that Jackal had boasted he could find him."},
{id:"squirrel-and-the-kingship",title:"Squirrel and the Kingship",region:"Africa",country:"Angola (Kimbundu); Chatelain, Folk-tales of Angola (1894)",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Squirrel loses a chance to become king because he cannot wait for the ceremony.",moral:"Patience matters when people are preparing something important.",leftPage:"The people planned to make Squirrel their king. The royal clothing and other signs of office were not ready yet, but Squirrel insisted that the ceremony must happen that very day. The people heard how impatient he was.",rightPage:"They decided that someone who could not wait for the preparations might not govern well. The people chose not to make Squirrel king, and the tale ends by saying that his impatience cost him the kingship."},
{id:"dog-and-the-kingship",title:"Dog and the Kingship",region:"Africa",country:"Angola (Kimbundu); Chatelain, Folk-tales of Angola (1894)",theme:"Carefulness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Dog runs away with a chicken breast during his royal installation and loses the kingship.",moral:"A moment of selfishness can cost you the trust of a group.",leftPage:"The people prepared Dog’s royal installation with a chair, musicians, special clothing, and a feast. As everyone gathered, Dog spotted a chicken breast. He grabbed it and ran into the bush rather than staying for the ceremony.",rightPage:"The people dispersed, and Dog did not become king. The story says that taking the food and running cost him the kingship."},
{id:"dog-and-jackal-angola",title:"Dog and Jackal",region:"Africa",country:"Angola (Kimbundu); Chatelain, Folk-tales of Angola (1894)",theme:"What We Need",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Dog stays in the village after finding that people there offer him a satisfying meal.",moral:"People choose where to stay when they find care and enough to eat.",leftPage:"Jackal sent Dog to the village to fetch fire so they could roast locusts. In the village, Dog met people who gave him a meal of mush. He found the food and welcome appealing and decided that the village was a better place for him.",rightPage:"Dog stayed in the village instead of returning to Jackal. The story ends by explaining Jackal’s howl as a lament that Dog had been lured away by the meal."},
{id:"the-story-of-the-frog-uganda",title:"The Story of the Frog",region:"Africa",country:"Uganda (Baganda); Baskerville, The Flame Tree and Other Folk-Lore Stories (1900)",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A young woman chooses the suitor who treated her frog friend with care.",moral:"Kindness toward a small creature can show a person’s character.",leftPage:"A young woman rescued a frog from the hot sun, and the two became friends. Later, her guardians arranged for four men to seek her hand in marriage. Three drove the frog away, but the fourth stepped carefully over it and treated it kindly.",rightPage:"The young woman chose the man who had treated her friend gently. The source ends by saying that they lived happily ever after."},
{"id":"hare-earned-cow-and-chieftainship","title":"The Hare Who Earned a Cow and a Chieftainship","region":"Africa","country":"Uganda; Adapted from Baskerville, The Flame Tree and Other Folk-Lore Stories (1925)","theme":"Leadership","readingTime":"4 min","summary":"A clever young hare earns a cow for the old Chief’s feast. Caregiver note: In Baskerville’s source, the Chief wants a cow killed for the feast, but the story does not narrate a slaughter. It also includes the hare’s threat to kill Hyena if he tastes the meat; the retelling keeps the no-tasting instruction but omits the threatened punishment and presents the skinning attempt non-graphically.","moral":"Good leadership is earned through contribution, not only clever talk.","leftPage":"The old Chief wanted a cow for a feast, so the hares built a round hut and a high fence. The hare brought a hollow log full of bumblebees into the hut they had built, then went to the herdsmen. He told them stories and sang for a week, and they gave him a cow.","rightPage":"The hare returned with the cow, drove it into the hut’s fenced yard, and beat the log. The bees buzzed and frightened the animals waiting outside. He gave each animal a task to earn meat: Lion tried to fetch water in a basket; the water poured through it, and he ran away. The buffalo’s axe handle broke against a rock, so she dropped it and fled. The leopard searched for jointless bamboo, wandered into a new province, and settled there. The hare warned Hyena he could skin the cow but must not taste the King’s meat. Hyena began skinning the cow, licked his paws, and could not resist the meat. The hare raised the alarm, “The hyena is eating the King’s meat!” and beat the hollow log; the bees buzzed, and Hyena dropped his knife and fled. The hares held the Chief’s feast and chose the clever hare as their new chief."},
{id:"the-man-who-knew-too-much",title:"The Man Who Knew Too Much",region:"Africa",country:"Uganda (Baganda); Baskerville, The Flame Tree and Other Folk-Lore Stories (1900)",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A gardener learns humility after a falling plum injures his eye and he recovers.",moral:"Listen to others instead of assuming you know better than everyone.",leftPage:"A proud gardener said he could have improved the planting choices of Kintu, the first man. As the gardener spoke, a plum fell from a tree and hurt his eye. He had to stay in the dark while it healed, and the injury troubled him for several days.",rightPage:"The gardener recovered and understood that his certainty had been misplaced. He asked his friends to stop flattering him. The story ends with him recognizing the value of humility."},
{id:"the-caterpillar-and-the-wild-animals",title:"The Caterpillar and the Wild Animals",region:"Africa",country:"Kenya (Maasai); Hollis, The Masai: Their Language and Folklore (1905)",theme:"Honesty",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A caterpillar’s enormous boasts frighten the animals until Frog challenges his story.",moral:"Boasts can create trouble when they make others afraid.",leftPage:"A caterpillar occupied Hare’s house and boasted that he could crush a rhinoceros and turn an elephant into dung. Jackal, Leopard, Rhinoceros, and Elephant heard the claims and retreated. Then Frog answered with an equally intimidating boast.",rightPage:"The caterpillar admitted that he was only a caterpillar. The animals seized him and dragged him outside, laughing at the trouble he had caused. The source describes no injury."},
{id:"how-the-turtle-outwitted-the-pig",title:"How the Turtle Outwitted the Pig",region:"Africa",country:"Cameroon; Bender, African Jungle Tales (1919)",theme:"Cleverness",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"Turtle uses a whetstone disguise to avoid paying Pig for a stolen necklace.",moral:"Tricking someone may win a moment, but it does not make things fair.",leftPage:"Mrs. Turtle borrowed Mrs. Pig’s necklace for a dance, but the necklace was stolen. When Mr. Pig came to demand repayment, Turtle disguised himself as a whetstone and let Pig carry him away. Then Turtle said Pig had stolen his whetstone.",rightPage:"Turtle refused to pay until Pig returned the whetstone. Pig could not find it, because it was Turtle in disguise. The story ends by explaining that pigs still grub in the earth for the missing whetstone."},
{id:"cunning-rabbit-and-his-well",title:"Cunning Rabbit and His Well",region:"Africa",country:"Sierra Leone; Cronise & Ward, Cunnie Rabbit, Mr. Spider, and the Other Beef (1903)",theme:"Sharing",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"Rabbit wins a contest for his well and shares its water with thirsty animals.",moral:"A resourceful person can help a whole community when resources are scarce.",leftPage:"During a drought, the animals could find no water, so Rabbit dug a well. Spider challenged Rabbit to a contest of strength. Rabbit chased Spider up to the sky, blew a horn that turned day dark and dark to day, and kept Spider there through a rainy season and a dry season. Spider finally admitted he could not continue. Elephant challenged Rabbit next, but Rabbit outlasted him too; they struggled until fire burned their fighting place to sand.",rightPage:"The animals offered clothes in exchange for water, and Rabbit let each drink from a great cup. They cooked a huge meal together. Floodwater and ashes spread over the animals as they swam home; the tale says animals had once been white, and that some became red, brown, black, or spotted."},
{id:"the-lion-the-hyena-and-the-fox",title:"The Lion, the Hyena, and the Fox",region:"Africa",country:"Ethiopia (Tigre); Tales, Customs, Names and Dirges of the Tigre Tribes (1915)",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"Fox exposes Lion’s false claim to Hyena’s calf and helps return the calf to its mother.",moral:"A clever truth can help challenge an unfair claim.",leftPage:"Lion claimed Hyena’s calf, pretending that his bull had fathered it. Hyena was afraid to challenge the powerful lion. Fox heard about the claim and answered with an equally absurd one: Fox’s father had given birth to Fox.",rightPage:"Lion tried to catch Fox, but Fox escaped. Fox then helped Hyena get her calf back. The story ends with the saying, “Give the hyena her calf,” said the fox."},
{id:"the-elephant-and-the-rabbit-zanzibar",title:"The Elephant and the Rabbit",region:"Africa",country:"Zanzibar; Madan, Kiungani, or: Story and History from Central Africa (1887)",theme:"Cleverness",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"Rabbit twice sneaks into Elephant’s dance with borrowed-looking horns and escapes.",moral:"A trick may work once, but it is wise to stop before it causes trouble.",leftPage:"Elephant held a dance for animals with horns. Rabbit wanted to join, so he fixed wax horns to his head and slipped into the party. The guests were served beer, an adults’ drink in the source. When the wax horns melted, Hyena noticed Rabbit, but he slipped away.",rightPage:"Later, Rabbit returned disguised with buffalo horns and escaped again. The party ended happily, and Rabbit did not try a third time."},
{id:"the-little-iron-house",title:"The Little Iron House",region:"Africa",country:"Zambia (Ba-Tonga); Torrend, Specimens of Bantu Folk-Lore (1921)",theme:"Courage",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A rabbit girl turns away boastful suitors and welcomes Rabbit, who arrives last.",moral:"People may choose whom they welcome, even when others make grand boasts.",leftPage:"Master Rabbit built a little iron house for a rabbit girl and invited the animals. Large animals arrived one by one, each boasting as a suitor. The rabbit girl answered with a song declining them, and none of the visitors could persuade her to change her mind.",rightPage:"At last Rabbit arrived, and the girl welcomed him, saying she was pledged to him. Their companions did not approach the house, and the other animals dispersed. The source ends there, without describing a wedding."},
{id:"the-past-and-the-future",title:"The Past and the Future",region:"Africa",country:"Angola (Kimbundu); Chatelain, Folk-tales of Angola (1894)",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A judge explains why the future holds what travelers have yet to find.",moral:"What lies ahead is still open to discovery.",leftPage:"Two travelers stopped at a palm-wine tapper’s place and asked for a drink of palm wine, an adult beverage in the story. They gave their names as “Whence-we-come” and “Where-we-go.” The tapper objected to the second name, so the travelers took their disagreement to a judge.",rightPage:"The judge ruled that “Where-we-go” was right: nothing more can be obtained from where someone has already come, but what a person may find lies ahead. The source ends with the words, “Finished.”"},
{id:"morning-sunrise",title:"Morning Sunrise",region:"Africa",country:"Gold Coast; Barker & Sinclair, West African Folk-Tales (1917)",theme:"Honesty",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"Thinker is the only suitor who comes after a father falsely announces his daughter’s death.",moral:"People’s actions can show when they truly care.",leftPage:"A young woman’s father falsely announced that she had died, hoping to see which of her three suitors would come to mourn. The first two refused to come. Thinker came, believing the news and wanting to show his care. No one had actually died or been hurt.",rightPage:"The father named Thinker as the woman’s husband, and the two married and lived happily. In this version, the father decides the match after his test; the tale does not describe the woman choosing a husband herself."},
{id:"the-story-of-the-animals-and-the-fruit",title:"The Story of the Animals and the Fruit",region:"Africa",country:"Zambia (Lamba); Doke, Lamba Folk-Lore, Tale XXVIII (1927)",theme:"Perseverance",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"Tortoise remembers the fruit’s name and helps the hungry animals gather it during a famine.",moral:"Careful attention can help a group make use of what it has.",leftPage:"During a famine, the animals asked a tree’s chief for the name of its fruit. The fruit would fall only when someone spoke its name. Several animals went to learn it but forgot before they returned. Tortoise listened, remembered the name, and said it aloud.",rightPage:"The fruit fell, and the animals ate it together. When the fruit ran out, they went their separate ways. The story does not say that the famine ended."},
{id:"the-story-of-the-fairy-foxes",title:"The Story of the Fairy Foxes",region:"Africa",country:"Uganda (Baganda); Baskerville, The King of the Snakes and Other Folk-Lore Stories (1922)",theme:"Respect for nature",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A king returns zoo animals to their homes after learning how unhappy they are in captivity.",moral:"Wild animals belong in the places where they can live freely.",leftPage:"A king built a zoo and brought animals from forests, jungles, and swamps. The animals were unhappy in their cages. A dwarf explained that they missed their homes. The king began to love the surrounding forest and understood why the animals longed for their own places.",rightPage:"The king ordered every zoo animal returned to its forest, jungle, or swamp. The story ends with mysterious cries in Mukono Forest: they may come from bats, sloths, or fairy foxes with wings of night mist."},
{id:"the-story-of-the-two-friends-uganda",title:"The Story of the Two Friends",region:"Africa",country:"Uganda (Baganda); Baskerville, The King of the Snakes and Other Folk-Lore Stories (1922)",theme:"Friendship",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A hare’s kindness helps a withdrawn boy return home and choose to learn pottery.",moral:"A good friend listens and helps you find a hopeful next step.",leftPage:"A potter’s son wandered into the forest, wondering whether he was wise or foolish. He met a lion, antelope, leopard, and elephant, and thought about useful ideas, gratitude, friendship, and work. A hare listened kindly and gave him shelter for the night.",rightPage:"The hare encouraged the boy to learn his father’s craft. The boy returned home, asked his father to teach him pottery, and grew into a famous potter. He continued visiting the hare when he needed comfort."},
{id:"the-cheats-of-kijongo",title:"The Cheats of Kijongo",region:"Africa",country:"Uganda (Baganda); Baskerville, The Flame Tree and Other Folk-Lore Stories (1900)",theme:"Honesty",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Two cheats at a market discover that each has tricked the other.",moral:"People who try to cheat others may be cheated themselves.",leftPage:"At a market, one cheat offered rags as barkcloth. Another offered a parcel of food that was mostly ant wings. Each hoped to fool a buyer, but the two exchanged their parcels with one another.",rightPage:"When they opened the parcels, each discovered the other’s trick. The tale closes with a proverb about cheats meeting at Kijongo. No one was injured."},
{id:"the-lioness-and-the-antelope",title:"The Lioness and the Antelope",region:"Africa",country:"Tanzania (Mtaug’ata, Swahili coast); Steere, Swahili Tales (1870)",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"An antelope’s greeting makes a hungry lioness ashamed to attack.",moral:"A kind greeting can help turn a tense moment toward peace.",leftPage:"A lioness had a cub and, after seven hungry days, went to look for food. She crept toward an antelope that was feeding. The antelope saw her and greeted her as “cousin,” speaking as if they were family.",rightPage:"The lioness felt ashamed and did not seize the antelope. That is the end of this short tale: no attack occurs, and the cub is not harmed."},
{id:"charcoal-burner-and-fuller",title:"The Charcoal-Burner And The Fuller",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Good Judgment",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A fuller declines a house-sharing plan because the neighbors' different work would undo each other's efforts.",moral:"People who live or work differently may not be good partners for every task.",leftPage:"A charcoal-burner asked a fuller, who cleaned and whitened cloth, to live with him. They could share one home and save money, he said. The fuller considered the offer. Their work, however, would clash: charcoal dust could darken the cloth he worked hard to make white.",rightPage:"“If I live with you,” the fuller replied, “your charcoal will undo my work.” He politely declined. The two neighbors could remain friendly without sharing a house. The fable ends with the idea that people with similar ways often get along more easily."},
{id:"boy-hunting-locusts",title:"The Boy Hunting Locusts",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Caution",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A boy learns to recognize that a scorpion is not another locust to catch.",moral:"Pause and look carefully before reaching for something unfamiliar.",leftPage:"A boy had caught many locusts and was pleased with his collection. Then he spotted another small creature nearby and thought it was one more locust. He reached toward it, but it was a scorpion, not an insect he should handle.",rightPage:"The scorpion raised its tail and showed its sting. It warned that if the boy touched it, he could lose both the scorpion and the locusts he had gathered. The boy stopped before he was stung. He kept his catch and learned to look closely before reaching."},
{id:"fisherman-piping",title:"The Fisherman Piping",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Good Judgment",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A musician-fisherman discovers that playing his flute will not bring fish into his net.",moral:"Choose actions that fit the task you want to accomplish.",leftPage:"A fisherman who played the flute stood beside his net and played a lively tune. He hoped the fish would hear the music and leap into the net. He played for a long while, but the water stayed still and no fish came.",rightPage:"The fisherman put away his flute and cast his net into the water. When he hauled it in, fish were caught and leaped about in the net. He laughed and said that they would not dance while he played, but danced only after he stopped."},
{id:"pomegranate-apple-tree-bramble",title:"The Pomegranate, Apple-Tree, and Bramble",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Good Judgment",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Two trees argue over beauty until a bramble boasts that they should stop arguing in its presence.",moral:"Boasting does not make someone better than the people they criticize.",leftPage:"A Pomegranate and an Apple-Tree began arguing about which of them was more beautiful. Each praised its own fruit and appearance. Their disagreement grew louder, though neither could persuade the other to change its mind.",rightPage:"A Bramble growing nearby interrupted them. It boasted that they ought to stop their argument because it was there. The boast did not settle which tree was more beautiful; it only showed that the Bramble was proud of itself too."},
{id:"dog-in-the-manger",title:"The Dog in the Manger",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Sharing",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A dog keeps oxen from hay he cannot eat, even though they need it for food.",moral:"Do not keep others from using something that is of no use to you.",leftPage:"A dog settled down in a manger full of hay. When the oxen came to eat, the dog growled and snapped at them, keeping them away. The dog could not eat the hay himself, but he would not let the oxen have it either.",rightPage:"One ox told the dog that it was selfish to guard food he could not use while stopping others from eating it. The oxen could only look at the hay from a distance. The fable leaves the dog in the manger and points to the unfairness of keeping useful things from those who need them."},
{id:"oxen-and-axle-trees",title:"The Oxen and the Axle-Trees",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Fairness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Oxen pulling a heavy wagon wonder why its wheels complain when they bear the load.",moral:"Those carrying the greatest burden may be the least likely to complain.",leftPage:"A team of oxen pulled a heavily loaded wagon along a country lane. The oxen leaned into the work and kept moving. All the while, the wagon's axle-trees groaned and creaked loudly.",rightPage:"The oxen asked why the axle-trees complained so much when the oxen were doing the hard work of pulling the wagon. The fable offers no answer from the wheels. It asks listeners to notice that those who bear the greatest effort may speak less about it."},
{id:"boasting-traveler",title:"The Boasting Traveler",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Honesty",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A traveler boasts about a huge jump in Rhodes, and a bystander asks him to demonstrate it where they stand.",moral:"A claim is more convincing when you can show it rather than merely boast.",leftPage:"A traveler told a group that he had once made an astonishingly long jump in Rhodes. He said people there had seen him do it and could confirm his story. The listeners heard him out, but the traveler offered no proof where they were.",rightPage:"A bystander pointed out that, if the traveler really could make such a jump, he could simply show them right then. The traveler had no demonstration to offer. The fable turns his grand story into a challenge to prove what he claims."},
{id:"boy-and-filberts",title:"The Boy and the Filberts",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Carefulness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A boy frees his hand from a narrow pitcher by taking fewer filberts at once.",moral:"Taking a manageable amount can help you reach your goal.",leftPage:"A boy found a pitcher filled with filberts. He reached inside and grabbed as many as he could hold. But when he tried to pull his closed hand out through the narrow opening, it would not fit.",rightPage:"A bystander advised him to let some filberts go and take only half as many. The boy did so, and his hand came out of the pitcher easily. He could enjoy the nuts he had taken without getting stuck."},
{id:"belly-and-members",title:"The Belly and the Members",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Working Together",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"The body's members refuse to help the Belly, then discover that all parts depend on one another.",moral:"A community works best when its members support one another.",leftPage:"The hands, feet, mouth, and eyes complained that they worked while the Belly rested. They decided to stop helping it. The hands would bring no food, the mouth would take in nothing, and the other parts would do no work for the Belly.",rightPage:"Before long, the whole body grew weak. The hands, feet, mouth, and eyes understood that they depended on the Belly, just as the Belly depended on them. They regretted refusing to help and restored their work together."},
{id:"father-and-his-two-daughters",title:"The Father and His Two Daughters",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Family",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A father hears two daughters hope for opposite weather because their households depend on different work.",moral:"The same circumstances can help one person and trouble another.",leftPage:"A father visited one of his daughters, who was married to a gardener. She hoped for rain to help the garden. Later, he visited his other daughter, who was married to a tile-maker. She hoped the weather would stay dry and sunny so the tiles could be made.",rightPage:"The father saw that each daughter's wish made sense for the work of her household. But rain would not suit the tile-maker, and sunshine would not suit the gardener. He wondered how he could be glad for both when their hopes were opposite."},
{id:"old-woman-and-wine-jar",title:"The Old Woman and the Wine-Jar",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Contentment",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"An old woman enjoys the lingering scent of an empty jar that once held fine wine.",moral:"A memory can bring pleasure even after the thing itself is gone.",leftPage:"An old woman came upon an empty jar that had once held fine wine. There was no wine left to drink. Still, a sweet fragrance lingered inside the jar, and she paused to enjoy it.",rightPage:"She smelled the jar again and remembered how lovely the scent was. “The wine itself must have been delicious,” she said, “if its empty jar still smells so sweet.” The tale ends with her enjoying the fragrance that remained."},
{id:"rivers-and-the-sea",title:"The Rivers and the Sea",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Problem-solving",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"The rivers complain that the Sea makes their fresh water salty, and the Sea points out where the mixing happens.",moral:"Before blaming someone else, consider how your own actions contribute to a problem.",leftPage:"The Rivers complained to the Sea that its water was salty. They said that when their own sweet, drinkable water flowed into it, the Sea made it briny. They thought the Sea was to blame for changing their water.",rightPage:"The Sea replied that the rivers would not become salty if they stopped flowing into it. Its answer pointed out the part the rivers played in the change they disliked. The fable ends with the Sea's brief reply, leaving the Rivers to consider it."},
{id:"the-trees-and-the-axe",title:"The Trees and the Axe",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Adapted from Townsend, Three Hundred Æsop’s Fables (1867)",theme:"Caution",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A forest lends a young ash tree for an axe handle, then sees the axe fell its tallest trees.",moral:"A small concession can have lasting consequences.",leftPage:"A man came to a forest and asked the trees for wood to make a handle for his axe. The trees agreed and gave him a young ash tree. He fitted it to his axe and began cutting down the forest’s tallest trees.",rightPage:"The axe had felled the forest’s tallest trees. An old oak saw the damage and told a cedar that giving up the ash had been the first mistake. If the forest had protected the ash, it might have remained standing. The fable warns that one small concession can put many at risk."},
{id:"seaside-travelers",title:"The Seaside Travelers",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Good Judgment",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Travelers repeatedly revise their guess about a distant object before discovering it is a bundle of sticks.",moral:"Wait until you can see clearly before deciding what something is.",leftPage:"Travelers stood high on a seaside cliff and saw something far away on the water. They thought it was a large ship and waited to watch it reach the harbor. As it came nearer, they changed their guess: perhaps it was only a small boat.",rightPage:"When the object reached the beach, the travelers went to look. It was not a ship or a boat, but a large bundle of sticks. From far away, they had mistaken its size and shape."},
{id:"mercury-and-the-sculptor",title:"Mercury and the Sculptor",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Good Judgment",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Mercury asks a sculptor what his statue is worth and learns the sculptor would give it away with the others.",moral:"Pride in yourself does not make others value you more.",leftPage:"Mercury disguised himself as an ordinary man and visited a sculptor. He asked how much a statue of Jupiter cost, then asked the price of a statue of Juno. The sculptor named a price for each.",rightPage:"Mercury pointed to his own statue and suggested it ought to cost even more, since he was the messenger of the gods. The sculptor replied that if Mercury bought the other two, he would throw in Mercury's statue for free."},
{id:"oak-and-the-reeds",title:"The Oak and the Reeds",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Good Judgment",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"After a storm uproots a mighty oak, nearby reeds explain how bending helped them remain standing.",moral:"Adapting to difficulty can sometimes help us endure it.",leftPage:"A great wind swept across a stream. It uprooted a large Oak and threw it down among the Reeds. The Oak looked at the slender plants still growing there and wondered how they had survived while it, so strong and tall, had fallen.",rightPage:"The Reeds explained that they bent with the wind instead of standing stiffly against it. Their flexibility had let the gust pass over them. The Oak had tried to resist and was uprooted."},
{id:"the-lamp",title:"The Lamp",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Good Judgment",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"An oil lamp boasts about its brightness, then goes out in a puff of wind and is relit.",moral:"Do your best without boasting as if you were greater than everyone else.",leftPage:"A lamp filled with oil shone brightly and proudly claimed that it gave more light than the Sun. It seemed to forget that its own little flame depended on the oil inside it and could not compare with daylight.",rightPage:"A puff of wind blew the lamp out. Its owner relit it and told it to shine without boasting. The lamp could give useful light again, but its bright moment had not made it greater than the Sun."},
{id:"crow-and-the-pitcher",title:"The Crow and the Pitcher",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Problem-solving",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A thirsty crow raises water in a pitcher by dropping stones into it one at a time.",moral:"Patiently trying different ideas can help solve a difficult problem.",leftPage:"A thirsty Crow found a pitcher with a little water at the bottom, too low for its beak to reach. The Crow tried to drink, but could not. It did not give up. Nearby, it found small stones and began dropping them into the pitcher.",rightPage:"The Crow added stones one by one. As they filled the bottom of the pitcher, the water rose higher. At last it reached a level where the Crow could drink. The bird had solved its problem through careful effort and a simple idea."},
{id:"fox-and-the-grapes",title:"The Fox and the Grapes",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Honesty",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A fox cannot reach some grapes and pretends she did not want them anyway.",moral:"Be honest with yourself when you are disappointed.",leftPage:"A hungry Fox spotted ripe grapes hanging high on a trellis. She leaped for them, but could not reach. She tried again from another place, then made several more attempts. The grapes remained just beyond her paws.",rightPage:"Tired of trying, the Fox walked away. To make herself feel better, she said the grapes were probably sour and unripe. But she had wanted them when she first saw them; her excuse did not change that she could not reach them."},
{id:"peacock-and-juno",title:"The Peacock and Juno",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Self-Acceptance",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A peacock wishes he could sing like a nightingale, and Juno reminds him of his own gifts.",moral:"Everyone has different strengths; value your own without comparing them all.",leftPage:"A Peacock complained to Juno that people admired the Nightingale's song, while his own voice made listeners laugh. He wished he had been given a beautiful voice too. Juno listened to his disappointment.",rightPage:"Juno comforted the Peacock by pointing to his splendid appearance. Different creatures, she explained, have different gifts. The Peacock could not sing like the Nightingale, but his own beauty was admired."},
{id:"lark-and-her-young-ones",title:"The Lark and Her Young Ones",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Good Judgment",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A lark moves her nestlings only when the farmer says he will harvest the wheat himself.",moral:"Pay attention to what people actually plan to do, not just what they hope others will do.",leftPage:"A Lark had made her nest in a wheat field with her young ones. The farmer came and asked his neighbors to help reap the wheat. The young birds heard him and worried, but their mother told them to stay: the neighbors might not come.",rightPage:"Later the farmer returned and said he would hire workers to reap the wheat himself. The Lark knew he now meant to act, so she moved her young ones to a safer place before the harvest began."},
{id:"town-mouse-and-country-mouse",title:"The Town Mouse and the Country Mouse",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Contentment",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A country mouse chooses her simple, safe home over rich food that comes with frightening interruptions.",moral:"A peaceful, modest life may be better than a feast that leaves you afraid.",leftPage:"A Country Mouse welcomed her Town Mouse cousin with plain food. The visitor urged her to come to a house where there were many fine things to eat. The Country Mouse went along, and the two found a table full of delicacies.",rightPage:"Whenever someone entered, the mice had to run and hide. After another interruption, the Country Mouse decided she preferred the simple food and safety of home. She returned to her quiet life rather than stay in a place where every meal brought fear."},
{id:"man-and-the-satyr",title:"The Man and the Satyr",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Trust and honesty",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A satyr ends a friendship after seeing a man blow on his hands to warm them and on food to cool it.",moral:"Be clear and consistent so others can understand and trust your actions.",leftPage:"A Man and a Satyr became companions while sharing a drink. On a cold day, the Man blew on his fingers to warm them. Later, when food was hot, he blew on it again, this time to cool it.",rightPage:"The Satyr noticed that the Man used the same breath for opposite purposes. He decided he could not trust such inconsistent behavior and ended their friendship. The two went their separate ways."},
{id:"the-two-bags",title:"The Two Bags",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Good Judgment",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A fable imagines people carrying their neighbors' faults in front and their own faults behind.",moral:"It is easy to notice others' mistakes and harder to see our own.",leftPage:"An old saying tells that every person is born carrying two bags. One hangs in front and holds other people's faults. Another hangs behind and holds that person's own faults.",rightPage:"Because the front bag is easy to see, people quickly notice what others do wrong. The bag behind is harder to look at, so they may overlook their own mistakes. The fable uses the two bags to invite self-reflection."},
{id:"travelers-and-the-plane-tree",title:"The Travelers and the Plane-Tree",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Gratitude",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Two travelers rest in a plane-tree's shade while one calls the tree useless for bearing no fruit.",moral:"Notice and appreciate helpful things even when they do not offer everything you want.",leftPage:"On a hot day, two tired travelers stopped to rest beneath a broad Plane-Tree. Its shade covered them as they sat and caught their breath. One traveler looked up at the branches and complained that the tree bore no fruit.",rightPage:"The Plane-Tree answered that the travelers were resting in its shade even as they called it useless. It was giving them comfort, though it offered no fruit. The travelers were reminded to value the help already around them."},
{id:"gnat-and-the-bull",title:"The Gnat and the Bull",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Good Judgment",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A gnat asks a bull whether it has been a bother, and the bull says it barely noticed.",moral:"Do not assume that everyone notices or depends on you as much as you imagine.",leftPage:"A Gnat settled on a Bull's horn and stayed there for a long time. At last, the Gnat prepared to fly away. Before leaving, it wanted to know whether the Bull had minded its presence.",rightPage:"“If you want me to go, I will,” said the Gnat. The Bull replied that he had hardly noticed the Gnat and would not miss it. The tiny visitor's importance to itself was much greater than it had been to the Bull."},
{id:"crab-and-its-mother",title:"The Crab and Its Mother",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Good Judgment",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A mother crab asks her child to walk straight, then cannot demonstrate how to do it herself.",moral:"Show others how to do what you ask of them.",leftPage:"A Mother Crab told her child that walking sideways was not a good way to move. She asked the young Crab to walk straight forward instead. The child listened, then asked its mother to show how.",rightPage:"The Mother Crab tried, but she could not walk straight forward either. Her child pointed out that it was difficult to follow instructions the mother could not demonstrate. The mother accepted the child's reproof."},
{id:"two-pots",title:"The Two Pots",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Carefulness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"An earthen pot asks a brass pot to keep its distance while they are carried together by a river.",moral:"Protect what is fragile by setting clear boundaries before trouble begins.",leftPage:"A river carried an earthen pot and a brass pot downstream together. The brass pot was strong, but the earthen pot was fragile. The smaller pot worried that if the two bumped, it might break.",rightPage:"The earthen pot asked its companion to keep a little distance. Even a slight touch, it explained, could be enough to break it. It did not want to come near the brass pot. The story ends with the request before any collision occurs."},
{id:"traveler-and-his-dog",title:"The Traveler and His Dog",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Honesty",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A traveler scolds his dog for delaying the trip, but the dog says it has been ready and waiting.",moral:"Before blaming someone, check whether the problem may be your own delay.",leftPage:"A traveler was ready to set out and saw his Dog stretching by the door. He sharply told the Dog to hurry, saying everything was prepared except for him. The Dog looked up and wagged its tail.",rightPage:"“I am ready,” the Dog replied. “I have been waiting for you.” The traveler had mistaken the one who was waiting for the one causing the delay. The little joke turns his complaint back on himself."},
{id:"kingdom-of-the-lion",title:"The Kingdom of the Lion",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Fairness",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A gentle lion king declares peace among the animals, but a hare still feels nervous beside stronger creatures.",moral:"A promise of peace matters most when everyone feels safe enough to trust it.",leftPage:"A Lion, described as a just and gentle king, called the animals together. He announced that they should live in peace, even those who were usually enemies. The animals gathered to hear his promise.",rightPage:"A Hare said how glad he was that weak animals could now live safely beside the strong. Even so, he ran away, still afraid. The meeting ended with the Lion's peace pledge, while the Hare's fear showed that trust does not always arrive at once."},
{"id":"the-fox-and-the-mask","title":"The Fox and the Mask","region":"Ancient Greece","country":"Greece; Adapted from Townsend, Three Hundred Æsop’s Fables (1867)","theme":"Wisdom","readingTime":"2 min","summary":"A fox finds an actor’s mask and notices that a fine appearance cannot give it brains.","moral":"Thoughtful character matters more than a beautiful appearance.","leftPage":"A fox wandered into an actor’s house, where costumes and stage props were kept. While rummaging through them, he found a mask shaped like a human head and placed his paws on it. Its face looked beautiful, and the fox admired it.","rightPage":"“What a beautiful head,” said the fox, “yet it is of no value, for it has no brains.” The mask looked impressive, but it could not think. This little fable invites us to look beyond appearances and value what a person can understand and do."},
{id:"peasant-and-the-eagle",title:"The Peasant and the Eagle",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A peasant frees an eagle from a trap, and the eagle later draws him away from a collapsing wall.",moral:"Kindness may be returned in an unexpected way.",leftPage:"A Peasant found an Eagle caught in a trap and freed it. Later, the Peasant sat beneath a wall that was not safe. The Eagle saw him there and swooped down, snatching a bundle from his head.",rightPage:"The Peasant moved after the Eagle and was drawn away from the wall. It collapsed nearby, but he was unharmed. He understood that the Eagle had repaid the kindness he had shown when it was trapped."},
{id:"crow-and-the-raven",title:"The Crow and the Raven",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Honesty",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A crow imitates a raven's ominous reputation, but travelers recognize its ordinary call.",moral:"Pretending to be something you are not may not convince anyone.",leftPage:"A Crow envied the Raven, whose call people treated as a sign or omen. When travelers approached, the Crow perched in a tree and cawed as loudly as it could, hoping they would think it had the Raven's importance.",rightPage:"One traveler told the other that it was only a Crow's cry. They continued on their way, and the Crow's attempt to imitate an omen had failed."},
{id:"fox-and-the-woodcutter",title:"The Fox and the Woodcutter",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Honesty",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A woodcutter hides a fox from pursuing hounds but points toward its hiding place when asked.",moral:"Actions reveal whether your help matches your words.",leftPage:"A Fox was fleeing hounds and asked a Woodcutter for a safe place to hide. The Woodcutter let the Fox into his hut. When the huntsman arrived and asked whether he had seen it, the Woodcutter said no.",rightPage:"While speaking, however, the Woodcutter pointed toward the hut where the Fox was hiding. The huntsman missed the hint and went on. The Fox left safely, but did not thank the Woodcutter: his words had denied help while his gesture gave the hiding place away."},
{id:"traveler-and-fortune",title:"The Traveler and Fortune",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Carefulness",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Fortune wakes a weary traveler who has lain down beside a deep well before he can fall into danger.",moral:"Take care around danger instead of blaming others for a careless choice.",leftPage:"A tired Traveler came to a deep well and lay down at its very edge. He was so weary that he was about to fall asleep there, close to danger. Dame Fortune appeared and woke him before he could tumble in.",rightPage:"Fortune warned the Traveler not to blame her for troubles that might come from his own foolish choices. The Traveler had put himself in danger by resting at the well's brink. He was awake and safe before he fell."},
{id:"wolf-and-the-housedog",title:"The Wolf and the Housedog",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Courage",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A wolf learns that a well-fed mastiff wears a collar and chooses freedom over meals with a chain.",moral:"Comfort may not be worth giving up the freedom that matters to you.",leftPage:"A Wolf met a well-fed Mastiff wearing a wooden collar. The Wolf asked who gave him his food and why he dragged the collar. The Mastiff answered that his master fed him and kept him tied.",rightPage:"The Wolf thought about the collar and chain. Though the Mastiff had plenty to eat, the Wolf did not want to live that way. He said he would rather remain free than have a heavy chain spoil his appetite. Then the Wolf went on his way."},
{id:"hen-and-the-swallow",title:"The Hen and the Swallow",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Caution",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A swallow warns a hen that the eggs she is warming will hatch into vipers that may harm her.",moral:"Consider what a choice may lead to before you nurture it.",leftPage:"A Hen found viper eggs and carefully warmed them until they hatched. A Swallow saw what she was doing and warned her about the young vipers. They might grow dangerous and harm others, beginning with the Hen herself.",rightPage:"The Swallow's warning came before any bite or attack. The story ends with the Hen being cautioned about the vipers she had helped hatch."},
{id:"ass-and-his-purchaser",title:"The Ass and His Purchaser",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Good Judgment",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A buyer tests an ass by seeing which companions he chooses, then returns him when he joins the laziest.",moral:"The company someone chooses can tell you something about their habits.",leftPage:"A man thinking of buying an Ass took him home to see how he would behave with the other asses. The new arrival looked around and quickly joined the laziest one, who was also the biggest eater.",rightPage:"The buyer took the Ass back. He said he already knew what kind of companion the Ass had chosen, and that was enough for him to understand the Ass's character."},
{id:"the-fishermen",title:"The Fishermen",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Perseverance",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Fishermen rejoice at a heavy net, then find mostly sand and stones and learn not to expect too much too soon.",moral:"Do not let hopes outrun what you know; outcomes can differ from expectations.",leftPage:"Some Fishermen felt their net growing heavy in the water. They were sure it must hold a great catch, and they danced with excitement as they hauled it in. An old fisherman shared their hope but waited to see what was there.",rightPage:"When the net came up, it held mostly sand and stones and only a few fish. The fishermen's joy faded. The old fisherman reminded them that sorrow often follows joy when people become certain of an outcome before they know what happened."},
{id:"mice-in-council",title:"The Mice in Council",region:"Ancient Greece",country:"Greece; Townsend translation, 1867 edition",theme:"Problem-solving",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"Mice agree that a bell could warn them when the cat approaches, but no one will attach it.",moral:"A plan is not useful unless someone can safely carry it out.",leftPage:"The Mice held a council to find a way to know when their enemy, the Cat, was coming. Someone suggested tying a bell around the Cat's neck. Then the mice would hear it ring and have time to hide.",rightPage:"The mice liked the idea and praised the plan. But when they asked who would tie the bell on the Cat, nobody volunteered. The plan remained only an idea, because the mice had not worked out how to carry it out."},
{id:"how-the-moon-became-beautiful",title:"How the Moon became Beautiful",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Davis & Chow-Leung, Chinese Fables and Folk Stories (1908)",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"The Moon's loneliness changes when Tseh-N’io's kindness inspires him to shine gently on the world.",moral:"Kindness can help others see their own beauty.",leftPage:"The Moon wondered why no one admired him. The stars and flowers tried to comfort him, and the flowers introduced him to Tseh-N’io, a gentle maiden. She chose to leave her home and live with the Moon, though her mother searched for her.",rightPage:"Tseh-N’io's kindness changed the Moon. His face grew bright and beautiful like hers, and he began to send gentle light across the world. The old tale says her beauty and goodness helped the lonely Moon become lovely too."},
{id:"the-fox-and-the-tiger",title:"The Fox and the Tiger",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Wilhelm, The Chinese Fairy Book (1921), ch. XI",theme:"Cleverness",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A fox convinces a tiger that the animals fear him, though the tiger is the one behind the fox.",moral:"Look carefully before deciding why others are afraid.",leftPage:"A tiger was about to eat a fox when the fox declared, “I am the true king of the beasts.” He asked the tiger to walk behind him so they could see what the other animals would do. The tiger followed, and every animal they met fled.",rightPage:"The fox said the animals had run from him. The tiger believed the boast and hurried away, not understanding that the animals had seen him following the fox. The fox escaped without anyone being hurt."},
{id:"the-fox-and-the-raven",title:"The Fox and the Raven",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Wilhelm, The Chinese Fairy Book (1921), ch. XIII",theme:"Caution",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A fox uses extravagant praise to make a raven drop its food, then warns that flattery may hide a selfish purpose.",moral:"Be cautious when praise comes from someone who wants something.",leftPage:"A raven perched with a piece of meat in its beak. A fox praised the bird's wisdom, devotion, courage, and voice. The raven was pleased and opened its beak to answer.",rightPage:"The meat fell, and the fox ate it. Before leaving, the fox warned that praise can be used to get what the flatterer wants. The raven had lost its meal, but no one was harmed."},
{id:"the-spirit-of-the-wu-lian-mountain",title:"The Spirit of the Wu-Lian Mountain",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Wilhelm, The Chinese Fairy Book (1921), ch. XL",theme:"Courage",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A temple guardian rescues a scholar carried away by a mountain monster during a storm.",moral:"Help can arrive when a frightening situation seems beyond your control.",leftPage:"A scholar was studying late at night when a mountain monster seized him and carried him into a storm. A golden-armored guardian of a temple appeared. The monster dropped the scholar onto the temple tower.",rightPage:"A priest helped the scholar climb down safely. He was not injured, though his hair stayed stiff for half a year. The old tale ends with the scholar safe after a frightening night."},
{id:"the-little-hunting-dog",title:"The Little Hunting Dog",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Wilhelm, The Chinese Fairy Book (1921), ch. XLII",theme:"Friendship",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A scholar's room is cleared of fleas and gnats by tiny hunters, one of whose little dogs stays behind.",moral:"A small companion can bring great comfort.",leftPage:"A scholar was plagued by fleas and gnats. One day a procession of tiny knights, falcons, and hunting dogs swept through his room and cleared away the insects. When the little hunters departed, an ant-sized dog was left behind.",rightPage:"The scholar found that the tiny dog was tame and could hunt the remaining insects in his bed. He grew fond of it and kept it as a companion. In the story, the little hunter turns an irritating problem into a friendship."},
{id:"the-miserly-farmer",title:"The Miserly Farmer",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Wilhelm, The Chinese Fairy Book (1921), ch. XXXIII",theme:"Generosity",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A farmer refuses a pear to a poor monk, while an artisan's gift leads to a magical feast and a humbling trick.",moral:"Generosity matters more than guarding every possession.",leftPage:"A poor monk asked a farmer for one pear from his cart, but the farmer refused. An artisan bought a pear for the monk instead. The monk ate its seed, and a pear tree seemed to grow at once; he shared its fruit with the people who gathered.",rightPage:"When the monk left, the farmer discovered that his own pears and cart axle were gone. He understood that the tree had been a trick made from the axle, and the crowd laughed at his refusal to share. The tale warns against miserliness; it does not present taking the farmer's property as a good example."},
{id:"the-wind-the-clouds-and-the-snow",title:"The Wind, the Clouds, and the Snow",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Davis & Chow-Leung, Chinese Fables and Folk Stories (1908)",theme:"Gratitude",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"The Sun hears the Wind and Clouds remind Snow of the help that once brought it to the earth.",moral:"Remember the help you have received.",leftPage:"The Wind and the Clouds reminded the Snow that the cloud mother had once sent them down to help people when the earth needed them. They also reminded Snow that it had once been water in rivers and seas, lifted high by clouds. Snow admitted it had forgotten.",rightPage:"The Sun listened as judge and decided in favor of the Wind and Clouds. Snow had to acknowledge the help it had received. In this fable, the quarrel ends when the forgotten kindness is remembered."},
{id:"the-fish-and-the-flowers",title:"The Fish and the Flowers",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Davis & Chow-Leung, Chinese Fables and Folk Stories (1908)",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"Flowers answer complaining fish without quarrelling, and the morning brings a fragrant surprise.",moral:"You do not have to join a quarrel when someone complains.",leftPage:"During a cold winter, a merchant moved narcissus bulbs into a warmer fish house. The fish complained about the flowers' scent and demanded that they leave. The flowers did not quarrel with them.",rightPage:"By morning, the blossoms had made the whole house fragrant. The fish complained again, but the flowers quietly ignored them. The story ends with the flowers refusing to answer unkindness with another quarrel."},
{id:"the-hen-the-cat-and-the-birds",title:"The Hen, the Cat, and the Birds",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Davis & Chow-Leung, Chinese Fables and Folk Stories (1908)",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A young bird points out that the hen is asking the cat to take a risk she will not take herself.",moral:"Think for yourself before following someone else's risky suggestion.",leftPage:"A farmer's boy kept three young wood larks in a cage. Later he hung the cage in a tree so they could sing among the other birds. A hen urged a cat to attack them, even though she would not take the risk herself.",rightPage:"One bird reminded the cat of that and of her own kittens. The cat thought about it, agreed, and went away. The three birds remained unharmed, and the hen's suggestion was not followed."},
{id:"a-bridegroom-for-miss-mole",title:"A Bridegroom for Miss Mole",region:"East Asia",country:"Korea; Griffis, Korean Fairy Tales (1911/1922)",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A mole father searches for the mightiest possible bridegroom and learns that even a stone colossus has a weakness.",moral:"Strength depends on what a person can do, not only on size or reputation.",leftPage:"A proud mole father wanted the mightiest possible husband for his daughter. He asked the sky, the Sun, the clouds, the Wind, the mountain, and a stone colossus which was strongest. Each pointed to something stronger than itself.",rightPage:"At last the colossus said a mole could undermine its base. The old tale says Miss Mole married a handsome young mole and the couple lived happily. This is the story's old-fashioned arranged-marriage ending; the lesson is in the search for strength, not in treating marriage as a prize."},
{id:"the-mirror-that-made-trouble",title:"The Mirror That Made Trouble",region:"East Asia",country:"Korea; Griffis, Korean Fairy Tales (1911/1922)",theme:"Surprises",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A village mistakes reflections in a newly arrived mirror for visitors until everyone understands what they are seeing.",moral:"A little explanation can clear up a confusing first impression.",leftPage:"A mirror arrived in a Korean village where people had never seen one before. Family members looked into it and mistook the reflected faces for other people. Their guesses led to a series of comic misunderstandings in the household.",rightPage:"At last, the family discovered that the faces belonged to them. The villagers laughed at their confusion, and mirrors became popular there. This is the comic first-mirror story, not the different tale about a mother's keepsake."},
{id:"the-hang-the-money-up-tree",title:"The Hang-the-Money-Up Tree",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Nixon-Roulet, Japanese Folk Stories and Fairy Tales (1908)",theme:"Love and care",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A wife's offering beneath a pine tree reaches the ruler, who recalls her exiled husband.",moral:"Steadfast care can be seen even in a small offering.",leftPage:"A devoted wife lived apart from her husband after he was sent into exile. She prayed beneath a pine tree and hung coins on its branches as an offering. A farmer tried to steal them, but the tree suddenly changed and frightened him away.",rightPage:"The ruler heard of the wife's devotion and called her to his throne. She asked for her husband's return, and the ruler sent word recalling him from exile. The pine became a shrine known as the Hang-the-Money-Up Tree."},
{id:"the-goddess-of-green-growing-things",title:"The Goddess of Green-Growing Things",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Nixon-Roulet, Japanese Folk Stories and Fairy Tales (1908)",theme:"Community",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"Amaterasu shares useful seeds and plants, and a shrine to Mihashirano is built as a famine ends.",moral:"Care for the gifts that sustain a community.",leftPage:"The source begins plainly: Susanoo had slain Ukimochi in anger. Amaterasu found a mulberry tree, silkworm, rice, barley, and beans growing from Ukimochi's body. She gave the seeds and plants to the earth, where they helped people grow food and make silk.",rightPage:"Amaterasu sent Susanoo's daughter Mihashirano to earth, but she wandered without a home. A fisherman named Sakino met her and built a shrine for her at Miyajima. The story ends by saying the goddess lived there happily and the famine ended."},
{id:"the-boy-and-the-spirits-of-things",title:"The Boy and the Spirits of Things",region:"East Asia",country:"Ainu community; Nixon-Roulet, Japanese Folk Stories and Fairy Tales (1908)",theme:"Respect for nature",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"An ill boy senses that the spirit of his father's neglected axe is unhappy, and the axe is cared for.",moral:"Treat the tools and natural things around you with care.",leftPage:"In Nixon-Roulet's Ainu-labeled account, a boy said that the spirits of trees, wind, birds, and other things were his friends. When he became ill, he sensed that the spirit of his father's neglected axe was unhappy.",rightPage:"The father cleaned and renewed the axe, then made an offering. The boy recovered, and later became a respected augur. The tale presents care for the axe's spirit as part of the boy's recovery; it is one account, not a description of all Ainu traditions."},
{id:"the-laughing-dumpling",title:"The Laughing Dumpling",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Nixon-Roulet, Japanese Folk Stories and Fairy Tales (1908)",theme:"Cleverness",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"An old woman follows a runaway rice dumpling into an ogre's home, then escapes with a magic paddle.",moral:"A resourceful person can find a way home from trouble.",leftPage:"An old woman's rice dumpling rolled away and down a hole. She followed it and found herself in an ogre's home, where the ogre kept her to cook. The old woman waited for a chance to leave.",rightPage:"She escaped with a magic paddle and returned home safely. Back in Kyoto, she used the paddle to make rice cakes for the people. The story ends with the food shared beyond her own household."},
{id:"how-it-was-settled-who-should-rule-the-world",title:"How it was settled who should rule the World",region:"East Asia",country:"Ainu community; Chamberlain, Aino Folk-Tales (1888)",theme:"Cleverness",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A fox-god tricks the other gods into seeing the sunrise first, settling which group will rule.",moral:"A clever trick can settle a contest, but not always honestly.",leftPage:"In Chamberlain's Ainu-attributed tale, good and bad gods agreed that the ones who first saw sunrise would rule the world. The fox-god looked west, where the coming light shone on the horizon, and called to the others that sunrise had begun.",rightPage:"The gods turned to look. The source says the brilliant, or good, gods won the right to rule because the fox had made them see the sunrise first. The contest ended without anyone being hurt."},
{"id":"the-two-foxes-the-mole-and-the-crows","title":"The two Foxes, the Mole, and the Crows","region":"East Asia","country":"Ainu; Adapted from Chamberlain, Aino Folk-Tales (1888), tale XI","theme":"Honesty","readingTime":"4 min","summary":"The foxes make garments from leaves and food from tree gum. At the mole’s imitation village, the foxes eat mulberries and grapes while bird-people dance; later, the mole allows the foxes and crows to share fruit that the crows drop from trees.","moral":"It is better to live honestly and share than to keep up a deception.","leftPage":"Two foxes wanted to appear human. They made treasures and garments from leaves and food and cakes from tree gum. Nearby, the mole made a village of his own, with treasures and clothes. When the foxes visited, the bird-people danced, then flew away; the foxes ate mulberries and grapes from the trees.","rightPage":"When the foxes return for more food, the Mole reveals that his village and treasures are imitations. He tells them to stop pretending to be human; the foxes and their companions, the crows, may eat together the fruit the crows drop from the trees. The foxes stop imitating people, and the animals live together in friendship."},
{id:"the-kind-giver-and-the-grudging-giver",title:"The Kind Giver and the Grudging Giver",region:"East Asia",country:"Ainu community; Chamberlain, Aino Folk-Tales (1888)",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A fisherman who shares his food with a raven prospers, while a second man's greedy effort ends in comic failure.",moral:"Kindness matters more than trying to force a reward.",leftPage:"A fisherman shared a fish with a raven and was welcomed by the raven's divine family. They gave him puppies that could produce gold and silver. Another man gave a raven a muddy fish and later tried to get rich by overfeeding his own puppies.",rightPage:"The kind man prospered by feeding his puppies carefully. The greedy man's house filled with ordinary droppings instead of riches. The tale ends by urging kind-heartedness; it uses a comic mess to contrast the two men's choices."},
{id:"the-man-who-was-changed-into-a-fox",title:"The Man who was changed into a Fox",region:"East Asia",country:"Ainu community; Chamberlain, Aino Folk-Tales (1888)",theme:"Honesty",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A dishonest man is changed into a fox, then restored after a tree spirit warns him to change his conduct.",moral:"Change harmful behavior and make better choices.",leftPage:"A man lied to people and extorted things from them. A spirit changed him into a fox. Frightened that dogs might attack him if he returned home, he hid beside an oak tree and called for help.",rightPage:"The tree's divine spirit restored him and warned him to behave better. The man woke in human form, worshipped the tree, and returned home. The source says he did not misbehave again; no dog attacked him."},
{id:"the-old-man-of-the-sea",title:"The Old Man of the Sea",region:"East Asia",country:"Ainu community; Chamberlain, Aino Folk-Tales (1888)",theme:"Quick thinking",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A sailor distracts a sea monster drawing his boat toward its mouth, and the creature lets the boat go.",moral:"A quick, unexpected idea can help in a frightening moment.",leftPage:"A bag-shaped sea monster pulled a boat toward its mouth with a powerful current. One of the sailors tossed in a piece of clothing. The strange offering proved so unappealing to the monster that it stopped drawing the boat closer.",rightPage:"The monster let the boat go, and the two sailors were not hurt. The old Ainu-attributed tale ends with their escape and a comic clothing gag."},
{id:"the-horned-owl",title:"The [Horned] Owl",region:"East Asia",country:"Ainu community; Chamberlain, Aino Folk-Tales (1888)",theme:"Good Judgment",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"An Ainu-attributed belief says a person who passes beneath the youngest owl may receive good fortune.",moral:"Some traditional stories explain how people understood signs of good fortune.",leftPage:"The tale describes six owl brothers. One small owl balances backward, and the youngest is associated with good fortune. A person walking beneath that owl may hear something that sounds like rain falling.",rightPage:"The story explains that people thought the sound was gold falling from the owl's eyes, and that the person would become rich. It is told as a traditional belief about luck, not as a promise that anyone will find gold."},
{id:"the-peacock-in-the-sky",title:"The Peacock in the Sky",region:"East Asia",country:"Ainu community; Chamberlain, Aino Folk-Tales (1888)",theme:"Family",readingTime:"2 min",summary:"A peacock living in the sky comes to earth to have a young bird, then flies home with it.",moral:"A parent and young one can stay together through a change of home.",leftPage:"In this short Ainu-attributed tale, a peacock lives in the cloudless sky and has eagles as servants. The peacock comes down to earth only when it is time to have a young bird.",rightPage:"After the young bird is born, the peacock flies back to the sky with it. The story ends with parent and offspring together, without danger or harm."},
{id:"the-ancient-man",title:"The Ancient Man",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Wilhelm, The Chinese Fairy Book (1921), ch. XXIX",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"Huang An explains that the tortoise beside him has shown its head only five times in the centuries he has known it.",moral:"The stories people tell can make time feel much larger than a lifetime.",leftPage:"People asked Huang An, an ageless-looking man, about the very old tortoise he sat upon. He answered that in all the many centuries he had known the tortoise, it had shown its head only five times.",rightPage:"Huang An carried the tortoise away on his back. The collection says the story gave rise to the legend that he was ten thousand years old. No one is harmed, and the tale leaves the extraordinary ages as part of the legend."},
{id:"the-flower-elves",title:"The Flower-Elves",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Wilhelm, The Chinese Fairy Book (1921), ch. XXXIX",theme:"Helping Yourself",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A scholar helps flower spirits protect their garden from a storm, and they reward him with flowers that restore his youth.",moral:"A thoughtful act of help can protect what is fragile.",leftPage:"A scholar met flower spirits in his garden. They told him that a young pomegranate spirit had offended the wind spirits, and asked him to raise a protective flag. He did so before a powerful storm arrived.",rightPage:"The storm bent forests and broke trees, but the flowers remained safe. Grateful, the flower spirits gave the scholar flowers that restored his youth. In time, he joined the immortals, as the old story relates."},
{id:"the-dragon-after-his-winter-sleep",title:"The Dragon After His Winter Sleep",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Wilhelm, The Chinese Fairy Book (1921), ch. XLIII",theme:"Carefulness",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A scholar respectfully carries a tiny glowing creature outdoors, where it awakens as an enormous dragon and flies away.",moral:"Treat unfamiliar living things with care and respect.",leftPage:"A scholar saw a tiny glowing creature leave a scorched trail across his book. Suspecting it was a dragon, he carried the book outdoors and bowed to the little creature instead of disturbing it.",rightPage:"The creature stretched, grew enormous, and flew away amid thunder. The scholar went back indoors unharmed. The story ends with the dragon gone and only a scorched mark on the book."},
{id:"the-eagle-and-the-rice-birds",title:"The Eagle and the Rice Birds",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Davis & Chow-Leung, Chinese Fables and Folk Stories (1908)",theme:"Family",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"After a boy swaps eggs to test whether birds can change their nature, an eagle and her eaglets find their way back together.",moral:"Love and kinship can endure a period of separation.",leftPage:"A boy secretly swapped eggs between an eagle's nest and a rice bird's nest to claim he could change what kind of birds they would become. The eagle raised the rice birds for a time, but they later left to join their own kind. She searched for her eaglets and felt their absence.",rightPage:"At last the eagle found her own eaglets being cared for by another eagle. They recognized one another and returned home together. The boy lost his wager, and the story says each bird returned to its own kind."},
{id:"the-rattan-vine-and-the-rose-tree",title:"The Rattan Vine and the Rose Tree",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Davis & Chow-Leung, Chinese Fables and Folk Stories (1908)",theme:"Knowing Your Strengths",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A storm breaks the boastful rattan vine while the rose tree, standing on its own roots, remains upright.",moral:"Rely on your own strength instead of depending entirely on others.",leftPage:"A boastful rattan vine mocked a rose tree for growing slowly and relying on its own roots. The rose tree answered that it could stand by itself. Then a strong storm came, damaging houses and fences and breaking the vine in many places.",rightPage:"The vine's head lay on the ground, but the rose tree remained upright. It explained that it stood through its own strength, bending with the changing wind. The story ends with the rose tree standing and a lesson about self-reliance."},
{id:"how-an-old-man-lost-his-wen",title:"How an Old Man Lost His Wen",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Ozaki, Japanese Fairy Tales (1908), adapted from Sanjin",theme:"Cleverness",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A skilled dancer's wen is removed by the demons, but his neighbor's poor imitation leaves him with two.",moral:"A practiced skill can help you through a difficult moment.",leftPage:"A woodcutter had a large wen on his right cheek and had tried many ways to remove it. One rainy evening he sheltered in a hollow tree, where hundreds of demons gathered to feast, dance, and drink saké, an adult rice wine. Their chief asked if anyone could dance better. The old man feared the demons might kill him if they were displeased, but his love of dancing led him to step out and dance skillfully.",rightPage:"The demons praised him and offered him a cup of saké. They asked him to return the next day and took his wen as a pledge; it came away without pain, leaving his cheek smooth. His disagreeable neighbor, who had a wen on the other cheek, tried to copy him but danced badly. The chief threw the first man's wen onto the neighbor's other cheek, so he went home with two."},
{"id":"east-light-and-the-bridge-of-fishes","title":"East Light and the Bridge of Fishes","region":"East Asia","country":"Korea; Adapted from Griffis, Korean Fairy Tales (1922)","theme":"Courage","readingTime":"4 min","summary":"Caregiver note: the jealous king twice places the infant in danger—first in a pigpen, then among horses in the stable. Later, soldiers shoot arrows at East Light and his companions, who escape safely.","moral":"Kindness to animals and courage in danger can shape a leader.","leftPage":"Long ago, a young woman serving at a Korean king’s court gave birth to a baby boy after seeing a shining cloud. The jealous king sent the infant to a pigpen, then a stable, hoping the animals would harm him. The sows and mares warmed him and kept him alive. Seeing this, the king let the boy return to the palace, where he grew up kind to animals and skilled as an archer and rider.","rightPage":"When the king later grew jealous of East Light, the young man fled with three companions. A wide river blocked their way. East Light shot arrows into the water, and fish gathered until they formed a bridge. His friends crossed with him; the king’s soldiers fired arrows, but they fell short. East Light reached a new land, where its people welcomed him as their ruler. He led them for many years. The tale says the people later called the peninsula Cho-sen, glossing the name as “Morning Radiance” or “Land of the Morning Calm.”"},
{id:"the-sneezing-colossus",title:"The Sneezing Colossus",region:"East Asia",country:"Korea; Griffis, Korean Fairy Tales (1911/1922)",theme:"Surprises",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A woodcutter seeking a giant pear is sneezed from a stone colossus and finds the fruit waiting below.",moral:"An unexpected setback can lead to a good surprise.",leftPage:"A hungry woodcutter climbed a giant stone Buddha to reach an enormous pear. He crawled into the colossus's nostril to get close to the fruit. The colossus suddenly sneezed him out onto bushes below.",rightPage:"The woodcutter woke safely, found the fallen pear, and took it home. He shared it with his family at supper. The startling sneeze caused no injury, and the tale ends with a meal together."},
{id:"the-single-lantern-of-yamato",title:"The Single Lantern of Yamato",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Nixon-Roulet, Japanese Folk Stories and Fairy Tales (1908)",theme:"Generosity",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A poor woman's single lantern keeps shining after a rich donor's many festival lights go out.",moral:"A gift's worth comes from the heart behind it, not its size.",leftPage:"An impoverished elderly woman sold her hair so she could buy one small lantern for a temple. A rich donor gave many grand lanterns. During the festival, a strong wind extinguished the rich man's lights.",rightPage:"The old woman's little lantern continued to shine. The priest praised the humble gift she had offered with a good heart. The story contrasts the one small light with the many grand ones."},
{id:"the-travels-of-two-frogs",title:"The Travels of Two Frogs",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Griffis, Japanese Fairy World (1880)",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"Two frogs meet halfway between Kyoto and Osaka, then mistake their own views for the cities they hoped to see.",moral:"Check what you are looking at before drawing a conclusion.",leftPage:"A frog from Kyoto set out to see Osaka, while a frog from Osaka travelled toward Kyoto. They met halfway and climbed a hill to look toward their destinations. Each stood upright to see farther, but their eyes faced back toward the city they had left.",rightPage:"The frogs mistook their own cities for the ones ahead. They went home believing Kyoto and Osaka were alike, and told their families so. Their mistake came from looking in the wrong direction."},
{id:"benkei-and-the-bell",title:"Benkei and the Bell",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Griffis, Japanese Fairy World (1880)",theme:"Good Judgment",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"Benkei carries a temple bell away, but its ringing keeps asking to return to Miidera until he takes it back.",moral:"Respect what belongs to others and listen when something is asking to be returned.",leftPage:"The source describes an old rule that barred women from the Miidera monastery because monks held a prejudiced belief about women. A woman once entered to look in the polished bell as in a mirror; after she touched it, a hollow place appeared and its shine faded. Later, the monk Benkei secretly carried the bell away to Hiyeisan.",rightPage:"When the priests struck it, the bell moaned that it wanted to return to Miidera. Benkei grew angry, carried it back, and kicked it rolling down the valley. The monks hung it up again, and from then on it sounded like an ordinary bell."},
{id:"smells-and-jingles",title:"Smells and Jingles",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Griffis, Japanese Fairy World (1880)",theme:"Fairness",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A cook demands payment for the smell of his neighbor's eel, and the neighbor pays with the sound of jingling coins.",moral:"A fair answer can match a silly claim with a sensible joke.",leftPage:"A miserly merchant enjoyed the smell of his neighbor's eel dinners without buying any. The cook decided to send him a bill for the smells. The merchant listened to the demand and considered how to answer it.",rightPage:"He jingled his coins and said that the sound of money was payment for the smell of the eel. The two were declared even. The story ends with a joke about paying for one kind of pleasure with another."},
{id:"the-waterfall-of-yoro-or-the-fountain-of-youth",title:"The Waterfall of Yoro, or the Fountain of Youth",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Griffis, Japanese Fairy World (1880)",theme:"Family",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A woodcutter brings adult rice wine from a waterfall to his father, and the emperor names the era Yoro after hearing of his care.",moral:"Care for family members through thoughtful acts.",leftPage:"A woodcutter found that a mountain waterfall tasted like saké, a rice wine for adults. He brought the drink home for his elderly father, who enjoyed it for many years. The story presents this as the son's care for his father; saké is an adult beverage, not a drink for children.",rightPage:"The emperor heard of the son's care and visited the waterfall. The tale says he named the era Yoro, or “Nourishing Old Age,” as a reward for filial piety. It calls the place a fountain of youth, but says that the waterfall now runs ordinary water."},
{"id":"a-wonderful-medicine","title":"A Wonderful Medicine","region":"East Asia","country":"Korea; Adapted from Gale, Korean Folk Tales: Imps, Ghosts and Fairies (1913), ch. XLV","theme":"Courage","readingTime":"3 min","summary":"Prince Cheung prepares a medicine in prayer, and the supernatural disturbances in his home cease.","moral":"Steady courage can help a family face a frightening disturbance.","leftPage":"Goblins and devils troubled the household of the elderly Prince Cheung. They snatched and crumpled a visitor’s hat, threw stones, and caused confusion. When the disturbances continued, Prince Cheung moved to another house and prepared a special medicine while praying.","rightPage":"Afterward, the spirits departed. The account says neither the goblins nor signs of them returned in the years that followed. The tale ends with the household free of the frightening disruption."},
{id:"little-silvers-dream-of-the-shoji",title:"Little Silver’s Dream of the Shoji",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Griffis, Japanese Fairy World (1880)",theme:"Courage",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"After hearing ghost stories, Little Silver dreams of a ship striking a rock, then wakes safely and laughs at the dream.",moral:"A frightening dream can feel real, but waking brings a chance to see it clearly.",leftPage:"Miss Little Silver felt uneasy after hearing ghost stories. To ward off bad dreams, she put a picture of a tapir on her pillow. That night she dreamed of ghosts aboard a ship that struck a rock.",rightPage:"Little Silver woke safely. The next morning she laughed at the dream and at her nurse's belief about the tapir picture. The shipwreck happened only in the dream."},
{id:"the-good-thunder",title:"The Good Thunder",region:"East Asia",country:"Japan; Grace James, Japanese Fairy Tales (1910)",theme:"Family",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"Rai-Taro lives with an elderly farming couple, learns human life, and eventually returns to his father in the cloud castle.",moral:"Time spent caring for one another remains meaningful even when people must part.",leftPage:"Rai-Taro, son of the thunder god, watched human life and was sent down to experience it. An elderly farming couple took him in. He learned about work, suffering, and love; the tale mentions a distant battle but shows no combat or injury.",rightPage:"Rai-Taro returned to his father in the cloud castle. His foster mother wept at the parting, and her husband comforted her by saying it would not be forever. The story ends with their sadness acknowledged, not erased."},
{id:"the-lemon-tree-and-the-pumelo",title:"The Lemon Tree and the Pumelo",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Davis & Chow-Leung, Chinese Fables and Folk Stories (1908)",theme:"Friendship",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A Pumelo tree helps a Lemon tree's fruit become welcome, and the two share happiness through many generations.",moral:"Helping another can make both friends happier.",leftPage:"An old orchard held a Lemon tree and a Pumelo tree. The Lemon tree felt sad because people disliked its sour fruit, while the Pumelo's fruit was welcomed at feasts. The Pumelo comforted its friend and said the master admired the Lemon tree's beauty.",rightPage:"The next spring, the master and his son grafted branches of the Lemon tree onto the Pumelo. The new lemons were pleasant to taste, and children saved them for the coming feast. The trees shared happiness and friends for many generations."},
{id:"the-melon-and-the-professor",title:"The Melon and the Professor",region:"East Asia",country:"China; Davis & Chow-Leung, Chinese Fables and Folk Stories (1908)",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A fig bruises a proud professor's nose, helping him reconsider his claim that creation is poorly ordered.",moral:"Think carefully before deciding that you know better than everyone else.",leftPage:"Wu-Kiao, a proud and learned professor, saw a watermelon growing on a vine and figs on a tree. He said the melon should grow on the strong tree and the small figs on the thin vine. He called the arrangement a mistake in creation.",rightPage:"A fig fell on his nose and bruised it slightly. Wu-Kiao realized that if a watermelon-sized fruit fell from that height, it might kill him. He admitted he had been wrong and said he should study and think more carefully."},
{id:"why-the-sea-is-salt",title:"Why the Sea Is Salt",region:"Europe",country:"Norway; Asbjørnsen–Moe, Dasent translation (1859)",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A poor man trades a flitch of bacon for a magical quern, whose endless salt-grinding explains the salty sea.",moral:"A gift with great power should be handled with care.",leftPage:"At Christmas, a poor brother had no food, so he asked his rich brother for help. The rich brother offered him a flitch of bacon only if he would go to Hell. The poor man kept his word and set out. On the way, an old woodcutter told him that the people inside would trade a magical hand-quern for the bacon. The quern could grind whatever its owner asked for.",rightPage:"The poor man returned home with the quern, and it made food and other things whenever he asked. Later, his rich brother borrowed it for a voyage. He asked it to grind salt, but did not know how to make it stop. Salt filled the ship until it sank beneath the sea with the quern. In this Norse tale, that is why the sea is salty."},
{id:"the-fox-as-herdsman",title:"The Fox as Herdsman",region:"Europe",country:"Norway; Asbjørnsen–Moe, Dasent translation (1859)",theme:"Honesty",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A woman chooses a fox as her herdsman, then discovers he has helped himself to her cream.",moral:"Trust is easier to keep when people are honest about what they do.",leftPage:"A woman looking for someone to tend her animals listened to the calls of a bear and a wolf, but neither call pleased her. Then the fox gave his call, and she liked it, so she hired him as her herdsman. When she returned, she found that the fox had eaten her cream.",rightPage:"The fox ran off with the last dab of cream still on him. The woman flung that dab after him, and it landed on the tip of his tail. The tale says that is how the fox got his white-tipped tail."},
{id:"the-cat-on-the-dovrefell",title:"The Cat on the Dovrefell",region:"Europe",country:"Norway; Asbjørnsen–Moe, Dasent translation (1859)",theme:"Courage",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A cat’s fierce-seeming defense sends the trolls away from a family’s Christmas home.",moral:"A small, brave helper can make a difference.",leftPage:"One Christmas Eve, a traveler carrying a white bear asked to stay at Halvor’s cottage. The family usually left home that night because trolls came to eat their Christmas meal. The bear hid beneath the stove. When the trolls appeared, the cat stood its ground and faced them.",rightPage:"The trolls heard the cat and imagined that its kittens must be fearsome too. Frightened, they hurried away and never returned to eat Christmas at Halvor’s home. The family was safe, and the traveler and his bear could stay."},
{id:"princess-on-the-glass-hill",title:"Princess on the Glass Hill",region:"Europe",country:"Norway; Asbjørnsen–Moe, Dasent translation (1859)",theme:"Perseverance",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"The youngest son succeeds at an enchanted riding challenge after his brothers fail.",moral:"Patience and steady effort can bring success.",leftPage:"Each year, a farmer’s meadow was mysteriously stripped of its grass on St. John’s night. His sons took turns watching it. The two older brothers tried first, but the youngest, known as Boots, kept watch too. He discovered what was happening and later rode up the steep, glassy hill in an enchanted challenge that others could not manage.",rightPage:"Boots reached the top and was recognized as the successful rider. In this old tale, the princess marries him, and the story ends with a wedding feast. The impossible glass hill belongs to the story’s magic, not to a real-world challenge."},
{id:"how-one-went-out-to-woo",title:"How One Went Out to Woo",region:"Europe",country:"Norway; Asbjørnsen–Moe, Dasent translation (1859)",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A poor household’s attempts to look prosperous turn a visit from a suitor into a joke.",moral:"A little honesty is better than pretending to be someone else.",leftPage:"A young man went to visit a household while looking for a wife. The family had little, but tried to seem well-off. The husband polished things with the only new sleeve on his coat, and his wife showed off her one new shoe. Their daughter wore one new cap and kept nodding as the visitor looked around.",rightPage:"When asked why she kept nodding, the daughter explained that she could not be everywhere at once. The visit ends with that joke. The story reflects an old-fashioned courtship custom; it does not give a fuller account of what became of the young man and the daughter."},
{id:"gudbrand-on-the-hill-side",title:"Gudbrand on the Hill-side",region:"Europe",country:"Norway; Asbjørnsen–Moe, Dasent translation (1859)",theme:"Contentment",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"Gudbrand’s wife approves of each unlikely trade he makes, and their good cheer wins a bet.",moral:"Kind support matters more than having everything go to plan.",leftPage:"Gudbrand and his wife loved one another and were content with their life. When she suggested selling one of their two cows for ready money, Gudbrand set out to do so. On the way home he traded the cow through a series of goods, until he had no money left.",rightPage:"His wife listened to each part of the story and praised his choices instead of scolding him. A neighbor who had bet that she would be angry had to pay Gudbrand a hundred dollars. The couple stayed cheerful together, even though the trip had not gone as planned."},
{id:"not-a-pin-to-choose-between-them",title:"Not a Pin to Choose Between Them",region:"Europe",country:"Norway; Asbjørnsen–Moe, Dasent translation (1859)",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A wife’s attempts to trade a cow and hen for seed-corn end in an unexpected purchase and a wry reply.",moral:"People can be quick to judge others without seeing their own mistakes.",leftPage:"A couple needed seed-corn, so the wife took their cow and a hen to town to sell them. She made one bargain after another, hoping to get what they needed. Instead, she came home with an unlikely purchase and no seed-corn.",rightPage:"Her husband called her foolish. Then he thought of the people who had made the bargains with her and admitted that they had not been any wiser. There was, he said, not a pin to choose between them. The old story’s joke is about everyone’s muddled trading, not a rule about women."},
{id:"the-three-aunts",title:"The Three Aunts",region:"Europe",country:"Norway; Asbjørnsen–Moe, Dasent translation (1859)",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"Three strange helpers finish a young woman’s impossible spinning task and change her future.",moral:"People should not be judged by their appearance, and work should be shared fairly.",leftPage:"A poor young woman took a position at a palace. Jealous maids told the queen that she had claimed she could spin a pound of flax in a day, and the queen ordered her to do it. The young woman did not know how she could finish the impossible task. Three old women arrived and offered to help, spinning all the flax for her.",rightPage:"The prince asked the three helpers about their unusual features. The young woman explained that years of spinning had caused them, and the prince promised that his bride would never have to spin. In this version, he and the young woman marry. The story treats the helpers with gratitude rather than making their appearance a joke."},
{id:"the-cock-the-cuckoo-and-the-black-cock",title:"The Cock, the Cuckoo, and the Black-Cock",region:"Europe",country:"Norway; Asbjørnsen–Moe, Dasent translation (1859)",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"Three birds disagree about sharing a cow and leave the question playfully unsettled.",moral:"It helps to agree on fair rules before sharing something.",leftPage:"A cock, a cuckoo, and a black-cock bought a cow together, but could not agree how to divide it. They decided that the first to wake in the morning would own the whole cow. The cock woke first and claimed it. Then the cuckoo woke and asked for half.",rightPage:"Last, the black-cock woke and asked for an equal share. The tale ends with the three birds’ competing claims and leaves the question open for the reader."},
{"id":"hans-in-luck","title":"Hans in Luck","region":"Europe","country":"Germany; Adapted from Grimm’s Household Tales (1912), trans. Taylor, Jardine & Edwardes","theme":"Contentment","readingTime":"4 min","summary":"Hans trades away his wages on the road home and feels glad to be free of the last heavy burden. Caregiver note: the source includes Hans’s fall when the horse throws him and the later cow kick; this short retelling omits both incidents.","moral":"Happiness does not always come from owning more.","leftPage":"After working for seven years, Hans received a lump of silver and set off to visit his mother. On the road, he traded it for a horse, then traded again for a cow, a pig, a goose, and finally a sharpening stone. Each exchange left him with something different to carry.","rightPage":"Near the end of his journey, Hans stopped beside a stream to drink and set the sharpening stone down. As he bent over the water, it rolled into the stream. Glad to be free of its weight, he walked home happily."},
{id:"jorinda-and-jorindel",title:"Jorinda and Jorindel",region:"Europe",country:"Germany; Taylor–Edwardes translation of Grimm (1884 text)",theme:"Courage",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"Jorindel finds a magic flower and uses it to free Jorinda and the other captive maidens.",moral:"Hope and persistence can help people find a way through frightening trouble.",leftPage:"An old fairy changed maidens who came near her castle into birds and kept them in cages. Jorinda was changed into a bird, while Jorindel was held in place by the fairy’s spell. He could not help her then, but he kept searching for a way to break the enchantment.",rightPage:"After a dream showed him a magic flower, Jorindel returned to the castle with it. The flower broke the spell, and Jorinda and the other captive maidens were freed. Jorinda and Jorindel later married, as the old tale’s ending says."},
{id:"the-queen-bee",title:"The Queen Bee",region:"Europe",country:"Germany; Taylor–Edwardes translation of Grimm (1884 text)",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A youngest brother’s kindness to animals is repaid when they help him pass an enchanted test.",moral:"Kindness to others can return as help when you need it.",leftPage:"Two older princes wanted to disturb an ant-hill and catch ducks, but their youngest brother protected the animals. The brothers later came to an enchanted castle, where they had to complete difficult tasks. Ants, ducks, and bees came to help the youngest prince because he had treated them kindly.",rightPage:"With the animals’ help, he completed the tasks and identified the youngest princess. The enchantment was broken. In this old tale, the three brothers marry the three princesses; the story’s reward follows its period’s marriage custom."},
{id:"the-golden-goose",title:"The Golden Goose",region:"Europe",country:"Germany; Taylor–Edwardes translation of Grimm (1884 text)",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A mocked youngest son shares his food and is rewarded with a goose whose feathers make a funny procession.",moral:"Generosity can bring unexpected good fortune.",leftPage:"The youngest son was mocked by his family, but he shared his meal with a little grey-haired man he met in the woods. The man rewarded him with a golden goose. When people tried to pull out its feathers, they found themselves stuck to the bird and to one another. The older brothers had earlier been hurt in small axe mishaps during their own trips to the woods.",rightPage:"The stuck-together people followed the goose in a comical line. A princess who had never laughed saw the procession and laughed at last. The king rewarded the young man with the princess’s hand and, in time, his kingdom. This is the tale’s old-fashioned marriage ending."},
{id:"the-elves-and-the-shoemaker",title:"The Elves and the Shoemaker",region:"Europe",country:"Germany; Taylor–Edwardes translation of Grimm (1884 text)",theme:"Gratitude",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"Unseen helpers make shoes for a struggling shoemaker, who thanks them with clothes of their own.",moral:"Notice help you receive, and find a kind way to show gratitude.",leftPage:"A hardworking shoemaker had leather for only one last pair of shoes. He cut the leather pieces and left them ready on his workbench before bed. In the morning, he found a beautifully finished pair. The shoes sold well, and soon he had enough leather for two more pairs. Again, the work was done before he came downstairs.",rightPage:"The shoemaker and his wife stayed awake one night and saw little elves making the shoes. They sewed tiny clothes for the helpers, who put them on and left merrily. The shoemaker and his wife continued to prosper, and the story ends with their gratitude to the elves."},
{id:"the-three-sillies",title:"The Three Sillies",region:"Europe",country:"England; Jacobs, English Fairy Tales (1890)",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A suitor sets out to find people sillier than his sweetheart’s family and returns with plenty of examples.",moral:"It is easy to worry about an imagined problem instead of what is happening now.",leftPage:"A farmer’s daughter saw a mallet in the cellar and imagined that it might someday fall on a future child. She began to weep over this possibility, though no child was there and no accident had happened. Her suitor decided to travel and look for people sillier than her family.",rightPage:"On his journey, the suitor met several people whose foolish actions convinced him that he had found enough silliness. He returned and married the farmer’s daughter, and the couple’s story ends happily. The danger from the mallet was only imagined; nobody was hurt."},
{id:"the-magpies-nest",title:"The Magpie’s Nest",region:"Europe",country:"England; Jacobs, English Fairy Tales (1890)",theme:"Perseverance",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"The magpie begins to teach the birds nest-building, but they keep leaving before the lesson is finished.",moral:"Listening to the whole lesson can help you learn more.",leftPage:"The birds asked the magpie to teach them how to build nests. She began by showing them how to use mud, then to add twigs. One by one, the birds interrupted to say they already knew enough and flew away. At last, only the turtle-dove remained.",rightPage:"The magpie grew cross and left before finishing the lesson. The tale says this is why different birds make different kinds of nests."},
{id:"the-pedlar-of-swaffham",title:"The Pedlar of Swaffham",region:"Europe",country:"England; Jacobs, More English Fairy Tales (1894)",theme:"Perseverance",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A pedlar follows a repeated dream to London and hears of a treasure waiting back at home.",moral:"Listening carefully can help you recognise where to look for what matters.",leftPage:"A poor pedlar dreamed three times that he would hear joyful news on London Bridge. Though he found nothing there at first, he travelled to London to see what might happen. A shopkeeper on the bridge told him about a dream of treasure at Swaffham, where the shopkeeper had never gone.",rightPage:"The pedlar returned to Swaffham and found the treasure where the story’s dream directed him. He became rich and rebuilt the church in his town. The tale ends with the pedlar’s discovery and his gift to the community."},
{id:"johnny-cake",title:"Johnny-Cake",region:"Europe",country:"England; Jacobs, English Fairy Tales (1890)",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A runaway personified cake outruns its pursuers until a fox uses flattery to catch it.",moral:"Flattery can be a trick, so think carefully before trusting it.",leftPage:"An old woman baked a Johnny-cake and asked a boy to watch it. The cake rolled out of the oven and ran away. It outran the boy, his parents, and others who tried to catch up with it. The cake was proud that nobody could keep pace.",rightPage:"A fox praised the Johnny-cake and asked it to come closer so he could hear its story. The cake moved nearer, and the fox caught and ate it. This is the ending of the old, make-believe chase tale."},
{id:"the-hedley-kow",title:"The Hedley Kow",region:"Europe",country:"England (Yorkshire); Jacobs, More English Fairy Tales (1894)",theme:"Contentment",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A woman’s imagined riches keep changing as a trickster plays a harmless trick with a pot.",moral:"A joyful imagination can make an ordinary day feel like an adventure.",leftPage:"An old woman found a large black pot beside the road and began imagining how lucky she was. As she carried it home, the Hedley Kow made it seem to change into one treasure after another. Each time she thought she had found something wonderful, the object changed again.",rightPage:"At last, the marvel vanished and the woman had no treasure to show for her walk. Still, she went home delighted by the adventure and the good luck she had imagined. The story closes with her happiness, not with a real fortune."},
{id:"the-three-wishes",title:"The Three Wishes",region:"Europe",country:"England; Jacobs, More English Fairy Tales (1894)",theme:"Carefulness",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A woodcutter and his wife use three wishes on a pudding and on undoing their first two wishes.",moral:"Think before you use an opportunity you cannot get back.",leftPage:"A woodcutter was about to cut down an old oak when a fairy asked him to spare it. In return, she offered him and his wife three wishes. At home, they began to imagine what they might ask for. Before they could settle on a sensible plan, the woodcutter wished for a black pudding.",rightPage:"His wife was cross about the wasted wish and wished the pudding would stick to his nose. It did, so their last wish had to remove it. They ended with the pudding for supper, but no other wish remained. The tale’s impossible food gag is a reminder to choose carefully."},
{"id":"the-stars-in-the-sky","title":"The Stars in the Sky","region":"Europe","country":"Scotland; Adapted from Jacobs, More English Fairy Tales (1894)","theme":"Wonder","readingTime":"3 min","summary":"Caregiver note: the lassie swims in a mill-dam and paddles in a brooklet, then faces a cold, frightening climb; she reaches home safely. Never enter a dam or stream to imitate the tale. Only swim in a safe, appropriate place under responsible adult supervision.","moral":"A long search can lead somewhere unexpected, and home can be a safe return.","leftPage":"A tiny lassie longed to play with the stars. She asked a mill-dam and a brooklet where to find them; they said the lights shone on their waters. She swam in the mill-dam and paddled in the brook, but saw no star. The Good Folk gave her a riddle: ask Four Feet to carry her to No Feet at all, then ask No Feet to carry her to a stair without steps. She followed their directions.","rightPage":"A horse carried her to the sea, where a big fish took her along a shining path to a bright stair rising into the sky. She climbed and climbed, but did not get higher. Cold, dizzy, and frightened, she let go and sank through darkness. At once she found herself back home, sitting by her bedside and crying. The adventure ended where it began, safely at home."},
{id:"the-white-parrot",title:"The White Parrot",region:"Europe",country:"Spain; Eells, Tales of Enchantment from Spain (1920)",theme:"Perseverance",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"Mariquita waits for the enchanted white parrot to sleep, then frees her brother and the other petrified people.",moral:"Patience and courage can help a person undo a mistake.",leftPage:"Mariquita wished for a silver fountain in her family’s courtyard, and the old woman who visited told her brother where to find it. On his journey, he came upon a white parrot and seized it too soon. The parrot’s magic turned him to stone, along with other people. Mariquita set out to find her brother and the bird.",rightPage:"Mariquita waited until the white parrot was asleep before freeing her brother and the other people who had been turned to stone. They returned home together and held a feast that lasted three days. Everyone was restored in this version of the Spanish tale."},
{id:"the-carnation-youth",title:"The Carnation Youth",region:"Europe",country:"Spain; Eells, Tales of Enchantment from Spain (1920)",theme:"Perseverance",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A young woman endures false accusations and difficult tasks before breaking the enchantment of the youth in a carnation.",moral:"Steadiness and help from others can carry someone through unfair treatment.",leftPage:"A laborer’s daughter burned a carnation and glimpsed a youth enchanted inside it. Later, she worked as a maid. Jealous servants falsely said she had boasted that she could finish impossible tasks. With the youth’s help and the birds’ magic, she washed the laundry and restored her mistress’s eyesight.",rightPage:"During a procession, the young woman’s candle went out. She spoke to the youth, and the enchantment was broken. He was restored, and the two married, as the source’s old-fashioned ending relates."},
{id:"the-enchanted-castle-in-the-sea",title:"The Enchanted Castle in the Sea",region:"Europe",country:"Spain; Eells, Tales of Enchantment from Spain (1920)",theme:"Courage",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A young man stranded at a sea castle awakens an enchanted princess, who later recognizes him by a handkerchief.",moral:"A kind act can be remembered and returned.",leftPage:"A young man burdened by debt accepted a stranger’s offer of work. The stranger left him at a castle far out at sea, where the young man feared he might be alone without food. He found a hidden hall with provisions and discovered an enchanted princess. When she awoke, she gave him a handkerchief.",rightPage:"Later, at the princess’s wedding, she recognized the handkerchief and said she would marry only the young man who had awakened her. He was brought to her, and they married joyfully. The story ends with their reunion and marriage."},
{id:"the-prince-fernando",title:"The Prince Fernando",region:"Europe",country:"Spain; Eells, Tales of Enchantment from Spain (1920)",theme:"Perseverance",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"Maria uses a little puppet play to help Cristobal remember her after a curse makes him forget.",moral:"Remembering the truth can restore a bond that has been disrupted.",leftPage:"Maria and Cristobal escaped from a genie’s domain, but the genie cursed Cristobal to forget Maria if a dog licked his hand or an old woman embraced him. The curse came true, and Cristobal returned to his kingdom without remembering her. Maria found work in a noble household and waited for a chance to reach him.",rightPage:"At a betrothal feast, Maria put on a puppet play about their adventures. The puppets’ repeated blows caused magical pain in Cristobal’s head as the story reminded him of what had happened. He recognized Maria, and they married. Later they became king and queen and had children, as this version concludes."},
{"id":"the-frog-and-his-clothes","title":"The Frog and His Clothes","region":"Europe","country":"Spain; Adapted from Eells, Tales of Enchantment from Spain (1920)","theme":"Problem-solving","readingTime":"3 min","summary":"Caregiver note: the frog coerces the fisherman into giving his daughter in marriage and threatens to kill him if he refuses. Later, the neighbor threatens to dash the frog against a rock.","moral":"Careful attention can reveal a way to solve a puzzling problem.","leftPage":"A frog came to a fisherman’s home and demanded to marry his daughter. He threatened to kill the fisherman if he refused. The daughter married him; by day he was a frog, while at night he appeared as a handsome man.","rightPage":"A neighbor’s candle wax fell on the sleeping human husband, turning him into a frog. She put him in her pocket and carried him away. The fisherman’s daughter already had her husband’s folded clothes and carried them to a fair. There, she heard him ask her to put the clothes in the neighbor’s pocket. She did, and when he wore them, the enchantment ended for good; he remained human."},
{id:"the-flower-of-beauty",title:"The Flower of Beauty",region:"Europe",country:"Spain; Eells, Tales of Enchantment from Spain (1920)",theme:"Friendship",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A painter’s apprentice breaks a giant’s curse and restores a marble bride; this old tale includes a lamb’s death, so grown-ups may wish to read it first.",moral:"Loyal help can matter when a friend faces a difficult trial.",leftPage:"A painter’s apprentice helped a prince find the Flower of Beauty. On her wedding day, a giant’s curse changed her into a marble statue. The prince and the whole countryside were sorrowful. The apprentice returned to a palace where he had once heard mysterious voices, hoping to learn how the spell could be broken.",rightPage:"The voices said that lamb’s blood would restore her. A lamb was slaughtered, and the apprentice bathed the statue’s veins in its blood. The Flower of Beauty became a living woman again. She and the prince were grateful to the apprentice, who stayed at court as its painter."},
{"id":"the-rabbit","title":"The Rabbit","region":"Europe","country":"Portugal; Adapted from Pedroso, Portuguese Folk-Tales, Monteiro trans. (1882)","theme":"Gratitude","readingTime":"4 min","summary":"Caregiver note: includes a dead body and supernatural revival. A princess throws water over a dead man and prays; he returns to life, and the invisible hands take human form, revealing the spellbound court. She discovers he is a prince, and they marry.","moral":"Compassion can help restore hope after a frightening discovery.","leftPage":"A princess heard a story about a mysterious rabbit and became curious about the place where it lived. She went there with an old woman and saw a splendid enchanted palace, where the work seemed to happen without visible people. In one room, a maid saw a dead man and ran out in fright. The princess went inside herself.","rightPage":"The princess threw water over the man and prayed. He came back to life as a handsome prince—the same person who had been appearing as the rabbit. The invisible hands took human form, revealing the spellbound court. The princess and prince agreed to marry, and the old woman was sent home with riches."},
{id:"the-cock-and-the-mouse",title:"The Cock and the Mouse",region:"Europe",country:"Principato Ulteriore, Italy; Crane, Italian Popular Tales, ch. LXXX (1885)",theme:"Problem-solving",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"After a mouse’s nut gives a cock a small head wound, a chain of exchanges brings the cloth that heals it.",moral:"Persistence and help from others can solve a small problem.",leftPage:"A mouse tossed a nut, and it struck a cock on the head, leaving a small cut. The cock went looking for help. He asked for water, then wood, then bread, then dog-hairs, and finally a rag. Each helper sent him onward to the next.",rightPage:"The cock got the rag and used it on his small head wound. It healed, and the cock recovered. The tale’s ending is gentle: the cock is well again, and no one dies."},
{id:"the-clever-peasant",title:"The Clever Peasant",region:"Europe",country:"Sicily, Italy; Crane after Gonzenbach No. 50 (1885)",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A peasant explains how four coins support four generations, then outwits a minister’s attempt to learn his answer.",moral:"Thoughtful answers can show how family members care for one another; the source’s final joke about a wife is dated and sexist.",leftPage:"A king asked a peasant how he used four coins. The peasant explained that one fed himself, another supported his children, another repaid his father’s care, and another supported his wife. A minister wanted to learn the answer and tried to trick him by showing him the king’s face on a hundred coins.",rightPage:"The peasant explained how the minister’s trick revealed what he wanted to know, and the king rewarded the peasant with wealth. The source then closes with a dated sexist joke at the wife’s expense. That is the old version’s framing, not a fair lesson about wives or women."},
{id:"the-foundling-prince",title:"The Foundling Prince",region:"Europe",country:"Russia; Afanasyev, Magnus translation (1915)",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A clever young foundling solves the king’s riddles about how to arrive both one way and another.",moral:"Careful thinking can make sense of a puzzling challenge.",leftPage:"A prince disappeared as a baby and was raised by a peasant. Fifteen years later, the king set a clever foundling a riddle: arrive neither naked nor dressed, neither on foot nor on horseback, and at neither day nor night. The young man thought about each part of the challenge before setting out.",rightPage:"He came wrapped in a net, riding a goat at twilight, with the goat positioned across the courtyard and street. The king recognized him as his son. The chapter ends with that recognition, without adding a further adventure."},
{"id":"the-sun-and-how-it-was-made-by-divine-will","title":"The Sun and how it was Made by Divine Will","region":"Europe","country":"Russia; Adapted from Balmont’s poem; prose translation by Leonard A. Magnus (1916)","theme":"Gratitude","readingTime":"3 min","summary":"Adapted from Konstantin Balmont’s religious poem, translated in prose by Leonard A. Magnus. Caregiver note: includes Christian references and birds whose feathers are scorched by the sun and renewed.","moral":"The rooster gives thanks to the Creator for the sun’s light.","leftPage":"In Balmont’s poem, angels dress the sun for its daily journey. Birds of Paradise fly ahead, dip their wings in the ocean, and are scorched by the sun’s heat; their feathers are renewed. When the sun wanes to the West, the angels strip off its robes and crown and lay them on the Lord’s throne.","rightPage":"When the Lord’s angels later take the robes and crown from the throne to prepare the sun for its journey, the rooster awakens and sings in praise of Christ, the Giver of Light. The poem ends with the rooster praising its Creator and “Amen.”"},
{id:"the-potter",title:"The Potter",region:"Europe",country:"Russia; Afanasyev, Magnus translation (1915)",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A witty potter turns an unfair purchase attempt into a comic exchange with the tsar.",moral:"A quick mind can help someone stand up to an unfair bargain.",leftPage:"A traveling potter traded witty remarks with Tsar Ivan, who ordered clay tableware from him. A boyar tried to buy the shipment on unfair terms. The potter bargained cleverly, leaving the boyar without his money and himself in the carriage. The tsar watched the affair and had the potter and boyar exchange clothes.",rightPage:"The tsar asked the potter what he had done with the geese. The potter answered that he had plucked them bald. That is the chapter’s closing joke; the story ends with his quick reply."},
{id:"christ-and-the-geese",title:"Christ and the Geese",region:"Europe",country:"Russia; Afanasyev, Magnus translation (1915)",theme:"Carefulness",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"Peter wishes to be God for half a day, but learns that someone still needs to watch the geese.",moral:"Everyday responsibilities still matter, even when a grand adventure seems more exciting.",leftPage:"Peter wished that he could be God for half a day, and Christ granted the wish. As they walked, they saw a girl leave her geese unattended while she went to a feast. Christ pointed out that the flock needed someone to watch it. Peter stayed behind to guard the geese while Christ went on.",rightPage:"Peter remained with the flock and never again wished to be God. The Russian source ends with this small, comic lesson about taking responsibility for what is in your care."},
{id:"christ-and-folk-songs",title:"Christ and Folk-songs",region:"Europe",country:"Russia; Afanasyev, Magnus translation (1915)",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"Christ explains to Peter why he stopped to listen to one household’s folk-song but passed another singing hymns.",moral:"Listen with respect before deciding what another person’s words mean.",leftPage:"As Christ and Peter walked through a village, Christ stopped to listen to a household singing a folk-song, while Peter continued ahead. Later, Peter stayed to hear hymns, but Christ passed by. Peter wondered why Christ had treated the two houses differently and asked him to explain.",rightPage:"Christ said that the first household had a “good scent,” while the second had no reverence. The chapter closes with that religious explanation. It presents the beliefs of its source rather than a general judgment about every song or hymn."},
{"id":"the-magic-mirror","title":"The Magic Mirror","region":"Europe","country":"Spain (Granada); Adapted from Sellers, Tales from the Lands of Nuts and Grapes (1888)","theme":"Honesty","readingTime":"4 min","summary":"A king’s barber bluffs the court with a mirror that supposedly reveals moral blemishes; a shepherdess is summoned to look.","moral":"The tale praises honesty over fearful self-deception.","leftPage":"A king’s barber claimed that a mirror could reveal marks left by a guilty conscience. He told the king that the only magic was what the court ladies’ evil consciences made them imagine. The shepherdess looked into the mirror and blushed.","rightPage":"The court ladies inspected the mirror and cried that it was a cheat. The king said they had only themselves to blame; he admired the shepherdess’s honesty and chose her as his bride. The tale makes clear the glass itself had no magic."},
{id:"the-fairies-mint",title:"The Fairies’ Mint",region:"Europe",country:"Wales (Anglesey); Emerson, Welsh Fairy-Tales and Other Stories (1894)",theme:"Carefulness",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"An Anglesey miller discovers fairies turning cockle-shells into golden coins in his mill.",moral:"Sometimes it is wiser to leave a harmless mystery undisturbed.",leftPage:"An Anglesey miller noticed that sacks had moved and grain had been disturbed during the night. One night, he and a friend watched the mill and saw it lit up and working at midnight. Through a small window, the miller saw little people feeding bags into the millstones. Golden coins came out where the flour usually fell.",rightPage:"The miller unlocked the door, and the lights went out. Inside, he found no one, only cockle-shells scattered around the millstones. People later reported seeing the mill lit and working again at midnight. The miller left the fairies alone to coin their money."},
{id:"gwrgan-farfdrwchs-fable",title:"Gwrgan Farfdrwch’s Fable",region:"Europe",country:"Wales (Arvon); Williams source, transcribed by Emerson (1894)",theme:"Carefulness",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A goat declines a lion’s invitation to leave her high rock for a valley because she does not trust his teeth.",moral:"It is wise to notice a risk and choose the safer path.",leftPage:"On a high rock in Arvon, a goat looked down at a lion in the valley. The lion invited her to come down, praising the food, flowers, and shady groves by the streams. He said she would find plenty to enjoy there.",rightPage:"The goat thanked the lion, but said that his teeth made his company unsafe. She preferred to stay on the rock. The lion did not chase her, and the tale ends with the goat safe where she chose to remain."},
{id:"the-giantesss-apron-full",title:"The Giantess’s Apron-Full",region:"Europe",country:"Wales (Bwlch-y-Ddeufaen); Williams source, transcribed by Emerson (1894)",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A traveler’s answer persuades a giant and his wife to drop the stones they meant to use for a bridge to Mona.",moral:"A small piece of information can change a large plan.",leftPage:"A giant and his wife were travelling toward Mona, intending to settle there. The giant carried two large stones, one under each arm, and his wife carried smaller stones in her apron. They planned to use them to build a bridge across the narrow channel. On the way, they met a traveler with a large bundle of old shoes.",rightPage:"The giant asked how far Mona was. The traveler replied that it was so far he had worn out all those shoes coming from there. The giant dropped his stones, which the tale identifies with two upright rocks, and his wife emptied her apron. The heap is still called the giantess’s apron-full in the story."},
{id:"ellens-luck",title:"Ellen’s Luck",region:"Europe",country:"Wales (North Wales); Emerson, Welsh Fairy-Tales and Other Stories (1894)",theme:"Gratitude",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"Ellen finds a copper by the same gutter each Sunday until she tells her mother where it comes from.",moral:"Some good fortune is brief, and it is best to be honest about it.",leftPage:"One Sunday in North Wales, Ellen found a copper beside an open gutter. She was careful with money and saved it. Each Sunday afternoon, she walked past the same drain and found another copper there.",rightPage:"One day, Ellen’s mother found her pile of pennies and asked where they came from. Ellen told her. She went back to the gutter many Sundays afterward, but never found another copper."},
{"id":"pride-goeth-before-a-fall","title":"Pride Goeth Before a Fall","region":"South Asia","country":"India; Adapted from Jacobs (1892), ch. XVIII; after Tales of the Sun (1890)","theme":"Cleverness","readingTime":"4 min","summary":"Caregiver note: the source includes an armed robbery with weapons, forced removal of the merchants' clothes, and compelled dancing; this retelling keeps the action non-graphic. Ten cloth merchants use a coded song and teamwork to recover their goods.","moral":"Clear thinking and teamwork can help people stand up to unfair strength.","leftPage":"Ten cloth merchants were returning from a long journey with money from their sales. In a forest, three robbers stopped them with swords and clubs, took their goods and clothes, and ordered them to dance. The robbers sat nearby, certain the merchants had no way to answer. But the traders knew a secret language they used with one another.","rightPage":"The merchants’ leader began a song that sounded like a dance tune but carried a message in their trading words. The ten friends understood: there were ten of them and only three robbers. Moving together as they sang, they surrounded the robbers, tied them with cloth, and recovered their belongings. The merchants returned home with their goods."},
{id:"the-broken-pot",title:"The Broken Pot",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892)",theme:"Carefulness",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A Brahman’s grand plans for a pot of rice vanish when he acts out a future he has only imagined.",moral:"Take care of what you have before counting on what you might gain.",leftPage:"A Brahman carried a pot of rice and began imagining what he could do with it. He would sell the rice, earn money, and build a fine future. Soon he was picturing a child of his own and how he would discipline that imagined child.",rightPage:"As he acted out his plan, he kicked the pot. It broke, and the rice spilled across the ground. His riches and grand future had existed only in his thoughts; now even the rice was gone."},
{id:"the-tiger-the-brahman-and-the-jackal",title:"The Tiger, the Brahman, and the Jackal",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892)",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A jackal’s clever question persuades a threatening tiger to climb back into its cage.",moral:"A thoughtful question can help solve a frightening problem.",leftPage:"A Brahman found a tiger shut inside a cage. The tiger asked to be let out and promised to be grateful. The Brahman opened the door, but as soon as the tiger was free, it said it would eat him.",rightPage:"A jackal arrived and pretended not to understand how the tiger had been shut inside. To show him, the tiger climbed back into the cage. The jackal shut the door, and the Brahman continued safely on his way."},
{"id":"a-lesson-for-kings","title":"A Lesson for Kings","region":"South Asia","country":"India; Adapted from Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892), ch. XVII","theme":"Wisdom","readingTime":"3 min","summary":"Two kings searching for their faults meet on a narrow road and compare what makes a ruler truly righteous.","moral":"Meet anger with calm, wickedness with goodness, stinginess with gifts, and lies with truth.","leftPage":"The king of Benares ruled justly but worried that people praised him only because they feared to name his faults. He travelled beyond the city with his charioteer to seek honest advice. Another king, Mallika, was searching for his own faults too. Their chariots met on a narrow road where neither could easily pass.","rightPage":"The charioteers found the kings equal in age, lands, and power, so they compared their conduct. Mallika’s charioteer called it virtuous to meet strength with strength and wickedness with wickedness. The Benares charioteer praised a better rule: meet anger with calm, wickedness with goodness, stinginess with gifts, and lies with truth. Mallika and his charioteer got down and moved aside for Brahma-datta."},
{id:"how-the-wicked-sons-were-duped",title:"How the Wicked Sons were Duped",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Jacobs, Indian Fairy Tales (1892)",theme:"Honesty",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"Greedy sons discover that the bags they hoped would hold their father’s riches contain only stones and gravel.",moral:"Greed can make people mistake empty promises for real value.",leftPage:"An old father’s sons paid little attention to him until he led them to believe that his wealth was growing. They began to watch eagerly, expecting that riches would one day be theirs. After their father died, they hurried to open the bags they thought he had filled with treasure.",rightPage:"The bags held only stones and gravel. The sons had been fooled by their own greed and their father’s trick. They found no fortune in the bags, and the tale ends with their hopes exposed as empty."},
{id:"the-indigent-brahman",title:"The Indigent Brahman",region:"South Asia",country:"India (Bengal); Day, Folk-Tales of Bengal (1883)",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A poor Brahman uses a magical pot to confront a landholder who took it, then chooses mercy.",moral:"When someone has wronged you, mercy can open a way to a better ending.",leftPage:"A poor Brahman prayed for help, and Durga gave him an earthen pot that made an endless supply of sweets. A wealthy landholder took the pot for himself. The Brahman used its magic to bring forth demons, and the landholder and his household became frightened.",rightPage:"The landholder begged for mercy. The Brahman relented, and the demons ceased to threaten the household. The Brahman’s family kept the magical pot and lived comfortably thereafter."},
{id:"the-bears-bad-bargain",title:"The Bear’s Bad Bargain",region:"South Asia",country:"India (Punjab); Steel, Tales of the Punjab (1894)",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A bear’s bargain for food leaves him with unripe pears and a stomachache, while a woodman’s family keeps its meal.",moral:"A greedy bargain may leave everyone worse off.",leftPage:"An old woodman and his wife lived beside an orchard. They were allowed to eat pears that fell into their yard, and they had khichri, wood, and ripe pears of their own. A bear came and tried to bargain with them for food.",rightPage:"The bear ended up eating unripe pears and fled with a stomachache. The woodman and his wife kept their khichri, wood, and ripe pears. The bear’s poor bargain had not improved his meal."},
{id:"the-jackal-and-the-iguana",title:"The Jackal and the Iguana",region:"South Asia",country:"India (Punjab); Steel, Tales of the Punjab (1894)",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"An iguana sees through a jackal’s grand disguise and lets its song puncture his boastful act.",moral:"A proud display cannot make a boast true.",leftPage:"A jackal hung old shoes from his ears and built himself a platform from bones. He declared that he was a prince. An iguana saw through the display and retreated into its hole instead of believing him.",rightPage:"The jackal dug after the iguana until he was worn out. From inside its hole, the iguana sang, exposing the jackal’s pretensions. The jackal had exhausted himself trying to make his boast seem real."},
{id:"the-sandy-road",title:"The Sandy Road",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Rhys Davids trans., Buddhist Jātaka Tales, Vol. I (1880)",theme:"Perseverance",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"When a caravan runs out of water, a determined young attendant finds a way to reach water beneath the sand.",moral:"Steady effort can help a whole group through a difficult journey.",leftPage:"A caravan crossed a sandy, dry country, and its water ran out. The travelers needed help. A young attendant did not give up: he searched the ground and found a stone buried beneath the sand.",rightPage:"He broke the buried stone, and water appeared. The caravan could drink and continue its journey. In the tale’s frame, the young attendant’s perseverance is remembered as leading to spiritual attainment."},
{id:"the-story-of-chullaka-the-treasurer",title:"The Story of Chullaka the Treasurer",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Rhys Davids trans., Buddhist Jātaka Tales, Vol. I (1880)",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"Chullaka turns a small opportunity into a fortune through a series of careful trades and rises to high office.",moral:"Resourcefulness can help a small beginning grow into something valuable.",leftPage:"Chullaka noticed an opportunity in a small object that someone had discarded. He used it to begin trading, then made further exchanges, building on each one. He paid attention to what people needed and used each success to make the next trade.",rightPage:"Over time, Chullaka built wealth and eventually rose to high office. The frame of the tale recalls that he had become wealthy earlier through the help of the same teacher. His careful use of a small opportunity shaped his good fortune."},
{id:"the-measure-of-rice",title:"The Measure of Rice",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Rhys Davids trans., Buddhist Jātaka Tales, Vol. I (1880)",theme:"Honesty",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A wise valuer corrects a foolish royal estimate of rice and is appointed in his place.",moral:"Fair decisions depend on careful and honest judgment.",leftPage:"The king asked a royal valuer to assess a quantity of rice. The valuer gave a wildly inaccurate answer. A wise person showed the king how the rice should properly be valued.",rightPage:"The king saw the difference between the foolish estimate and the careful one. He dismissed the first valuer and appointed the wise person instead. The rice was judged fairly at last."},
{id:"the-banyan-deer",title:"The Banyan Deer",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Rhys Davids trans., Buddhist Jātaka Tales, Vol. I (1880)",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A deer offers himself in place of a pregnant doe, moving a king to end the hunt and protect the animals.",moral:"Compassion can change a powerful person’s decision.",leftPage:"A king’s hunting threatened the deer herds. When a pregnant doe was in danger, the Banyan Deer offered to take her place. He was ready to face the king so that she could be spared.",rightPage:"The king was moved by the Banyan Deer’s offer. He stopped the killing and granted protection to the animals. The doe and the herd were no longer hunted by the king."},
{id:"the-greedy-antelope",title:"The Greedy Antelope",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Rhys Davids trans., Buddhist Jātaka Tales, Vol. I (1880)",theme:"Carefulness",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"An antelope drawn by food falls into danger, but the king recognizes the problem and releases it unharmed.",moral:"Pause before following a tempting reward into danger.",leftPage:"An antelope was drawn toward food and wandered into danger, where a hunter gained power over it. The king saw how the antelope’s attraction to the food had led it into trouble.",rightPage:"The king released the antelope back to the forest, and it went free unharmed. The tale warns that greed can lead someone toward danger, even when the promised food seems inviting."},
{id:"the-wily-antelope",title:"The Wily Antelope",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Rhys Davids trans., Buddhist Jātaka Tales, Vol. I (1880)",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A sharp-eyed antelope recognizes a hunter’s trick involving a tree and escapes without harm.",moral:"Look carefully before trusting what appears safe.",leftPage:"A hunter planned to catch an antelope with a trick involving a tree and a concealed weapon. The antelope noticed that something was wrong and did not approach as the hunter expected.",rightPage:"The antelope escaped, and the hunter went away unharmed too. The plan failed because the animal paid attention to the danger hidden in the hunter’s scheme."},
{id:"the-dog-who-turned-preacher",title:"The Dog who turned Preacher",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Rhys Davids trans., Buddhist Jātaka Tales, Vol. I (1880)",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A wise dog shows the king that palace dogs also damaged leather, so the threatened city dogs are spared and fed.",moral:"Make sure you know the facts before blaming a whole group.",leftPage:"Some royal leather goods were damaged, and the king blamed the city’s dogs. He threatened them with punishment. A wise dog asked the king to check whether the palace dogs had also been near the leather.",rightPage:"The dog showed that palace dogs had eaten leather too. The king understood that he had blamed the city dogs unfairly. He relented and arranged for the dogs to be fed."},
{id:"the-elephant-and-the-dog",title:"The Elephant and the Dog",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Rhys Davids trans., Buddhist Jātaka Tales, Vol. I (1880)",theme:"Friendship",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A king discovers an elephant’s devotion to a dog and brings the separated friends together again.",moral:"Friendship deserves care, even when friends are very different.",leftPage:"An elephant and a dog became devoted companions. Then the dog was taken away. The elephant missed him so much that he stopped eating and grew sorrowful.",rightPage:"The king learned what had happened and understood the elephant’s grief. He brought the dog back, and the friends were reunited. The king honored the elephant for his deep friendship."},
{id:"the-bull-who-won-the-bet",title:"The Bull who Won the Bet",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Rhys Davids trans., Buddhist Jātaka Tales, Vol. I (1880)",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A bull stops pulling after harsh words, then helps his owner win a bet when the owner apologizes and speaks kindly.",moral:"Treat animals with respect, and they may be willing to work with you.",leftPage:"A kind bull agreed to pull a heavy load for a bet. When his owner spoke harshly to him, the bull stopped. The owner realized his words had hurt the friendship between them.",rightPage:"The owner apologized and spoke kindly. The bull then pulled the load, and the owner won the wager. The bull had not been injured; he had been waiting to be treated with respect."},
{id:"the-holy-quail",title:"The Holy Quail",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Rhys Davids trans., Buddhist Jātaka Tales, Vol. I (1880)",theme:"Courage",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A newly hatched quail speaks truthfully as a fire approaches, and the flames turn aside from the nest.",moral:"Truth and courage can help protect the vulnerable.",leftPage:"A newly hatched quail was too young to fly when a forest fire came near the nest. The little bird spoke about its harmless nature and the truth of its situation. The flames drew closer, but the quail did not flee.",rightPage:"The fire turned aside, and the nest was spared. The young quail and the other nestlings survived. The story presents the quail’s truthful words as the reason the flames changed their course."},
{id:"the-partridge-monkey-and-elephant",title:"The Partridge, Monkey, and Elephant",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Rhys Davids trans., Buddhist Jātaka Tales, Vol. I (1880)",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A partridge, monkey, and elephant learn which is oldest by remembering the growth of a tree.",moral:"Respect and good conduct help friends live well together.",leftPage:"A partridge, a monkey, and an elephant wondered which of them was oldest. Each recalled how the tree had grown when they first knew it. Their memories helped them understand their different ages.",rightPage:"The animals agreed to respect the oldest among them. The partridge taught them the value of reverence and good conduct. They understood one another better by listening to their memories of the tree."},
{id:"nanda-on-the-buried-gold",title:"Nanda on the Buried Gold",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Rhys Davids trans., Buddhist Jātaka Tales, Vol. I (1880)",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"With a wise adviser’s help, Nanda recovers buried family treasure, restores the family’s affairs, and shares generously.",moral:"Use good fortune to help put things right and to give generously.",leftPage:"A young man named Nanda sought help finding family treasure that had been buried. A wise adviser showed him how to recover it. Nanda followed the advice and brought the hidden wealth back into use.",rightPage:"Nanda restored the family’s affairs and used the wealth generously. The buried gold did more than enrich him: it helped his family and others. The tale ends with his fortune put to good use."},
{id:"the-speaking-horse",title:"The Speaking Horse",region:"South Asia",country:"Sri Lanka (Sinhalese); Parker, Village Folk-Tales of Ceylon, Vol. I (1910)",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A clever pundit asks for seven years to teach a king’s horse to speak, and the horse remains with him after the king dies.",moral:"A clever plan can buy time, though it may not bring the result promised.",leftPage:"A king wanted his horse taught to speak. A pundit asked for seven years and for the king to support the horse during that time. The king agreed, and the horse stayed with the pundit as the years passed.",rightPage:"Before the seven years were up, the king died. The horse remained with the pundit. The tale’s clever delay had kept the horse and pundit together, but it did not show the horse learning to speak."},
{"id":"concerning-a-man-and-two-yakas","title":"Concerning a Man and Two Yakās","region":"South Asia","country":"Sri Lanka; Adapted from Parker, Village Folk-Tales of Ceylon, Vol. I (1910), no. 17","theme":"Wisdom","readingTime":"3 min","summary":"A man earns provisions through bargains with two yakās. Caregiver note: one yakā plans to kill him, but the tale resolves through a frightened promise rather than a fight.","moral":"Cleverness can help people face danger without a fight.","leftPage":"A man was preparing a new field near a large tree. The yakā who lived there asked him not to clear it, promising provisions each evening. Later, a second yakā planned to kill the man and hid under his bed. The man told his wife he was hungry enough to eat a yakā; she pointed out a knife and a pineapple, and the yakā heard them.","rightPage":"Frightened that the man was coming to eat it, the second yakā begged him not to and offered monthly provisions. The man accepted. He kept the first yakā’s evening gifts and received the second yakā’s monthly supplies, becoming wealthy."},
{id:"the-jackal-and-the-turtle",title:"The Jackal and the Turtle",region:"South Asia",country:"Sri Lanka (Sinhalese); Parker, Village Folk-Tales of Ceylon, Vol. I (1910)",theme:"Problem-solving",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A turtle tricks a hungry jackal into carrying it to a pond, then slips away safely into the water.",moral:"Quick thinking can help you find a safe way out of trouble.",leftPage:"A jackal tried to eat a turtle. The turtle told him that it needed to be soaked in water first. The jackal believed this and carried the turtle to a pond.",rightPage:"When they reached the water, the turtle slipped away into the pond and escaped unharmed. The jackal had carried it to safety, and the turtle’s quick plan had worked."},
{id:"the-grateful-jackal",title:"The Grateful Jackal",region:"South Asia",country:"Sri Lanka (Rōḍiyā); Parker, Village Folk-Tales of Ceylon, Vol. I (1910)",theme:"Gratitude",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A jackal repays a boy who saved it by bringing workers to rescue him from a python.",moral:"Remember help you have received, and help in return when you can.",leftPage:"A boy saved a jackal from a python. Later, the python seized the boy and held him tightly. The boy could not free himself, and the jackal saw that he needed help.",rightPage:"The jackal took the boy’s cloth to attract the attention of nearby workers. They came over and rescued the boy. The jackal had repaid the kindness the boy showed when it was trapped."},
{"id":"how-gourds-were-put-in-small-mouthed-pots","title":"How Gourds were put in Small-Mouthed Pots","region":"South Asia","country":"Sri Lanka; Adapted from Parker, Village Folk-Tales of Ceylon, Vol. II (1914), no. 79","theme":"Problem-solving","readingTime":"3 min","summary":"A clever village girl turns tiny gourd flowers into an answer to the king’s demand. Caregiver note: the source includes a widowhood threat against the girl’s father and a later coerced marriage. This adaptation omits the threat and marriage, but retains the later clever-hat and equality scene.","moral":"Cleverness can expose unfair demands and earn respect.","leftPage":"A king asked the girl where the sesame flower came from on its plant. She turned the question back on him: where had he been before his mother married? Angry, he ordered her father to bring a hundred gourd fruits in a hundred narrow-mouthed copper pots. The girl placed tiny fruits at the base of gourd flowers into the pots.","rightPage":"The girl promised that she could put a dirty cloth on the king’s head. Months later, she sewed him a fine hat with a dirty cloth hidden inside. The king bought and wore it. When she asked him to break it open, he found the cloth and admitted that they were equal in cleverness. This child-friendly adaptation ends before the source’s forced marriage."},
{id:"the-story-of-the-two-liars",title:"The Story of the Two Liars",region:"South Asia",country:"Sri Lanka (Sinhalese); Parker, Village Folk-Tales of Ceylon, Vol. II (1914)",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"Two boastful liars compete with absurd tales until a bundle of supposed elephants turns out to hold two lice.",moral:"Outlandish boasts are easier to spot when you look closely.",leftPage:"Two people known for lying tried to outdo one another with absurd boasts. Each told a story more unbelievable than the last. One spoke of a bundle that supposedly contained a hundred-tusk elephants.",rightPage:"When the bundle was examined, the hundred-tusk elephants had dwindled to two lice named Gourd and Ash-pumpkin. That was the tale’s final joke. The liars’ grand words had led to a very tiny surprise."},
{"id":"the-frightened-yaka","title":"The Frightened Yakā","region":"South Asia","country":"Sri Lanka; Adapted from Parker, Village Folk-Tales of Ceylon, Vol. II (1914), no. 141","theme":"Courage","readingTime":"4 min","summary":"A yakā plans to possess a woman but flees after overhearing her remark about a bill-hook; later, it possesses the Queen until a boy helps drive it away. Caregiver note: the Queen is possessed; the source also includes a beheading threat to the helpers and a raised pestle. She returns to her senses after the yakā leaves.","moral":"Understanding a threat can help people find a way to face it together.","leftPage":"A yakā lived in a Murungā tree at a woman’s doorway for seven years, waiting to possess her. One day, she brought a bill-hook to the doorway and said it was good for cutting a yakā. The yakā heard the bill-hook remark, waited until she went inside, and fled without possessing her. Later, the same yakā possessed the Queen. A boy learned how to drive the spirit out.","rightPage":"The boy reminded the yakā that the Queen was their mother. It fled, and the Queen came to her senses. The king rewarded the boy and his helpers."},
{id:"how-the-sun-the-moon-and-the-wind-went-out-to-dinner",title:"How the Sun, the Moon and the Wind Went Out to Dinner",region:"South Asia",country:"India (Southern); Frere, Old Deccan Days (1870)",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"After a family dinner, the Moon is blessed for saving food for their mother, while the Sun and Wind receive curses.",moral:"Remember those who care for you, and share with them.",leftPage:"The Sun, the Moon, and the Wind went to dine with their aunt and uncle, Thunder and Lightning. When they returned, their mother, a Star, asked what they had brought home for her. The Sun and the Wind had brought nothing, but the Moon had saved food to share.",rightPage:"Their mother cursed the Sun to have scorching rays and the Wind to parch living things. She blessed the Moon to remain cool, calm, and bright. The tale closes by explaining the Moon’s gentle light."},
{id:"how-the-cat-came-to-live-with-man",title:"How the Cat came to live with Man",region:"South Asia",country:"India (Khasi community); Rafy, Folk-Tales of the Khasis (1920)",theme:"Kindness",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"A cat leaves her tiger brother’s home after children treat her gently and she finds a welcome among people.",moral:"Gentle treatment can make a home feel safe and welcoming.",leftPage:"A cat, the tiger’s sister, was poorly treated in her brother’s household. He sent her to fetch a firebrand. On the way, she met children who gently stroked her, and she forgot the errand.",rightPage:"The angry tiger threatened to beat her, but no blow fell. The cat dropped the firebrand and returned to live among people, where she caught mice and frogs for food and enjoyed the children’s gentle touch. The tale says she has lived with mankind ever since."},
{id:"the-lost-book",title:"The Lost Book",region:"South Asia",country:"India (Khasi community); Rafy, Folk-Tales of the Khasis (1920)",theme:"Community",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"After a Khasi’s sacred book is ruined in a river, the people continue worship through traditions passed down by their ancestors.",moral:"A community can preserve its beliefs and knowledge by passing them on together.",leftPage:"God gave a Khasi and a foreigner books of sacred law to carry home. A river had flooded, making the crossing difficult. The foreigner protected his book in his hair, while the Khasi carried his in his mouth as he crossed safely.",rightPage:"The Khasi’s book was ruined by the water. He returned to ask God for another, but God had gone. The Khasi gathered his people, and they agreed to continue worship through traditions passed down by their ancestors."},
{id:"the-merchant-of-seri",title:"The Merchant of Seri",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Babbitt, Jataka Tales: Animal Stories (1912)",theme:"Honesty",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"An honest merchant fairly buys an old woman’s gold bowl while a greedy rival loses the chance by pretending it is worthless.",moral:"Honest dealing builds trust; greed can cost you a fair opportunity.",leftPage:"Two merchants traveled from place to place selling household wares. An old woman had a bowl that was really gold. One merchant recognized its value but pretended it was worthless. The other merchant examined it honestly and offered all his money and wares in exchange.",rightPage:"The grandmother accepted the fair trade, and the honest merchant crossed the river with the bowl’s proceeds. The greedy merchant returned too late; she refused his offer because the bowl had already been paid for and taken. He realized he had lost a fortune, while the honest trader lived well for a time."},
{id:"the-girl-monkey-and-the-string-of-pearls",title:"The Girl Monkey and the String of Pearls",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Babbitt, More Jataka Tales (retold, 1895–1907)",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A guard uses a glass-bead decoy to catch a monkey who stole the queen’s pearls, and the king praises his reasoning.",moral:"Careful observation can reveal a clever answer to a difficult problem.",leftPage:"A girl monkey watched a servant guarding the queen’s pearls. While the servant slept, the monkey took the pearls and hid them in a tree. A guard suspected a monkey and hung glass beads in the garden as a decoy.",rightPage:"The monkey eventually showed off the pearls and was caught. The king recovered them and thanked the chief guard for his reasoning. The source gives no further punishment or injury to the monkey."},
{id:"the-three-fishes",title:"The Three Fishes",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Babbitt, More Jataka Tales (retold, 1895–1907)",theme:"Friendship",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A quick-thinking fish distracts a fisherman and frees two friends caught in a net.",moral:"A friend’s quick action can help a whole group escape danger.",leftPage:"Three fish left a remote river to live near a town. One urged the others to return to safer water, but they delayed. Before long, two fish were caught in a fisherman’s net, and their friend needed a plan.",rightPage:"The third fish splashed to distract the fisherman. He pulled in one corner of the net, releasing the other fish. The three friends thanked their rescuer, returned to their former home, and lived safely there."},
{id:"the-red-bud-tree",title:"The Red-Bud Tree",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Babbitt, More Jataka Tales (retold, 1895–1907)",theme:"Wisdom",readingTime:"3 min",summary:"Four princes describe one tree differently until their father explains that each saw it in a different season.",moral:"People can describe the same thing differently when they see it at different times.",leftPage:"Four princes visited the same tree at different times of year. One saw it bare, another covered in red buds, a third leafy, and the youngest bearing bean-pods. They argued about what the tree looked like.",rightPage:"Their father explained that they had all seen the same tree in different seasons. Each prince’s description was true for the time he visited. The disagreement ended when they understood one another’s observations."},
{id:"the-hawks-and-their-friends",title:"The Hawks and Their Friends",region:"South Asia",country:"India; Babbitt, More Jataka Tales (retold, 1895–1907)",theme:"Friendship",readingTime:"4 min",summary:"A kingfisher, turtle, and lion help a hawk family drive away hunters and keep their nestlings safe.",moral:"Friends working together can protect one another from danger.",leftPage:"A hawk family befriended a kingfisher, a turtle, and a lion. One day, hunters threatened the nestlings. The kingfisher put out their fire more than once, and the turtle helped drive them into the lake.",rightPage:"The lion arrived and the hunters fled. They came out of the lake safely. The hawk family thanked its friends; no nestling or other animal was harmed, and the friends remained safe."}
];
